(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d"

An *fea_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d* is an isotropic symmetric fourth order tensor in 3D space, which is characterized by two independent values.

The values stem:[E] and stem:[nu] give terms in the stem:[6 xx 6] array representation of the *inverse* of the tensor as follows:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
   \left(
\begin{array}{cccccc}
{\displaystyle \frac{1}{E}} & -{\displaystyle \frac{\nu}{E}} & -{\displaystyle
\frac{\nu}{E}} & 0      & 0      & 0      \\
            & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{E}}    & -{\displaystyle \frac{\nu}{E}} & 0

& 0      & 0      \\
            &                & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{E}}    & 0      & 0      & 0

  \\
            &                &        &  {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G}}   & 0      & 0

  \\
            & \mbox{symmetric} &  &   & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G}}    & 0

    \\
            &                &        &        &        & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G}}

\end{array}
\right)
++++

where

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
 G = {\displaystyle \frac{E}{2(1 + \nu)}}
++++

For an elasticity tensor:

* stem:[E] is the Young's modulus;
* stem:[nu] is the Poisson's ratio;
* stem:[G] is the shear modulus;
* the terms are shown for the inverse of an elasticity tensor, which is a compliance tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[1]"

the term stem:[E] defined above, which is the Young's modulus for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[2]"

the term stem:[nu] defined above, which is the Poisson's ratio for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_iso_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d"

An *fea_iso_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d* is an iso-orthotropic symmetric fourth order tensor in 3D space, which is characterized by three independent values.

The values stem:[E], stem:[nu], and stem:[G] give terms in the stem:[6 xx 6] array representation of the *inverse* of the tensor as follows:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
   \left(
\begin{array}{cccccc}
{\displaystyle \frac{1}{E}} & -{\displaystyle \frac{\nu}{E}} & -{\displaystyle
\frac{\nu}{E}} & 0      & 0      & 0      \\
            & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{E}}    & -{\displaystyle \frac{\nu}{E}} & 0

& 0      & 0      \\
            &                & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{E}}    & 0      & 0      & 0

  \\
            &                &        &  {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G}}   & 0      & 0

  \\
         &\mbox{symmetric} & &     & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G}}    & 0

 \\
            &                &        &        &        & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G}}

\end{array}
\right)
++++

For an elasticity tensor:

* stem:[E] is the Young's modulus;
* stem:[nu] is the Poisson's ratio;
* stem:[G] is the shear modulus;
* the terms are shown for the inverse of an elasticity tensor, which is a compliance tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_iso_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[1]"

the term stem:[E] defined above, which is the Young's modulus for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_iso_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[2]"

the term stem:[nu] defined above, which is the Poisson's ratio for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_iso_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[3]"

the term stem:[G] defined above, which is the shear modulus for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_transverse_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d"

An *fea_transverse_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d* is a transverse isotropic symmetric fourth order tensor in 3D space, which is characterized by five independent values.

The values stem:[E_{"ll"}], stem:[E_{"tt"}], stem:[nu_{"tt"}], stem:[nu_{"tl"}], and stem:[G_{"lt"}] give terms in the stem:[6 xx 6] array representation of the *inverse* of the tensor as follows:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
   \left(
\begin{array}{cccccc}
{\displaystyle \frac{1}{E_{ll}}} & -{\displaystyle \frac{\nu_{tl}}{E_{tt}}} &
-{\displaystyle \frac{\nu_{tl}}{E_{tt}}} &
                                                0      & 0      & 0      \\
            & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{E_{tt}}}    & -{\displaystyle
\frac{\nu_{tt}}{E_{tt}}} &
                                                0      & 0      & 0      \\
            &                & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{E_{tt}}}    & 0      & 0
& 0      \\
            &                &        &  {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G_{lt}}}   & 0
& 0      \\
         &\mbox{symmetric} & &     & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G_{tt}}}    &
0      \\
            &                &        &        &        & {\displaystyle
\frac{1}{G_{lt}}}
\end{array}
\right)
++++

where

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
G_{tt} = {\displaystyle \frac{E_{tt}}{2(1 + \nu_{tt})}}
++++

For an elasticity tensor:

* stem:[E_{"ll"}] is the longitudinal Young's modulus;
* stem:[nu_{"tt"}] is the Poisson's ratio for strains in the transverse direction when uniaxially stressed in a transverse direction;
* stem:[E_{"tt"}] is the transverse Young's modulus;
* stem:[nu_{"tl"}] is the ratio between strain in a transverse direction to strain in the longitudinal direction when uniaxially strained in the longitudinal direction;
* stem:[G_{"lt"}] is the modulus for shear in the longitudinal - transverse plane;
* the 1 axis is longitudinal;
* the 2 and 3 axes are transverse;
* the terms are shown for the inverse of an elasticity tensor, which is a compliance tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_transverse_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[1]"

the term stem:[E_{"ll"}] defined above, which is the Young's modulus in the longitudinal direction for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_transverse_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[2]"

the term stem:[nu_{"tt"}] defined above, which is the Poisson's ratio in the transverse plane for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_transverse_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[3]"

the term stem:[E_{"tt"}] defined above, which is the Young's modulus in a transverse direction for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_transverse_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[4]"

the term stem:[nu_{"tl"}] defined above, which is the Poisson's ratio between transverse and longitudinal directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_transverse_isotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[5]"

the term stem:[G_{"lt"}] defined above, which is the modulus for shear between longitudinal and transverse directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d"

An *fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d*
is a column normalised orthotropic symmetric fourth order tensor in 3D space, characterized by nine independent values.

The values stem:[E_{11}], stem:[E_{22}], stem:[E_{33}], stem:[nu_12], stem:[nu_23], stem:[nu_31], stem:[G_{12}], stem:[G_{23}], and stem:[G_{31}] give terms in the stem:[6 xx 6] array representation of the *inverse* of the tensor as follows:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
\left(
\begin{array}{cccccc}
{\displaystyle \frac{1}{E_{11}}} & -{\displaystyle \frac{\nu_{12}}{E_{22}}} &
-{\displaystyle \frac{\nu_{13}}{E_{33}}} & 0      & 0      & 0      \\
            & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{E_{22}}}    & -{\displaystyle
\frac{\nu_{23}}{E_{33}}} & 0      & 0      & 0      \\
            &                & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{E_{33}}}
& 0      & 0     & 0      \\
            &                &        &  {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G_{12}}}
& 0     & 0      \\
         &\mbox{symmetric} & &   & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G_{23}}}
& 0      \\
            &                &        &        &        & {\displaystyle
\frac{1}{G_{31}}}
\end{array}
\right)
++++

For an elasticity tensor:

* stem:[E_{11}] is the Young's modulus in the 1 axis direction;
* stem:[E_{22}] is the Young's modulus in the 2 axis direction;
* stem:[E_{33}] is the Young's modulus in the 3 axis direction;
* stem:[nu_{12}] is the Poisson's ratio for transverse strain in the 2 direction when uniaxially stressed in the 1 direction, stem:[nu_{12} = - varepsilon_2 / varepsilon_1] when stem:[s_{ij} = 0] for stem:[i,j != 1];
* stem:[nu_{13}] is the Poisson's ratio for transverse strain in the 3 direction when uniaxially stressed in the 1 direction, stem:[nu_{13} = - varepsilon_3 / varepsilon_1] when stem:[s_{ij} = 0] for stem:[i,j != 1];
* stem:[nu_{23}] is the Poisson's ratio for transverse strain in the 3 direction when uniaxially stressed in the 2 direction;
stem:[nu_{23} = - varepsilon_3 / varepsilon_2] when stem:[s_{ij} = 0] for stem:[i,j != 2];
* stem:[G_{12}] is the modulus for shear in the 1-2 plane;
* stem:[G_{23}] is the modulus for shear in the 2-3 plane;
* stem:[G_{31}] is the modulus for shear in the 3-1 plane;
* the terms are shown for the inverse of an elasticity tensor, which is a compliance tensor.

NOTE: The Poisson's ratio convention used here and symmetry results in stem:[E_{i i} nu_{ji} = E_{jj} nu_{ij}].

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[1]"

the term stem:[E_{11}] defined above, which is the Young's modulus in the 1 direction for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[2]"

the term stem:[E_{22}] defined above, which is the Young's modulus in the 2 direction for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[3]"

the term stem:[E_{33}] defined above, which is the Young's modulus in the 3 direction for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[4]"

the term stem:[nu_12] defined above, which is the Poisson's ratio between the 1 and 2 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[5]"

the term stem:[nu_13] defined above, which is the Poisson's ratio between the 1 and 3 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[6]"

the term stem:[nu_23] defined above, which is the Poisson's ratio between the 2 and 3 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[7]"

the term stem:[G_{12}] defined above, which is the modulus between the 1 and 2 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[8]"

the term stem:[G_{23}] defined above, which is the modulus between the 2 and 3 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_orthotropic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[9]"

the term stem:[G_{31}] defined above, which is the modulus between the 3 and 1 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d"

An *fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d*
is a column normalised monoclinic symmetric fourth order tensor in 3D space, which is characterized by thirteen independent values.

The values stem:[E_{11}], stem:[E_{22}], stem:[E_{33}], stem:[nu_12], stem:[nu_13], stem:[nu_23], stem:[G_{23}], stem:[G_{31}], stem:[G_{12}], stem:[nu_{1,12}], stem:[nu_{2,12}], stem:[nu_{3,12}], and stem:[nu_{23,31}] give terms in the stem:[6 xx 6] array representation of the *inverse* of the tensor as follows:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
   \left(
\begin{array}{cccccc}
{\displaystyle \frac{1}{E_{11}}} & -{\displaystyle \frac{\nu_{12}}{E_{22}}} &
-{\displaystyle \frac{\nu_{13}}{E_{33}}}
&  {\displaystyle \frac{\nu_{1,12}}{G_{12}}} & 0      & 0
\\
            & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{E_{22}}}    & -{\displaystyle
\frac{\nu_{23}}{E_{22}}}
&  {\displaystyle \frac{\nu_{2,12}}{G_{12}}} & 0      & 0
\\
            &                & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{E_{33}}}
&  {\displaystyle \frac{\nu_{3,12}}{G_{12}}} & 0      & 0       \\
            &                &        &  {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G_{12}}}   &
                              0       & 0
\\
         &\mbox{symmetric} & &     & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{G_{23}}}
    &  {\displaystyle \frac{\nu_{23,31}}{G_{31}}}
\\
            &                &        &        &        & {\displaystyle
\frac{1}{G_{31}}}
\end{array}
\right)
++++

For an elasticity tensor:

* stem:[E_{11}] is the Young's modulus in the 1 axis direction;
* stem:[E_{22}] is the Young's modulus in the 2 axis direction;
* stem:[E_{33}] is the Young's modulus in the 3 axis direction;
* stem:[nu_{12}] is the Poisson's ratio for transverse strain in the 2 direction when uniaxially stressed in the 1 direction, stem:[E_{11} nu{21} = E_{22} nu{12}];
* stem:[nu_{13}] is the Poisson's ratio for transverse strain in the 3 direction when uniaxially stressed in the 1 direction, stem:[E_{11} nu{31} = E_{33} nu{13}];
* stem:[nu_{23}] is the Poisson's ratio for transverse strain in the 3 direction when uniaxially stressed in the 2 direction, stem:[E_{22} nu{32} = E_{33} nu{23}];
* stem:[G_{23}] is the modulus for shear in the 2-3 plane;
* stem:[G_{31}] is the modulus for shear in the 3-1 plane;
* stem:[G_{12}] is the modulus for shear in the 1-2 plane;
* stem:[nu_{1,12}] is the coupling between the 1 axis extension and shear in the 1-2 plane, stem:[E_{11} nu_{1,12} = G_{12} nu{12,1}];
* stem:[nu_{2,12}] is the coupling between the 2 axis extension and shear in the 1-2 plane, stem:[E_{22} nu_{2,12} = G_{12} nu{12,2}];
* stem:[nu_{3,12}] is the coupling between the 3 axis extension and shear in the 1-2 plane, stem:[E_{33} nu{3,12} = G_{12} nu{12,3}];
* stem:[nu_{23,31}] is the coupling between shears in the 2-3 and the 3-1 planes, stem:[G_{23} nu_{23,31} = G_{13} nu_{31,23}];
* the terms are shown for the inverse of an elasticity tensor, which is a compliance tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[1]"

the term stem:[E_{11}] defined above, which is the Young's modulus in the 1 direction for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[2]"

the term stem:[E_{22}] defined above, which is the Young's modulus in the 2 direction for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[3]"

the term stem:[E_{33}] defined above, which is the Young's modulus in the 3 direction for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[4]"

the term stem:[nu_12] defined above, which is the Poisson's ratio between the 1 and 2 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[5]"

the term stem:[nu_13] defined above, which is the Poisson's ratio between the 1 and 3 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[6]"

the term stem:[nu_23] defined above, which is the Poisson's ratio between the 2 and 3 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[7]"

the term stem:[G_{23}] defined above, which is the modulus between the 2 and 3 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[8]"

the term stem:[G_{31}] defined above, which is the modulus between the 3 and 1 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[9]"

the term stem:[G_{12}] defined above, which is the modulus between the 1 and 2 directions for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[10]"

the term stem:[nu_{1,12}] defined above, which is the coupling between 1 extension and 1/2 plane shear for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[11]"

the term stem:[nu_{2,12}] defined above, which is the coupling between 2 extension and 1/2 plane shear for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[12]"

the term stem:[nu_{3,12}] defined above, which is the coupling between 3 extension and 1/2 plane shear for an elasticity tensor.

*)

(*"fea_scalar_vector_tensor_schema.fea_column_normalised_monoclinic_symmetric_tensor4_3d.ARRAY[13]"

the term stem:[nu_{23,31}] defined above, which is the coupling between 2/3 and 3/1 plane shear for an elasticity tensor.

*)

