(*"geometry_schema.dummy_gri"
A **dummy_gri** is a partial entity definition to be used when types of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> are constructed. It provides the correct supertypes and the <<express:representation_schema.representation_item.name,name>> attribute as an empty string.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dimension_count"
A **dimension_count** is a positive integer used to define the coordinate space dimensionality of a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dimension_count.wr:WR1"
A **dimension_count** shall be positive.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_form"
The **b_spline_curve_form** enumerated type is used to indicate that the <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve,b_spline_curve>> represents a part of a curve of some specific form.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_form.polyline_form"
a connected sequence of line segments represented by degree 1 B-spline basis functions;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_form.circular_arc"
an arc of a circle, or a complete circle represented by a B-spline curve;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_form.elliptic_arc"
an arc of ellipse, or a complete ellipse represented by a B-spline curve;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_form.parabolic_arc"
an arc of finite length of a parabola represented by a B-spline curve;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_form.hyperbolic_arc"
an arc of finite length of an hyperbola represented by a B-spline curve;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_form.unspecified"
a curve for which no particular form is specified;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form"
The **b_spline_surface_form** enumerated type is used to indicate that the <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface,b_spline_surface>> represents a part of a surface of some specific form.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.plane_surf"
a bounded portion of a plane represented by a B-spline surface of degree 1 in each parameter;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.cylindrical_surf"
a bounded portion of a cylindrical surface;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.conical_surf"
a bounded portion of a right circular cone;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.spherical_surf"
a bounded portion of a sphere, or a complete sphere, represented by a B-spline surface;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.toroidal_surf"
a torus, or portion of a torus, represented by a B-spline surface;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.surf_of_revolution"
a bounded portion of a surface of revolution;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.ruled_surf"
a surface constructed from two parametric curves by joining with straight lines corresponding points with the same parameter value on each of the curves.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.generalised_cone"
a special case of a ruled surface in which the second curve degenerates to a single point; when represented by a B-spline surface all the control points along one edge will be coincident;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.quadric_surf"
a bounded portion of one of the class of surfaces of degree 2 in the variables x, y and z;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.surf_of_linear_extrusion"
a bounded portion of a surface of linear extrusion represented by a B-spline surface of degree 1 in one of the parameters;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_form.unspecified"
a surface for which no particular form is specified;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.extent_enumeration"
The **extent_enumeration** type is used to describe the quantitive extent of an object.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.extent_enumeration.invalid"
the concept of extent is not valid for the quantity being measured;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.extent_enumeration.zero"
the extent is zero;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.extent_enumeration.finite_non_zero"
the extent is finite (bounded) but not zero;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.extent_enumeration.infinite"
the extent is not finite;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.knot_type"
The **knot_type** indicates that the B-spline knots have a particularly simple form enabling the knots themselves to be defaulted;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.knot_type.uniform_knots"
the form of knots appropriate for a uniform B-spline curve or surface;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.knot_type.unspecified"
the type of knots is not specified; this includes the case of non uniform knots;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.knot_type.quasi_uniform_knots"
the form of knots appropriate for a quasi-uniform B-spline curve or surface;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.knot_type.piecewise_bezier_knots"
the form of knots appropriate for a piecewise Bézier curve;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.linearly_independent_enum"
This type is used to indicate that a locally refined spline has been tested for linear independence.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.linearly_independent_enum.independent"
the set of combined B-spline functions is linearly independent;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.linearly_independent_enum.not_independent"
the set of B-spline functions is linearly dependent;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.linearly_independent_enum.not_tested"
the set of B-spline functions has not been tested for linear dependence.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum"
This type is used to indicate the type of the original locally refined spline to enable the standardized spline representation to be unpacked to this original format.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.__note"
See [6], [7] and [15] for further details of the specific types of locally refined splines.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.analysis_suitable_t_spline"
the local B-spline is of the analysis suitable t-spline type;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.hierarchical_b_spline"
the local B-spline is of the hierachical b-spline type;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.lr_b_spline"
the local B-spline is of the lr-b-spline type;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.semi_standard_t_spline"
the local B-spline is of the semi-standard t-spline type;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.standard_t_spline"
the local B-spline is of the standard t-spline type;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.preferred_surface_curve_representation"
The **preferred_surface_curve_representation** type is used to indicate the preferred form of representation for a surface curve, which is either a curve in geometric space or in the parametric space of the underlying surfaces;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.preferred_surface_curve_representation.curve_3d"
the curve in three-dimensional space is preferred;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.preferred_surface_curve_representation.pcurve_s1"
the first pcurve is preferred;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.preferred_surface_curve_representation.pcurve_s2"
the second pcurve is preferred;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spline_knot_values"
The **spline_knot_values** type defines a list of non-repeated real values in ascending order. It is used to represent the knot values in the definition of local B-splines. A local B-spline does not need to use all consecutive knots, and multiple use of knots may occur.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spline_knot_values.wr:WR1"
The knot values shall be strictly increasing in size.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.transition_code"
The **transition_code** type conveys the continuity properties of a composite curve or surface. The continuity referred to is geometric, not parametric continuity.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.transition_code.discontinuous"
the segments, or patches, do not join; this is permitted only at the boundary of the curve or surface to indicate that it is not closed.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.transition_code.continuous"
the segments, or patches, join, but no condition on their tangents is implied;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.transition_code.cont_same_gradient"
the segments, or patches, join, and their tangent vectors, or tangent planes, are parallel and have the same direction at the joint; equality of derivatives is not required;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.transition_code.cont_same_gradient_same_curvature"
the gradient and curvature are continuous; for a curve, the segments join, their tangent vectors are parallel and in the same direction, and their curvatures are equal at the joint; equality of derivatives is not required; for a surface this implies that the principal curvatures are the same and that the principal directions are coincident along the common boundary.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimming_preference"
The **trimming_preference** type is used to indicate the preferred way of trimming a parametric curve where the trimming is multiply defined.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimming_preference.cartesian"
trimming by cartesian point is preferred;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimming_preference.parameter"
trimming by parameter value is preferred;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimming_preference.unspecified"
no trimming preference is communicated.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement"
The **axis2_placement** select type represents the placing of mutually perpendicular axes in two-dimensional or in three-dimensional Cartesian space.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement.__note"
This select type enables entities requiring axis placement information to reference the axes without specifying the space dimensionality.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.curve_on_surface"
The **curve_on_surface** is a curve on a parametric surface. It may be a <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>, or a <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve,surface_curve>> including the specialised subtypes of <<express:geometry_schema.intersection_curve,intersection_curve>> and <<express:geometry_schema.seam_curve,seam_curve>>, or a <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface,composite_curve_on_surface>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_or_surface"
This select type enables a surface curve to identify as an attribute the associated <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>> surface or <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>> .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_boundary"
This type is used to select the type of bounding curve to be used in the definition of a <<express:geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface,curve_bounded_surface>> . It provides for the boundary to be either a <<express:geometry_schema.boundary_curve,boundary_curve>> or a <<express:geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve,degenerate_pcurve>> .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimming_select"
This select type identifies the two possible ways of trimming a parametric curve, by a cartesian point on the curve, or by a REAL number defining a parameter value within the parametric range of the curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector_or_direction"
This type is used to identify the types of entity which can participate in vector computations.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context"
A **geometric_representation_context** is a type of <<express:representation_schema.representation_context,representation_context>> in which <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> s are geometrically founded.

A **geometric_representation_context** is a distinct coordinate space, spatially unrelated to other coordinate spaces except as those coordinate spaces are specifically related by an appropriate transformation. (See 3.2 for definitions of geometrically founded and coordinate space.)
*)


(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context.coordinate_space_dimension"
the number of dimensions of the coordinate space which is the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>
*)


(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context.coordinate_space_dimension.__note"
Any constraints on the allowed range of *coordinate_space_dimension* are outside the scope of this document.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item"
A **geometric_representation_item** is a type of <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>> that has the additional meaning of having geometric position or orientation or both.

This meaning is present by virtue of:

* being a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> or a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> ;
* referencing directly a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> or a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> ;
* referencing indirectly a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> or a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> .
*)

(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.__example"
Consider a circle. It gains its geometric position and orientation by virtue of a reference to an <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement,axis2_placement>> that in turn references a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> and several <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> s.
*)
(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.__example"
A <<express:geometric_model_schema.manifold_solid_brep,manifold_solid_brep>> is a *geometric_representation_item* that through several layers of <<express:topology_schema.topological_representation_item,topological_representation_item>> s, references <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>> s, <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>> s and <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> s. Through additional intervening entities curves and surfaces reference <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> and <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> .
*)

(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.__note"
An indirect reference to a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> or a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> means that a given *geometric_representation_item* references the <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> or <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> through one or more intervening attributes. In many cases this information is given in the form of an <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement,axis2_placement>> .
*)

(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.__note"
The intervening entities, which are all of type <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>> , need not be of subtype *geometric_representation_item* . Consider the <<express:geometric_model_schema.manifold_solid_brep,manifold_solid_brep>> from the above example. One of the intervening levels of <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>> is a <<express:topology_schema.closed_shell,closed_shell>> . This is a <<express:topology_schema.topological_representation_item,topological_representation_item>> and does not require a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> in its own right. When used as part of the definition of a <<express:geometric_model_schema.manifold_solid_brep,manifold_solid_brep>> that itself is a *geometric_representation_item* , it is founded in a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> .
*)

(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.__note"
A *geometric_representation_item* inherits the need to be related to a <<express:representation_schema.representation_context,representation_context>> in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.__note"
As subtypes of <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>> there is an implicit and/or relationship between *geometric_representation_item* and <<express:topology_schema.topological_representation_item,topological_representation_item>> . The only complex instances intended to be created are <<express:topology_schema.edge_curve,edge_curve>> , <<express:topology_schema.face_surface,face_surface>> , and <<express:topology_schema.vertex_point,vertex_point>>
*)

(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.dim"
the coordinate <<express:geometry_schema.dimension_count,dimension_count>> of the **geometric_representation_item**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.wr:WR1"
The context of any representation referencing a **geometric_representation_item"** shall be a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.wr:WR1.__note"
The *dim* attribute is derived from the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context.coordinate_space_dimension,coordinate_space_dimension>> of a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> in which the *geometric_representation_item* is geometrically founded.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.wr:WR1.__note"
A *geometric_representation_item* is geometrically founded in one or more <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> s, all of which have the same <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context.coordinate_space_dimension,coordinate_space_dimension>> . See the rule <<express:geometry_schema.compatible_dimension,compatible_dimension>> .
*)

(*"geometry_schema.point"
A **point** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> and is a location in some real Cartesian coordinate space stem:[RR^m] , for stem:[m] = 1, 2 or 3.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_point"
A **cartesian_point** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> defined by its coordinates in a rectangular Cartesian coordinate system, or in a parameter space. The entity is defined in a one, two or three-dimensional space as determined by the number of coordinates in the list.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_point.__note"
For the purposes of defining geometry in this document only two or three-dimensional points are used.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_point.__note"
Depending upon the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> in which the point is used the names of the coordinates may be stem:[(x,y,z)], or stem:[(u,v)], or any other chosen values.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_point.coordinates"
the ordered list of 1, 2 or 3 coordinate values, according to the dimensionality of the space in which the point is defined;

**coordinates[1]** The component in the direction of the first axis, X or, in parameter space u;

**coordinates[2]** The component in the direction of the second axis Y, or in a parameter space v;

**coordinates[3]** The component in the direction of the Z axis; this will not be present in the case of a **cartesian_point** in a two-dimensional coordinate space.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_point\geometric_representation_item.dim"
The dimensionality of the space in which the **cartesian_point** is defined. This is an inherited derived attribute from the geometric representation item supertype and for a cartesian point is determined by the number of coordinates in the list.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_point"
A **cylindrical_point** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> which uses a cylindrical polar coordinate system, centred at the origin of the corresponding Cartesian coordinate system, to define its location.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_point.r"
the distance from the point to the z axis;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_point.theta"
the angle between the plane containing the point and the z axis and the xz plane;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_point.z"
the distance from the xy plane to the point.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_point.coordinates"
the rectangular cartesian coordinates of this point;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_point.wr:WR1"
The radius **r** shall be greater than, or equal to zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_point.wr:IP1"
The value of **theta** shall lie in the range stem:[0 le theta < 360 "P{degree}"].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_point"
A **spherical_point** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> which uses a spherical polar coordinate system, centred at the origin of the corresponding Cartesian coordinate system, to define its location.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_point.r"
the distance from the point to the z axis;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_point.theta"
the angle stem:[theta] between the z axis and the line joining the origin to the point;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_point.phi"
the angle stem:[phi] measured from the x axis to the projection onto the xy plane of the line from the origin to the point.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_point.coordinates"
the rectangular cartesian coordinates of this point;
*)

(*"geometry_schema.spherical_point.coordinates.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig2]]
.Spherical point attributes
====
image::Geomfig2.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.spherical_point.coordinates.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig2>> for an illustration of the attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.spherical_point.wr:WR1"
The radius **r** shall be greater than, or equal to zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_point.wr:IP1"
The value of **theta** shall lie in the range stem:[0 le theta le 180 "P{degree}"].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_point.wr:IP2"
The value of **phi** shall lie in the range stem:[0 le phi < 360 "P{degree}"].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.polar_point"
A **polar_point** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> which uses a two dimensional polar coordinate system, centred at the origin of the corresponding Cartesian coordinate system, to define its location.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.polar_point.r"
the distance from the point to the origin;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.polar_point.theta"
the angle between the x axis and the line joining the origin to the point.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.polar_point.coordinates"
the rectangular cartesian coordinates of this point;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.polar_point.wr:WR1"
The radius **r** shall be greater than, or equal to zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.polar_point.wr:IP1"
The value of **theta** shall lie in the range stem:[0 le theta < 360 "P{degree}"].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_on_curve"
A **point_on_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> which lies on a <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>> . The point is determined by evaluating the <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>> at a specific parameter value. The coordinate space dimensionality of the point is that of the **basis_curve**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_on_curve.basis_curve"
the <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>> to which **point_parameter** relates;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_on_curve.point_parameter"
the parameter value of the point location;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_on_curve\geometric_representation_item.dim"
the dimensionality of the space in which the **point_on_curve** is defined. This is the same as that of the **basis_curve**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_on_curve.wr:IP1"
The value of the **point_parameter** shall not be outside the parametric range of the **basis_curve**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_on_surface"
A **point_on_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> which lies on a <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>> . The point is determined by evaluating the <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>> at a specific pair of parameter values.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_on_surface.basis_surface"
the <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>> to which parameter values relate;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_on_surface.point_parameter_u"
the first parameter value of the point location;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_on_surface.point_parameter_v"
the second parameter value of the point location.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_on_surface.wr:IP1"
The parametric values specified for u and v shall not be outside the parametric range of the **basis_surface**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_in_volume"
A **point_in_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> which lies inside, or on the the surface of, a <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> . The point is determined by evaluating the <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> at the specified parameter values.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_in_volume.basis_volume"
the <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> to which parameter values relate;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_in_volume.point_parameter_u"
the first parameter value of the point location;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_in_volume.point_parameter_v"
the second parameter value of the point location.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_in_volume.point_parameter_w"
the third parameter value of the point location.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_in_volume.wr:IP1"
The parametric values specified for u, v and w shall not be outside the parametric range of the **basis_volume**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_replica"
A **point_replica** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> that defines a replica of an existing point (the parent) in a different location. The replica has the same coordinate space dimensionality as the parent point.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_replica.parent_pt"
the <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> to be replicated;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_replica.transformation"
the Cartesian transformation operator which defines the location of the point replica.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_replica.wr:WR1"
The coordinate space dimensionality of the **transformation** attribute shall be the same as that of the **parent_pt**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.point_replica.wr:WR2"
A **point_replica** shall not participate in its own definition.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve"
A **degenerate_pcurve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> that is defined as a parameter space curve, but in three-dimensional model space it collapses to a single point. It is thus a subtype of <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> , not of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>> .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve.__note"
For example, the apex of a cone could be represented as a *degenerate_pcurve* .
*)

(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve.basis_surface"
the surface on which the **degenerate_pcurve** lies;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve.reference_to_curve"
the association of the **degenerate_pcurve** and the parameter space curve which degenerates to the (equivalent) point.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve.wr:WR1"
The set of items in the <<express:representation_schema.definitional_representation,definitional_representation>> entity corresponding to the **reference_to_curve** shall have exactly one element
*)


(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve.wr:WR2"
The unique item in the set shall be a <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>> .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve.wr:WR3"
The dimensionality of this parameter space curve shall be 2.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve.wr:IP1"
Regarded as a curve in model space, the **degenerate_pcurve** shall have zero arc length.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.evaluated_degenerate_pcurve"
A **evaluated_degenerate_pcurve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve,degenerate_pcurve>> which gives the result of evaluating the <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>> and associates it with the corresponding <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.evaluated_degenerate_pcurve.__note"
For example, the apex of a cone could be represented as a *evaluated_degenerate_pcurve* .
*)

(*"geometry_schema.evaluated_degenerate_pcurve.equivalent_point"
the explicit point in the geometric coordinate system represented by the degenerate pcurve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.direction"
A **direction** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> that defines a general direction vector in two or three dimensional space. The actual magnitudes of the components have no effect upon the direction being defined, only the ratios x:y:z or x:y, or, in parameter space u:v, are significant.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.direction.__note"
The components of this entity are not normalised. If a unit vector is required it should be normalised before use.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.direction.direction_ratios"
list of REAL;

**direction_ratios[1]** The component in the direction of the first axis, X or, in parameter space u;

**direction_ratios[2]** The component in the direction of the second axis Y, or in a parameter space v;

**direction_ratios[3]** The component in the direction of the Z axis; this will not be present in the case of a direction in a two-dimensional coordinate space.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.direction.direction_ratios.__note"
The *direction_ratios* attribute is a list, the individual elements of this list are defined below.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.direction\geometric_representation_item.dim"
the coordinate space dimensionality of the **direction**. This is an inherited attribute of the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> supertype; for this entity it is determined by the number of **direction_ratios** in the list.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.direction.wr:WR1"
The magnitude of the direction vector shall be greater than zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector"
A **vector** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> that defines a vector in terms of the direction and the magnitude of the vector.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector.__note"
The magnitude of the vector must not be calculated from the components of the *orientation* attribute. This form of representation was selected to reduce problems with numerical instability. For example a vector of magnitude 2.0 mm and equally inclined to the coordinate axes could be represented with orientation attribute of (1.0,1.0,1.0) and magnitude attribute 2.0.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.vector.orientation"
the direction of the **vector**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector.magnitude"
the magnntude of the **vector**. All **vector** s of **magnitude** 0.0 are regarded as equal in value regardless of the **orientation** attribute.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector\geometric_representation_item.dim"
the coordinate space dimensionality of the **vector**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector.wr:WR1"
The **magnitude** shall be positive or zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.placement"
A **placement** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> that locates a geometric item with respect to the coordinate system of its geometric context. It locates the item to be defined and, in the case of the axis placement subtypes, gives its orientation.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.placement.location"
the geometric position of a reference point, such as the centre of a circle, of the item to be located
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis1_placement"
A **axis1_placement** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.placement,placement>> , that represents the direction and location in three-dimensional space of a single axis. An **axis1_placement** is defined in terms of a locating point (inherited from the placement supertype) and an axis direction; this is either the direction of **axis** or defaults to (0.0,0.0,1.0). The actual direction for the **axis1_placement** is given by the derived attribute **z**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis1_placement\placement.location"
a reference point on the **axis**
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis1_placement.axis"
the direction of the local Z axis;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis1_placement.z"
the normalised direction of the local Z axis;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis1_placement\geometric_representation_item.dim"
the coordinate space dimensionality of the **axis1_placement** which is determined from its **location**, and is always equal to 3.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis1_placement.wr:WR1"
The coordinate space dimensionality shall be 3.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_2d"
A **axis2_placement_2d** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.placement,placement>> that represents the location and orientation in two-dimensional space of two mutually perpendicular axes. An **axis2_placement_2d** is defined in terms of a point, (inherited from the placement supertype), and an axis. It can be used to locate and orientate an object in two-dimensional space and to define a placement coordinate system. The entity includes a point which forms the origin of the placement coordinate system. A direction vector is required to complete the definition of the placement coordinate system. The **ref_direction** defines the placement X axis direction; the placement Y axis direction is derived from this.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_2d\placement.location"
the spatial position of the reference point which defines the origin of the associated placement coordinate system.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_2d.ref_direction"
the direction used to determine the direction of the local X axis. If **ref_direction** is omitted, this direction is taken from the geometric coordinate system;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_2d.p"
the axis set for the placement coordinate system.

**p[1]** The normalised direction of the placement X axis. This is (1.0,0.0) if **ref_direction** is omitted.

**p[2]** The normalised direction of the placement Y axis. This is a derived attribute and is orthogonal to **p[1]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_2d.wr:WR1"
The space dimensionality of the **axis2_placement_2d** shall be 2.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d"
A **axis2_placement_3d** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.placement,placement>> that represents the location and orientation in three-dimensional space of two mutually perpendicular axes. An **axis2_placement_3d** is defined in terms of a point, (inherited from the placement supertype), and two (ideally orthogonal) axes. It can be used to locate and orientate a non axi-symmetric object in space and to define a placement coordinate system. The entity includes a point which forms the origin of the placement coordinate system. Two direction vectors are required to complete the definition of the placement coordinate system. The **axis** is the placement Z axis direction and the **ref_direction** is an approximation to the placement X axis direction. A placement coordinate system referenced by the parametric equations is derived from the **axis2_placement_3d** data for conic curves and elementary surfaces.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.__note"
Let *z* be the placement Z axis direction and *a* be the approximate placement X axis direction. There are two methods, mathematically identical but numerically different, for calculating the placement X and Y axis directions.

. The vector stem:[a] is projected onto the plane defined by the origin point stem:[P] and the vector stem:[bb z] to give the placement X axis direction as
stem:[x = langle a - (a cdot z) z rangle ].
The placement Y axis direction is then given by stem:[y = langle z xx x rangle ].

. The placement Y axis direction is calculated as stem:[y = langle z xx a rangle ] and then the placement X axis direction is given by
stem:[x = langle y xx z rangle ].

The first method is likely to be the more numerically stable of the two, and is used here.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.placement.location"
the spatial position of the reference point which defines the origin of the associated placement coordinate system.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.axis"
the exact direction of the local Z axis;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.ref_direction"
the direction used to determine the direction of the local X axis; if necessary an adjustment is made to maintain orthogonality to the **axis** direction. If **axis** and/or **ref_direction** is omitted, these directions are taken from the geometric coordinate system;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p"
the axes for the placement coordinate system. The directions of these axes are derived from the attributes, with appropriate default values if required.

**p[1]** The normalised direction of the local X axis.

**p[2]** The normalised direction of the local Y axis.

**p[3]** The normalised direction of the local Z axis.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig3]]
.Axis2_placement_3d
====
image::Geomfig3.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig3>> for interpretation of attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.wr:WR1"
The space dimensionality of the <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>> shall be 3.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.wr:WR2"
The space dimensionality of **axis** shall be 3.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.wr:WR3"
The space dimensionality of **ref_direction** shall be 3.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.wr:WR4"
The **axis** and the **ref_direction** shall not be parallel or anti-parallel. (This is required by the <<express:geometry_schema.build_axes,build_axes>> function.)
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator"
A **cartesian_transformation_operator** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> and <<express:representation_schema.functionally_defined_transformation,functionally_defined_transformation>> , that defines a geometric transformation composed of translation, rotation, mirroring and uniform scaling. The list of normalised vectors **u** defines the columns of an orthogonal matrix stem:[T]. These vectors are computed, by the <<express:geometry_schema.base_axis,base_axis>> function, from the direction attributes **axis1, axis2** and, in **cartesian_transformation_operator_3d, axis3**.

If stem:[det(T) = -1], the transformation includes mirroring.

The local origin point **A**, the scale value stem:[S] and the matrix stem:[bbT] together define a transformation.

The transformation for a <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> with position vector **P** is defined by

stem:[bb"P" -> bb"A" + bb"S TP"]

The transformation for a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> **d** is defined by

stem:[bb"d" -> bb"Td"]

The transformation for a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> with <<express:geometry_schema.vector.orientation,orientation>> stem:[d] and <<express:geometry_schema.vector.magnitude,magnitude>> stem:[k] is defined by

stem:[bb"d" -> bb"Td"] and stem:[k -> Sk]

For those entities whose attributes include an <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement,axis2_placement>>, the transformation is applied, after the derivation, to the derived attributes **p** defining the placement coordinate directions. For a transformed <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>>, the direction of the surface normal at any point is obtained by transforming the normal, at the corresponding point, to the original <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>>. The parametrisation of the transformed surface is defined using the transformed value of **p** as defined above. For geometric entities with attributes (such as the radius of a circle) which have the dimensionality of length, the values will be multiplied by stem:[S].

For transformations involving reflection or mirroring, with stem:[det(T) = -1], the relationship between the sense of the boundary and the interior of a <<express:geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface,curve_bounded_surface>>, or <<express:topology_schema.face_surface,face_surface>> is affected.

For a <<express:geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface,curve_bounded_surface>> if **n** is the direction of the surface normal and **t** is the direction of the tangent vector at a point on the boundary after transformation, then the interior is in the direction stem:[det(T) n xx t].

For a <<express:topology_schema.face,face>> or <<express:topology_schema.face_surface,face_surface>>, if stem:[det(T) = -1] the interior of the transformed face will lie to the right when traversing the bounding loops in the positive sense.

For curves on surface the <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve.reference_to_curve,reference_to_curve>> will be unaffected by any transformation.

The **cartesian_transformation_operator** shall only be applied to geometry defined in a consistent system of units with the same units on each axis. With all optional attributes omitted, the transformation defaults to the identity transformation. The **cartesian_transformation_operator** shall only be instantiated as one of its subtypes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig4a]]
.Cartesian_transformation_operator_3d
====
image::Geomfig4a.gif[]
====
*)
(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig4b]]
.Cartesian_transformation_operator_3d
====
image::Geomfig4b.gif[]
====
*)
(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig4c]]
.Cartesian_transformation_operator_3d
====
image::Geomfig4c.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig4a>>, <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig4b>>, and <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig4c>> for demonstration of effect of transformation.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.axis1"
the direction used to determine **u[1]**, the derived X axis direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.axis2"
the direction used to determine **u[2]**, the derived Y axis direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.local_origin"
the required translation, specified as a cartesian point. The actual translation included in the transformation is from the geometric origin to the local origin;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.scale"
the scaling value specified for the transformation;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.scl"
the derived scale stem:[S] of the transformation, equal to **scale** if that exists, or 1.0 otherwise.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.wr:WR1"
The derived scaling factor **scl** shall be greater than zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_3d"
A **cartesian_transformation_operator_3d** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator,cartesian_transformation_operator>>, that defines a geometric transformation in three-dimensional space composed of translation, rotation, mirroring and uniform scaling. The list of normalised vectors **u** defines the columns of an orthogonal matrix **T**. These vectors are computed from the direction attributes <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.axis1,axis1>>, <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.axis2,axis2>> and **axis3** by the <<express:geometry_schema.base_axis,base_axis>> function. If stem:[det(T) = -1], the transformation includes mirroring.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_3d\cartesian_transformation_operator.axis1"
the direction used to determine **u[1]**, the derived X axis direction. If necessary, **u[1]** is adjusted to make it orthogonal to **u[3]**;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_3d\cartesian_transformation_operator.axis2"
the direction used to determine **u[2]**, the derived Y axis direction. If necessary, **u[2]** is adjusted to make it orthogonal to **u[3]** and **u[1]**;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_3d.axis3"
the exact direction of **u[3]**, the derived Z axis direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_3d\cartesian_transformation_operator.local_origin"
the required translation, specified as a cartesian point. The actual translation included in the transformation is from the geometric origin to the local origin.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_3d\cartesian_transformation_operator.scale"
the scaling value specified for the transformation;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_3d\cartesian_transformation_operator.scale"
the derived scale stem:[S] of the transformation, equal to **scale** if that exists, or 1.0 otherwise;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_3d.u"
the list of mutually orthogonal, normalised vectors defining the transformation matrix **T**. They are derived from the explicit attributes **axis3**, <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.axis1,axis1>>, and <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.axis2,axis2>> in that order;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_3d.wr:WR1"
The coordinate space dimensionality shall be 3.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_2d"
A **cartesian_transformation_operator_2d** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator,cartesian_transformation_operator>>, that defines a geometric transformation in two-dimensional space composed of translation, rotation, mirroring and uniform scaling. The list of normalised vectors **u** defines the columns of an orthogonal matrix **T**. These vectors are computed from the direction attributes <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.axis1,axis1>> and <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.axis2,axis2>> by the <<express:geometry_schema.base_axis,base_axis>> function. If stem:[det(T) = -1], the transformation includes mirroring.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_2d\cartesian_transformation_operator.axis1"
the direction used to determine **u[1]**, the derived X axis direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_2d\cartesian_transformation_operator.axis2"
the direction used to determine **u[2]**, the derived Y axis direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_2d\cartesian_transformation_operator.local_origin"
the required translation, specified as a cartesian point. The actual translation included in the transformation is from the geometric origin to the local origin.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_2d\cartesian_transformation_operator.scale"
the scaling value specified for the transformation;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_2d\cartesian_transformation_operator.scale"
the derived scale stem:[S] of the transformation, equal to **scale** if that exists, or 1.0 otherwise;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_2d.u"
the list of mutually orthogonal, normalised vectors defining the transformation matrix **T**. They are derived from the explicit attributes <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.axis1,axis1>>, and <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator.axis2,axis2>> in that order;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_2d.wr:WR1"
The coordinate space dimensionality shall be 2.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve"
A **curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>>, that can be envisioned as the path of a point moving in its coordinate space.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve.wr:IP1"
A **curve** shall be arcwise connected.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve.wr:IP2"
A **curve** shall have an arc length greater than zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve.wr:IP3"
Where definitions of **curve** parameterisation are not defined in parametric or geometric terms, a **curve** shall be assumed by default to have arc length parameterisation.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve.wr:IP3.__note"
IP3 is based on the "Fundamental concepts and assumptions" in clause 4.2, in particular, "4.2.3 Parametrisation of analytic curves and surfaces".
*)

(*"geometry_schema.line"
A **line** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>>, that is an unbounded with constant tangent direction. A **line** is defined by a <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> and a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>. The positive direction of the line is in the direction of the **dir** vector.

The line is given by:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = P + u * V
++++

[%key]
stem:[P]:: <<express:geometry_schema.line.pnt,pnt>>;
stem:[bb V]:: <<express:geometry_schema.line.dir,dir>>;

and the parametric range is stem:[-oo < u < oo].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.line.pnt"
the location of the **line** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.line.dir"
the direction of the **line** ; the magnitude and units of **dir** affect the parametrisation of the line;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.line\geometric_representation_item.dim"
the coordinate space dimensionality of the **line**. This is an inherited attribute from the geometric representation item supertype.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.line.wr:WR1"
The attributes **pnt** and **dir** shall both be 2D or both be 3D entities.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.conic"
A **conic** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>>, that is a planar curve which could be produced by intersecting a plane with a cone.

A **conic** is defined in terms of its intrinsic geometric properties rather than being described in terms of other geometry.

A **conic** entity always has a placement coordinate system defined by <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement,axis2_placement>>; the parametric representation is defined in terms of this placement coordinate system.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.conic.position"
the location and orientation of the conic. Further details of the interpretation of this attribute are given for the individual subtypes.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.circle"
A **circle** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.conic,conic>>, section defined by a radius and the location and orientation of the **circle**.

Interpretation of the data for the three dimensional case shall be as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[R]:: <<express:geometry_schema.circle.radius,radius>>;

and the circle is parametrised as follows:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = C +R(cos(u)x + sin(u)y)
++++

and the parametric range is stem:[0 le u le 360 "P{degree}"], stem:[u] is an angular parameter and when a numerical value is specified it shall use the current units for plane_angle_measure.

In the placement coordinate system defined above, the circle has the equation stem:[C = 0], where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
C(x, y, z) = x^{2} + y^{2} - R^{2}
++++

The positive sense of the circle at any point is in the tangent direction, **T**, to the curve at the point, where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
T = (-C_{y}, C_{x}, 0).
++++
*)

(*"geometry_schema.circle.__note"
A circular arc is defined by using the <<express:geometry_schema.trimmed_curve,trimmed_curve>> entity in conjunction with the *circle* entity.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.circle\conic.position.location"
this inherited attribute defines the centre of the **circle** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.circle.radius"
the radius of the circle, which shall be greater than zero;
*)

(*"geometry_schema.circle.radius.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig5]]
.Circle
====
image::Geomfig5.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.circle.radius.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig5>> for interpretation of attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.ellipse"
An **ellipse** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.conic,conic>>, section defined by the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor diameters and the position (center or mid point of the line joining the foci) and orientation of the curve.

Interpretation of the data shall be as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[bb R_{1}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.ellipse.semi_axis_1,semi_axis_1>>;
stem:[bb R_{2}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.ellipse.semi_axis_2,semi_axis_2>>;

and the **ellipse** is parametrised as follows:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = C +R_{1}(cos(u)x) + R_{2}( sin(u)y)
++++

and the parametric range is stem:[0 le u le 360 "P{degree}"], stem:[u] is an angular parameter and when a numerical value is specified it shall use the current units for plane_angle_measure.

In the placement coordinate system defined above, the **ellipse** has the equation stem:[C = 0], where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
C (x, y, z) = {x/(R_{1})}^{2} + {y/(R_{2})}^{2} - 1
++++

The positive sense of the **ellipse** at any point is in the tangent direction, **T**, to the curve at the point, where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
T =(-C_{y}, C_{x}, 0).
++++
*)

(*"geometry_schema.ellipse.semi_axis_1"
the first radius of the **ellipse** which shall be positive.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.ellipse.semi_axis_2"
the second radius of the **ellipse** which shall be positive.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.ellipse.semi_axis_2.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig6]]
.Ellipse
====
image::Geomfig6.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.ellipse.semi_axis_2.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig6>> for interpretation of attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.hyperbola"
An **hyperbola** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.conic,conic>>, section defined by the lengths of the major and minor radii and the position (mid-point of the line joining two foci) and orientation of the curve. Interpretation of the data shall be as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[bb R_{1}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.ellipse.semi_axis,semi_axis>>;
stem:[bb R_{2}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.ellipse.semi_image_axis,semi_image_axis>>;

The hyperbola is parametrised as follows:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = C + R_{1}(cosh(u))x + R_{2}( sinh(u))y
++++

The parametrisation range is stem:[-oo < u < oo].

In the placement coordinate system defined above, the hyperbola has the equation stem:[C = 0], where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
C (x, y, z) = (x/R_{1})^{2} - (y/R_{2})^{2} - 1
++++

The positive sense of the hyperbola at any point is in the tangent direction, **T**, to the curve at the point, where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
T =(-C_{y}, C_{x}, 0).
++++

The branch of the hyperbola represented is that pointed to by the **x** direction.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.hyperbola.semi_axis"
the length of the semi-axis of the hyperbola. This is positive and is half the minimum distance between the two branches of the hyperbola.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hyperbola.semi_imag_axis"
the length of the semi-imaginary axis of the hyperbola which shall be positive;
*)

(*"geometry_schema.hyperbola.semi_imag_axis.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig7]]
.Hyperbola
====
image::Geomfig7.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.hyperbola.semi_imag_axis.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig7>> for interpretation of attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.parabola"
A **parabola** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.conic,conic>>, section defined by its focal length, position (apex), and orientation. Interpretation of the data shall be as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C_0]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[F]:: <<express:geometry_schema.parabola.focal_dist,focal_dist>>;

The parabola is parametrised as follows:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = C + F(u^{2}x + 2 * u * y).
++++

The parametrisation range is stem:[-oo < u < oo].

In the placement coordinate system defined above, the parabola has the equation stem:[C = 0], where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
C(x, y, z) = 4Fx - y^{2}.
++++

The positive sense of the parabola at any point is in the tangent direction, **T**, to the curve at the point, where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
T = (-C_{y}, C_{x}, 0).
++++
*)

(*"geometry_schema.parabola.__note"
The location and orientation of the curve: stem:[C_0] is the apex of the parabola and <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>> is the axis of symmetry.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.parabola.focal_dist"
the distance of the focal point from the apex point;
*)

(*"geometry_schema.parabola.focal_dist.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig8]]
.parabola
====
image::Geomfig8.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.parabola.focal_dist.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig8>> for interpretation of attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.parabola.wr:WR1"
The focal distance shall not be zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.clothoid"
A **clothoid** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>>, and is a planar curve in the form of a spiral. This curve has the property that the curvature varies linearly with the arc length. Interpretation of the data shall be as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[A]:: <<express:geometry_schema.clothoid.clothoid_constant,clothoid_constant>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;

The clothoid is parametrised as follows:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = C + A sqrt(pi/2) ((int_0^u cos((pi t^2)/2) dt) x + (int_0^u sin((pi t^2)/2) dt) y)
++++

The parametrisation range is stem:[-oo < u < oo].

The arc length stem:[s] of the curve, from the point stem:[C], is given by the formula: stem:[s = A u sqrt(pi/2)].

The curvature stem:[kappa] and radius of curvature stem:[rho], at any point of the curve, are related to the arc length by the formulae:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
rho = s / (A^2)
kappa = 1 / rho
++++
*)

(*"geometry_schema.clothoid.__note"
A more detailed description of the clothoid curve can be found in GIBSON[8].
*)

(*"geometry_schema.clothoid.position"
the location and orientation of the **clothoid** ;

<<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>> is the point on the clothoid with zero curvature.

stem:[p1] is the direction of the tangent to the curve at that point.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.clothoid.position.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig9]]
.Clothoid
====
image::Geomfig9.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.clothoid.position.__note"
If *position* is of type <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_2d,axis2_placement_2d>> the *clothoid* is defined in a two dimensional space.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.clothoid.position.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig9>> for interpretation of attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.clothoid.clothoid_constant"
the constant which defines the relationship between curvature and arc length for the curve;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.clothoid.wr:WR1"
The focal distance shall not be zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.circular_involute"
A **circular_involute** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>>, and is the involute of a circle. The involute of a planar curve is the locus of the end point of a thread as it is wound round the curve. If stem:[bb(P_0)] is the point where the involute meets the circle the distance from any point **P** on the involute to the tangential contact point **T** on the circle is equal to the arc length from stem:[bb(P_0)] to **T**. The **circular_involute** has a cusp at the point stem:[bb(P_0)] (stem:[u = 0]), and forms a double spiral enclosing the base circle.

Interpretation of the data shall be as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.circular_involute.position,position>>;
stem:[r]:: <<express:geometry_schema.circular_involute.base_radius,base_radius>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;

The **circular_involute** is parametrised as follows:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = C + r (cos(u) + u sin(u)) x + r (sin(u) - ucos(u)) y
++++

The parameter stem:[u] is measured in radians and the parametrisation range is stem:[-oo < u < oo]

The arc length stem:[s] of the curve, from the point stem:[u = 0], is given by the formula: stem:[s = 1 / 2 r u^2]

At any point on the involute the distance stem:["PC"] from a point stem:[p] on the curve with parameter stem:[u] to the centre point stem:[C] satisfies the equation:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
"PC"^{2} = r^{2}(1 + u^{2})
++++
*)


(*"geometry_schema.circular_involute.__note"
See GIBSON[9] for further properties of involute curves.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.circular_involute.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig10>> 10 for the interpretation of the attributes. This figure shows a portion of the curve for parameter values between -1.5 and +1.5.
*)



(*"geometry_schema.circular_involute.position"
the location and orientation of the **circular_involute** ;

<<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>> the centre stem:[C] of the base circle from which the involute is defined.

<<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_2d.p,p[1]>> or
<<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_2d.p,p[1]>> The direction from centre of the base circle to the cusp point stem:[bb(P_0)] of the **circular_involute**.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.circular_involute.base_radius"
the radius of the base circle, for which the **circular_involute** is the wrapping curve.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.circular_involute.base_radius.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig10]]
.Circular_involute
====
image::Geomfig10.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.bounded_curve"
A **bounded_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>> of finite arc length with identifiable end points.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bounded_curve.__note"
A **bounded_curve** is not included in the ONEOF list for curve and, as such, has an implicit and/or relationship with other subtypes of curve. The only complex instances intended to be created are <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_pcurve,bounded_pcurve>> and <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface_curve,bounded_surface_curve>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.bounded_curve.wr:IP1"
A bounded curve has finite arc length.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bounded_curve.wr:IP2"
A bounded curve has a start point and an end point.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.polyline"
A **polyline** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>> with stem:[(n-1)] linear segments, defined by a list of stem:[n] <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>>s,

stem:[bb(P_1, P_2, ... , P_n)].

The stem:[i"th"] segment of the curve is parametrised as follows:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = P_{i}(i-u) + P_{i+1}(u+1-i), "for" 1 le i le n-1
++++

where stem:[i-1 le u le i] and with parametric range of stem:[0 le u le n-1].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.polyline.points"
the <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>>s defining the **polyline** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve"
A **b_spline_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>>, it is a piecewise parametric polynomial or rational curve described in terms of control points and basis functions. The B-spline curve has been selected as the most stable format to represent all types of polynomial or rational parametric curves. With appropriate attribute values it is capable of representing single span or spline curves of explicit polynomial, rational, Bézier or B-spline type. The **b_spline_curve** has three special subtypes where the knots and knot multiplicities can be derived to provide simple default capabilities.

Interpretation of the data shall be as follows:

Polynomial case:

* The curve in the polynomial case is given by the following formula.
+
--
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = sum_(i=0)^k P_i N_i^d(u)
++++
--
//prior representation of polynomial case
//image::BScrv1.gif[]
//prior representation of rational case
//image::BScrv2.gif[]

. Rational case:

* The curve in the rational case is given by:

--
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = (sum_(i=0)^k(w_i P_i N_i^d(u))) / (sum_(i=0)^k(w_i N_i^d(u)))
++++

[%key]
stem:[k]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points,upper index on control points>>;
stem:[bb P_{i}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.control_points_list,control points>>;
stem:[w_{i}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve.weights_data,weights>>;
stem:[N_{i}]:: basis functions;
--


The knot array is an array of stem:[(k + d + 2)] real numbers stem:[u_{-d}, ldots , u_{k+1}], such that for all indices
stem:[j in [-d, k\]], stem:[u_j le u_{j+1}].

This array is obtained from the **knots** list by repeating each multiple knot according to the multiplicity. stem:[N_i^d], the stem:[i]-th normalised B-spline basis function of degree stem:[d], is defined on the subset
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
[u_{i-d}, ldots , u_{i+1}]
++++

of this array.

Let stem:[L] denote the number of distinct values amongst the stem:[d + k + 2] knots in the knot list; stem:[L] will be referred to as the 'upper index on knots'. Let stem:[m_j] denote the multiplicity (i.e., number of repetitions) of the stem:[j"th"] distinct knot. Then:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
Sigma^{L}_{i=1} m_{i} = d + k + 2.
++++

All knot multiplicities except the first and the last shall be in the range stem:[1, ..., d]; the first and last may have a maximum value of stem:[d + 1].

The **b_spline_curve** has three special subtypes where the knots and knot multiplicities are derived to provide simple default capabilities.

*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig11]]
.B_spline_curve
====
image::Geomfig11.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.__note"
Identification of B-spline curve default values and subtypes is important for performance considerations and for efficiency issues in performing computations.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.__note"
A B-spline is _rational_ if and only if the weights are not all identical; this can be represented by the *rational_b_spline_curve* subtype. If it is polynomial, the weights may be defaulted to all being 1.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.__note"
In the case where the B-spline curve is uniform, quasi-uniform or Bézier (including piecewise Bézier), the knots and knot multiplicities may be defaulted (i.e., non-existent in the data as specified by the attribute definitions).
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.__note"
When the knots are defaulted, a difference of 1.0 between separate knots is assumed, and the effective parameter range for the resulting curve starts from 0.0. These defaults are provided by the subtypes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.__note"
The knots and knot multiplicities shall not be defaulted in the non-uniform case.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.__note"
The defaulting of weights and knots are done independently of one another.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.__note"
Definitions of the B-spline basis functions  stem:[N_i^(d,)(u)] can be found in [5], [8], [11] and [12]. It should be noted that there is a difference in terminology between these references.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.__note"
In evaluating the basis functions, a knot stem:[u] of, for example, multiplicity _3_ is interpreted as a sequence _u, u, u,_ in the knot array.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig11>> for further information on curve definition.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.degree"
the algebraic degree of the basis functions;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.control_points_list"
the list of control points for the curve;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.curve_form"
used to identify particular types of curve; it is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.closed_curve"
indication of whether the curve is closed; it is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.closed_curve.__note"
Where part of the data is described as `for information only' this implies that if there is any discrepancy between this information and the properties derived from the curve itself, the curve data takes precedence.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.self_intersect"
flag to indicate whether the curve self-intersects or not; it is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points"
the upper index on the array of control points; the lower index is 0, this value is derived from the **control_points_list** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.control_points"
the array of control points used to define the geometry of the curve; this is derived from the list of control points.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.wr:WR1"
Any instantiation of this entity shall include one of the subtypes <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots,b_spline_curve_with_knots>><<express:geometry_schema.uniform_curve,uniform_curve>>, <<express:geometry_schema.quasi_uniform_curve,quasi_uniform_curve>> or <<express:geometry_schema.bezier_curve,bezier_curve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots"
A **b_spline_curve_with_knots** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve,b_spline_curve>>, for which the knot values are explicitly given. This subtype shall be used to represent non-uniform B-spline curves and may be used for other knot types.

Let stem:[L] denote the number of distinct values amongst the stem:[d + k + 2] knots in the knot list; stem:[L] will be referred to as the 'upper index on knots'. Let stem:[m_j] denote the multiplicity (i.e., number of repetitions) of the stem:[j"th"] distinct knot. Then:

--
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
Sigma_{i=1}^L m_i = d + k + 2
++++

[%key]
stem:[k]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points,upper index on control points>>;
stem:[d]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.degree,degree>>;
--

All knot multiplicities except the first and the last shall be in the range stem:[1, ..., d]; the first and last may have a maximum value of stem:[d + 1].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots.__note"
In evaluating the basis functions, a knot stem:[u] of, for example, multiplicity _3_ is interpreted as a sequence _u, u, u,_ in the knot array.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots.knot_multiplicities"
the multiplicities of the knots; this list defines the number of times each knot in the **knots** list is to be repeated in constructing the knot array;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots.knots"
the list of distinct knots used to define the B-spline basis functions;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots.knot_spec"
the description of the knot type; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots.knot_spec.__note"
Where part of the data is described as `for information only' this implies that if there is any discrepancy between this information and the properties derived from the curve itself, the curve data takes precedence.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.curve_form"
Used to identify particular types of curve; it is for information only.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.degree"
the algebraic degree of the basis functions;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.closed_curve"
Indication of whether the curve is closed; it is for information only.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.self_intersect"
Flag to indicate whether the curve self-intersects or not; it is for information only.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.dim"
the dimensionality of the coordinate space for the curve;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points"
The upper index on the array of control points; the lower index is 0. This value is derived from the list of control points.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.control_points"
The array of control points used to define the geometry of the curve. This is derived from the list of control points.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots.upper_index_on_knots"
the upper index on the knot arrays; the lower index is 1;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots.wr:WR1"
The function <<express:geometry_schema.constraints_param_b_spline,constraints_param_b_spline>> returns TRUE if no inconsistencies in the parametrisation of the B-spline are found.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots.wr:WR2"
The number of elements in the knot multiplicities list shall be equal to the number of elements in the knots list.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.uniform_curve"
A **uniform_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve,b_spline_curve>>, in which the knots are evenly spaced. Suitable default values for the knots and knot multiplicities are derived in this case. A B-spline is _uniform_ if and only if all knots are of multiplicity 1 and they differ by a positive constant from the preceding knot. In this subtype the knot spacing is 1.0, starting at _-d_, where stem:[d] is the degree.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.uniform_curve.__note"
If the B-spline curve is uniform and degree = 1, the B-spline is equivalent to a <<express:geometry_schema.polyline,polyline>>
*)

(*"geometry_schema.uniform_curve.__note"
The value k_up may be required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists. This is computed from the degree and the number of control points.

stem:[k_up] = <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points,upper_index_on_control_points>> + degree + 2.

If required, the knots and knot multiplicities can be computed by the functions:

<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>> or
<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.quasi_uniform_curve"
A **quasi_uniform_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve,b_spline_curve>>, in which the knots are evenly spaced, and except for the first and last, have multiplicity 1. Suitable default values for the knots and knot multiplicities are derived in this case. A B-spline is _quasi-uniform_ if and only if the knots are of multiplicity (degree+1) at the ends, of multiplicity 1 elsewhere, and they differ by a positive constant from the preceding knot. A quasi-uniform B-spline curve which has only two knots represents a Bézier curve. In this subtype the knot spacing is 1.0, starting at 0.0.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.quasi_uniform_curve.__note"
The value k_up may be required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists. This is computed from the degree and the number of control points.

stem:[k_up] = <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points,upper_index_on_control_points>> + degree + 2.

If required, the knots and knot multiplicities can be computed by the functions:

<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>> or
<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.bezier_curve"
A **bezier_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve,b_spline_curve>>, that represents in the most general case a piecewise Bézier curve. This is a special type of curve which can be represented as a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve,b_spline_curve>> in which the knots are evenly spaced and have high multiplicities. Suitable default values for the knots and knot multiplicities are derived in this case.

A B-spline curve is a piecewise Bézier curve if it is quasi-uniform except that the interior knots have multiplicity <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.degree,degree>> rather than having multiplicity one. In this subtype the knot spacing is 1.0, starting at 0.0. A piecewise Bézier curve which has only two knots, 0.0 and 1.0, each of multiplicity (degree+1), is a simple Bézier curve.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.bezier_curve.__example"
A one-segment cubic Bézier curve would have knot sequence (0,1) with multiplicity sequence (4,4).
*)
(*"geometry_schema.bezier_curve.__example"
A two-segment cubic piecewise Bézier curve would have knot sequence (0,1,2) with multiplicity sequence (4,3,4).
*)

(*"geometry_schema.bezier_curve.__note"
A simple Bézier curve can be defined as a B-spline curve with knots by the following data:



degree = (stem:[d])



upper index on control points (equal to stem:[d])



control points (_d + 1_ cartesian points)



knot type (equal to quasi-uniform knots)



knot multiplicities = (_d+1, d+1_)



knots = (0.0, 1.0)



No other data are needed, except for a rational Bézier curve. In this case the weights data ((_d + 1_) REALs) shall be given.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.bezier_curve.__note"
It should be noted that every piecewise Bézier curve has an equivalent representation as a B-spline curve. Because of problems with non-uniform knots not every B-spline curve can be represented as a piecewise Bézier curve., To define a piecewise Bézier curve as a B-spline:



The first knot is 0.0 with multiplicity (_d + 1_).



The next knot is 1.0 with multiplicity stem:[d]



(the knots for one segment are now defined, unless it is the last one).



The next knot is 2.0 with multiplicity stem:[d] (the knots for two segments are now defined, unless the second is the last one).



Continue to the end of the last segment, call it the stem:[n]-th segment, at the end of which a knot with value stem:[n], multiplicity (_d + 1_) is added.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.bezier_curve.__note"
For the piecewise Bézier case, if stem:[d] is the degree, _k+1_ is the number of control points, stem:[m] is the number of knots with multiplicity stem:[d], and stem:[N] is the total number of knots for the spline, then



stem:[(d + 2 + k) = N = (d + 1) + md + (d + 1)]


thus, stem:[m = (k - d)//d]



Thus, the knot sequence is stem:[(0, 1, ..., m, (m+1))] with multiplicities stem:[(d+1, d,. . ., d, d+1)].
*)

(*"geometry_schema.bezier_curve.__note"
The value k_up may be required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists. This is computed from the degree and the number of control points.



k_up = (SELF\backslash b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points)/(SELF\backslash b_spline_curve.degree + 1)



If required, the knots and knot multiplicities can be computed by the function calls:



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>(SELF\b_spline_curve.degree, k_up, piecewise_bezier_knots),



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>(SELF\b_spline_curve.degree,k_up, piecewise_bezier_knots).
*)

(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve"
A **rational_b_spline_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve,b_spline_curve>>, that is a piecewise parametric rational curve described in terms of control points and basis functions. This subtype is instantiated with one of the other subtypes of **b_spline_curve** which explicitly or implicitly provide the knot values used to define the basis functions.

All weights shall be positive and the curve is given by:
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = (sum_(i=0)^k(w_i P_i N_i^d(u))) / (sum_(i=0)^k(w_i N_i^d(u)))
++++

//prior representation
//+
//--
//image::BScrv2.gif[]

[%key]
stem:[k]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points,upper index on control points>>;
stem:[bb P_{i}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.control_points_list,control points>>;
stem:[w_{i}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve.weights_data,weights>>;
stem:[d]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.degree,degree>>.
stem:[N_{i}]:: basis functions;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve.weights_data"
the supplied values of the weights; see the derived attribute **weights** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve\b_spline_curve.degree"
the algebraic degree of the basis functions;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve\b_spline_curve.curve_form"
Used to identify particular types of curve; it is for information only.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve\b_spline_curve.closed_curve"
Indication of whether the curve is closed; it is for information only.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve\b_spline_curve.self_intersect"
Flag to indicate whether the curve self-intersects or not; it is for information only.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve\b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points"
the upper index on the array of control points; the lower index is 0; this value is derived from the list of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve\b_spline_curve.control_points"
the array of control points used to define the geometry of the curve.This is derived from the list of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve.weights"
the array of weights associated with the control points. This is derived from the **weights_data** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve.wr:WR1"
There shall be the same number of weights as control points.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve.wr:WR2"
All the weights shall have values greater than 0.0.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.local_b_spline"
A **local_b_spline** is a type of <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>> that is polynomial B-spline of degree d in one parameter defined over a list of knot values with associated multiplicities. The knot list is a list of distinct strictly increasing real values, the list of multiplicities defines the multiplicity of each knot. To ensure that all related **local_b_spline** functions refer to the same knot list the explicit knot list is defined with the entities, such as <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface,locally_refined_spline_surface>> , which use **local_b_spline** functions. Let stem:[m_j] denote the multiplicity (i.e. the number of repetitions) of the distinct knot number stem:[j] , and stem:[L] denote the size of the knot list. Then

.local_b_spline equation
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sum_{i=1}^{L} m_i = d + k + 2
++++

[%key]
stem:[k]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points,upper index on control points>>;
stem:[d]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.degree,degree>>;

All knot multiplicities except the first and the last shall be in the range stem:[1,..., d]; the first and last may have a maximum value of stem:[d+1]. The continuity of local B-spline at knot stem:[j] in the current parameter direction is stem:[d"-"m _j].
*)

(*"geometry_schema.local_b_spline.degree"
the polynomial degree of the **local_b_spline** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.local_b_spline.knots"
the list of pointers to the distinct knot values in increasing order;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.local_b_spline.multiplicities"
the list of positive integers defining the multiplicities of the knots.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.local_b_spline.wr:WR1"
The degree shall be positive;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.local_b_spline.wr:WR2"
The number of knots shall be equal to the number of multiplicities;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.local_b_spline.wr:WR3"
The values of the knots and multiplicities shall be compatible with the degree and the continuity requirements for a B-spline function. The function <<express:geometry_schema.constraints_param_local_b_spline,constraints_param_local_b_spline>> applies the following tests:

the sum of knot multiplicities = degree + 2,

for the first and last knot the multiplicity is bounded by 1 and (degree+1),

for all other knots the knot multiplicity is bounded by 1 and degree.

the consecutive knots are increasing in value.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve"
A **locally_refined_spline_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>> that is a piecewise parametric polynomial or rational curve described in terms of control points and local B-spline functions. If the set of B-spline functions are linearly independent, they will form a basis. With appropriate attribute values, the curve is capable of representing single span or spline curves of explicit polynomial, rational, Bézier or B-spline type. Furthermore, the degree of the curve can vary as the B-splines from which it is defined, need not to have the same polynomial degree.

Interpretation of the data is as follows:

--
[%key]
stem:[K]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.upper_index_on_control_points,upper index on control points>>,
stem:[d_i]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.degree,degree>> of b-spline i,
stem:[bb P_{i}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.control_points_list,control points>>,
stem:[s_{i}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.scaling_factors,scaling factors>>,
stem:[N_{i}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.b_splines,locally defined b-splines>>,
stem:[w_{i}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_curve.weights_data,weights>>.
--

The curve, in the polynomial case, is given by:
--
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u) = sum_(i=1)^K ( P_i s_i N_i^(d_i)(u) )
++++
--

//prior representation:image::LRcurve.gif[]

In the rational case all weights shall be positive and the curve is given by:
--
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u) = (
  sum_(i=1)^K (w_i P_i s_i N_i^(d_i)(u))
) / (
  sum_(i=1)^K (w_i s_i N_i^(d_i)(u))
)
++++
--

//prior representation:image::RationalLRcurve.gif[]

The knot values are defined in the <<express:geometry_schema.spline_knot_values,spline_knot_values>> type, and the knot multiplicity is defined in the entity <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.__note"
A locally refined spline curve is rational if and only if the weights are not all identical; this can be represented by the <<express:geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_curve,rational_locally_refined_spline_curve>> subtype. If it is polynomial, the weights may be defaulted to all being 1.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.__note"
The degree is associated with the local B-spline and does not need to be constant throughout the curve.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.__note"
When the polynomial degree is the same for all B-splines and no knot lines exist that do not cross the entire parameter domain, the *locally_refined_spline_curve* will coincide with <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots,b_spline_curve_with_knots>>. In that case <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve_with_knots,b_spline_curve_with_knots>> has the simplest and most compact representation.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.__note"
A *locally_refined_spline_curve* can be of type analysis suitable T-spline, hierarchical B-spline, LR-spline, semi standard T-spline or standard T-spline. The type is given by the enumeration <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum,locally_refined_spline_type_enum>>, and the packing and unpacking of the curve will depend on this type.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.b_splines"
the local B-spline functions from which the curve is defined. If they are linearly independent, the set of B-splines will form a basis. The **b_splines** and consequently also the **control_points_list** and **scaling_factors**, are ordered according to the first knot in the local B-spline function. In case of ambiguities, the sorting factors are the last knot in the B-spline function and finally the multiplicity of the first knot.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.knot_values"
the list of distinct knot values;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.closed_curve"
indication whether the curve is closed; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.control_points_list"
the list of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.scaling_factors"
the list of scaling factors, these factors are used to ensure that the non-zero B-splines at all points in the domain add up to unity (partition of unity). LR B-splines, hierarchical B-splines and some variants of T-splines ensure partition of unity by scaling. Other variants of T-splines use rational scaling.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.curve_form"
indication of special curve types;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.locally_refined_spline_type"
the type of the initial locally refined spline curve that provides rules for how the stored curve can be unpacked to its original format;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.self_intersect"
indication whether the curve is self-intersecting; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.domain"
the parameter domain corresponding to the curve. domain(1) is the minimum parameter value , domain(2) is the maximum parameter value. If the B-splines at the ends have a knot multiplicity equal to the degree+1, the domain will coincide with the domain given by the minimum and maximum values in <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.knot_values,knot_values>>. Otherwise, it will be coincide with a domain given by nearby knots depending on knot multiplicities. Otherwise, it will be coincide with a domain given by nearby knots depending upon knot multiplicities.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.wr:WR1"
the size of the <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.b_splines,b_splines>> list shall be equal to the number of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.wr:WR2"
the number of <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.scaling_factors,scaling_factors>> shall be equal to the number of control points.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.wr:WR3"
all **scaling_factors** shall be positive and not greater than 1.0.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_curve"
A **rational_locally_refined_spline_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve,locally_refined_spline_curve>> that is a piecewise parametric rational curve described in terms of control points and local B-spline functions.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_curve.weights_data"
the supplied values of the weights.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_curve.wr:WR1"
the size of the of **weights_data** list shall be equal to the number of control points
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_curve.wr:WR2"
all values in the **weights_data** list shall be positive.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve"
A **trimmed_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>>, which is created by taking a selected portion, between two identified points, of the associated basis curve. The basis curve itself is unaltered and more than one trimmed curve may reference the same basis curve. Trimming points for the curve may be identified:

by parametric value;

by geometric position;

by both of the above.

At least one of these shall be specified at each end of the curve. The **sense** makes it possible to unambiguously define any segment of a closed curve such as a circle. The combinations of sense and ordered end points make it possible to define four distinct directed segments connecting two different points on a circle or other closed curve. For this purpose cyclic properties of the parameter range are assumed; for example, 370 degrees is equivalent to 10 degrees. The trimmed curve has a parametrisation which is inherited from that of the particular basis curve referenced. More precisely the parameter stem:[s] of the trimmed curve is derived from the parameter stem:[t] of the basis curve as follows:

If sense is TRUE: stem:[s = t - t_1].

If sense is FALSE: stem:[s = t_1 - t].

In the above equations stem:[t_1] is the value given by **trim_1** or the parameter value corresponding to **point_1** and stem:[t_2] is the parameter value given by **trim_2** or the parameter corresponding to **point_2**. The resultant trimmed curve has a parameter stem:[s] ranging from 0 at the first trimming point to stem:[|t_2 - t_1|] at the second trimming point.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.__note"
In the case of a closed basis curve, it may be necessary to increment stem:[1] or stem:[2] by the parametric length for consistency with the sense flag.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.__note"
For example:

. If *sense_agreement* = TRUE and stem:[t_2 < t_1, t_2] should be increased by the parametric length.

. If *sense_agreement* = FALSE and stem:[t_1 > t_2], stem:[t_1] should be increased by the parametric length.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.basis_curve"
the <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>> to be trimmed. For curves with multiple representations any parameter values given as **trim_1** or **trim_2** refer to the master representation of the **basis_curve** only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.trim_1"
the first trimming point which may be specified as a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> (point_1), as a real parameter value (stem:["parameter"_1 = t_1]), or both;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.trim_2"
the second trimming point which may be specified as a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> (point_2), as a real parameter value (stem:["parameter"_2 = t_2]), or both;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.sense_agreement"
flag to indicate whether the direction of the trimmed curve agrees with or is opposed to the direction of **basis_curve**.

**sense_ agreement** = TRUE if the curve is being traversed in the direction of increasing parametric value;

**sense_agreement** = FALSE otherwise.

For an open curve, `sense_agreement = FALSE` if stem:[t_1 > t_2].

If stem:[t_2 > t_1], `sense_agreement = TRUE`.

The sense information is redundant in this case but is essential for a closed curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.master_representation"
where both parameter and point are present at either end of the curve this indicates the preferred form. Multiple representations provide the ability to communicate data in more than one form, even though the data are expected to be geometrically identical.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.master_representation.__note"
The *master_representation* attribute acknowledges the impracticality of ensuring that multiple forms are indeed identical and allows the indication of a preferred form. This would probably be determined by the creator of the data. All characteristics, such as parametrisation, domain, and results of evaluation, for an entity having multiple representations, are derived from the master representation. Any use of the other representations is a compromise for practical considerations.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.wr:WR1"
Either a single value is specified for **trim_1**, or, the two trimming values are of different types (point and parameter).
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.wr:WR2"
Either a single value is specified for **trim_2**, or, the two trimming values are of different types (point and parameter).
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.wr:IP1"
Where both the parameter value and the cartesian point exist for **trim_1** or **trim_2** they shall be consistent, i.e., the **basis_curve** evaluated at the parameter value shall coincide with the specified point.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.wr:IP2"
When a cartesian point is specified by **trim_1** or by **trim_2**, it shall lie on the **basis_curve**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.wr:IP3"
Except in the case of a closed **basis_curve**, where both parameter_1 and parameter_2 exist,
they shall be consistent with the sense flag, i.e., stem:["sense" = "parameter"_1 < "parameter"_2].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.wr:IP4"
If both parameter_1 and parameter_2 exist, stem:["parameter"_1 ne "parameter"_2].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.trimmed_curve.wr:IP5"
When a parameter value is specified by **trim_1** or **trim_2**, it shall lie within the parametric range of the **basis_curve**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve"
A **composite_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>>, that is a collection of curves joined end-to-end. The individual segments of the curve are themselves defined as <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment,composite_curve_segment>>s. The parametrisation of the composite curve is an accumulation of the parametric ranges of the referenced bounded curves. The first segment is parametrised from 0 to stem:[l_1], and, for stem:[i ge 2], the stem:[i] th segment is parametrised from

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sum_(k=1)^{i-1}l_k "to" sum_(k=1)^{i}l_k
++++

//prior representation::image::CompC1.gif[]

where stem:[l_k] is the parametric length (i.e., difference between maximum and minimum parameter values) of the curve underlying the stem:[k] th segment.

Let stem:[T] denote the parameter for the **composite_curve**.

Then, if the stem:[i"th"] segment is not a **reparametrised_composite_curve_segment**, stem:[T] is related to the parameter stem:[t_1, t_{i0}, le t_1, le t_{i1}] for the stem:[i"th"] segment by the equation:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
T = sum_(k=1)^{i-1} l_k + t_i - t_(i0)
++++

//prior representation::image::CompC2a.gif[]

if **segments[i].same_sense** = TRUE;

or by the equation:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
T = sum_(k=1)^{i-1}l_k + t_(i1) - t_i
++++

//prior representation::image::CompC2b.gif[]

if **segments[i].same_sense** = FALSE.

If **segments[i]** is of type **reparametrised_composite_curve_segment**,

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
T = Sigma_{k=1}^{i-1} l_{k} + tau
++++

Where stem:[tau] is defined in <<express:geometry_schema.reparametrised_composite_curve_segment,reparametrised_composite_curve_segment>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig12]]
.Composite_curve
====
image::Geomfig12.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig13>> for further information on curve definition and attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve.n_segments"
the number of component curves;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve.segments"
the component bounded curves, their transitions and senses. The transition attribute for the last segment defines the transition between the end of the last segment and the start of the first; this transition attribute may take the value **discontinuous**, which indicates an open curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve.self_intersect"
indication of whether the curve intersects itself or not; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve\geometric_item.dim"
the dimensionality of the coordinate space for the composite curve. This is an inherited attribute from the geometric representation item supertype.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve.closed_curve"
indication of whether the curve is closed or not; this is derived from the transition code on the last segment;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve.wr:WR1"
No transition code shall be discontinuous, except for the last code of an open curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment"
A **composite_curve_segment** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve,composite_curve>>, and is a bounded curve together with transition information which is used to construct a <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve,composite_curve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment.transition"
the state of transition (i.e., geometric continuity from the last point of this segment to the first point of the next segment) in a composite curve;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment.same_sense"
an indicator of whether or not the sense of the segment agrees with, or opposes, that of the parent curve. If **same_sense** is false, the point with highest parameter value is taken as the first point of the segment;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment.parent_curve"
the bounded curve which defines the geometry of the segment;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment.parent_curve.__note"
Since *composite_curve_segment* is not a subtype of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> the instance of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>> used as *parent_curve* is not automatically associated with the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> of the <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> using a <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve,composite_curve>> containing this *composite_curve_segment*. It is therefore necessary to ensure that the <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>> instance is explicitly included in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with the appropriate <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment.using_curves"
the set of <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve,composite_curve>>s which use this **composite_curve_segment** as a segment. This set shall not be empty. item supertype.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment.wr:WR1"
The **parent_curve** shall be a <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.reparametrised_composite_curve_segment"
A **reparametrised_composite_curve_segment** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment,composite_curve_segment>>, which provides the capability to re-define its parametric length without changing its geometry.

Let
[%key]
stem:[l]:: <<express:geometry_schema.reparametrised_composite_curve_segment.param_length>>.
stem:[SS]:: <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment.same_sense>>.

If stem:[t_0 le t le t_1] is the parameter range of <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment.parent_curve,parent_curve>>, the new parameter stem:[tau] for the **reparametrised_composite_curve_segment** is given by the equation when :

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
tau = cases(
  ( (t - t_0) l / (t_1 - t_0), " if SS = TRUE" ),
  ( (t_1 - t) l / (t_1 - t_0), " if SS = FALSE" )
)
++++
*)


(*"geometry_schema.reparametrised_composite_curve_segment.param_length"
the new parametric length of the segment. The segment is given a simple linear reparametrisation from 0.0 at the first point to **param_length** at the last point. The parametrisation of the composite curve constructed using this segment is defined in terms of **param_length**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.reparametrised_composite_curve_segment.wr:WR1"
The **param_length** shall be greater than zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pcurve"
A **pcurve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>>, that is a 3D curve defined by means of a 2D curve in the parameter space of a surface. If the curve is parametrised by the function stem:[(u,v) = f(t)], and the surface is parametrised by the function stem:[(x,y,z) = g(u,v)], the **pcurve** is parametrised by the function stem:[(x,y,z) = g(f(t))].

A **pcurve** definition contains a reference to its **basis_surface** and an indirect reference to a 2D curve through a <<express:representation_schema.definitional_representation,definitional_representation>> entity. The 2D curve, being in parameter space, is not in the context of the basis surface. Thus a direct reference is not possible. For the 2D curve the variables involved are stem:[u] and stem:[v], which occur in the parametric representation of the **basis_surface** rather than _x,y_ Cartesian coordinates. The curve is only defined within the parametric range of the surface.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pcurve.basis_surface"
the surface in whose parameter space the curve is defined;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pcurve.reference_to_curve"
the reference to the parameter space curve which defines the **pcurve** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pcurve.wr:WR1"
The set of items in the <<express:representation_schema.definitional_representation,definitional_representation>> entity corresponding to the **reference_to_curve** shall have exactly one element.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pcurve.wr:WR2"
The unique item in the set shall be a curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pcurve.wr:WR3"
The dimensionality of this parameter space curve shall be 2.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bounded_pcurve"
A **bounded_pcurve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>, which also has the properties of a <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bounded_pcurve.wr:WR1"
The referenced curve of the <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>> supertype shall be of type <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>>. This ensures that the **bounded_pcurve** is of finite arc length.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve"
A **surface_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>>, that is a curve on a surface. The curve is represented as a curve ({blank} **curve_3d** {blank}) in three-dimensional space and possibly as a curve, corresponding to a <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>, in the two-dimensional parametric space of a surface. The ability of this curve to reference a list of 1 or 2 <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve_or_surface,pcurve_or_surface>>s enables this entity to define either a curve on a single surface, or an intersection curve which has two distinct surface associations. A `seam' on a closed surface can also be represented by this entity; in this case each **associated_geometry** will be a pcurve lying on the same surface. Each <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>, if it exists, shall be parametrised to have the same sense as **curve_3d**. The **surface_curve** takes its parametrisation directly from either **curve_3d** or <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>> as indicated by the attribute master representation.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.__note"
Because of the ANDOR relationship with the *bounded_surface_curve* subtype an instance of a *surface_curve* may be any one of the following:



*  a *surface_curve;*
*  a <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface_curve,bounded_surface_curve>>;
*  an <<express:geometry_schema.intersection_curve,intersection_curve>>;
*  an <<express:geometry_schema.intersection_curve,intersection_curve>> AND <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface_curve,bounded_surface_curve>>;
*  a <<express:geometry_schema.seam_curve,seam_curve>>;
*  a <<express:geometry_schema.seam_curve,seam_curve>> AND <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface_curve,bounded_surface_curve>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.curve_3d"
the curve which is the three-dimensional representation of the **surface_curve** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.associated_geometry"
a list of one or two pcurves or surfaces which define the surface or surfaces associated with the surface curve. Two elements in this list indicate that the curve has two surface associations which need not be two distinct surfaces. When a pcurve is selected, it identifies a surface and also associates a basis curve in the parameter space of this surface;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.master_representation"
the **master_representation** defines the curve used to determine the unique parametrisation of the **surface_curve**.

The **master_representation** takes one of the values **curve_3d**, **pcurve_s1** or **pcurve_s2** to indicate a preference for the 3D curve, or the first or second pcurve, in the associated geometry list, respectively. Multiple representations provide the ability to communicate data in more than one form, even though the data is expected to be geometrically identical.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.master_representation.__note"
The *master_representation* attribute acknowledges the impracticality of ensuring that multiple forms are indeed identical and allows the indication of a preferred form. This would probably be determined by the creator of the data. All characteristics, such as parametrisation, domain, and results of evaluation, for an entity having multiple representations, are derived from the master representation. Any use of the other representations is a compromise for practical considerations.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.basis_surface"
the surface, or surfaces on which the **surface_curve** lies. This is determined from the **associated_geometry** list.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.wr:WR1"
The currve **curve_3d** shall be defined in three-dimensional space.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.wr:WR2"
The value **pcurve_s1** shall only be nominated as the master representation if the first element of the associated geometry list is a <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.wr:WR3"
The value **pcurve_s2** shall only be nominated as the master representation if the second element of the associated geometry list is a pcurve. This also requires that **pcurve_s2** shall not be nominated when the associated geometry list contains a single element.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.wr:WR4"
The curve **curve_3d** shall not be a <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.wr:IP1"
Where **curve_3d** and one or more <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>s exist they shall represent the same mathematical point set. (i.e., They shall coincide geometrically but may differ in parametrisation.)
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve.wr:IP2"
**curve_3d** and any associated <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>s shall agree with respect to their senses.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.intersection_curve"
A **intersection_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve,surface_curve>>, which results from the intersection of two surfaces. It is represented as a special subtype of the <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve,surface_curve>> entity having two distinct surface associations defined via the associated geometry list.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.intersection_curve.wr:WR1"
The intersection curve shall have precisely two associated geometry elements.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.intersection_curve.wr:WR2"
The two associated geometry elements shall be related to distinct surfaces. These are the surfaces which define the intersection curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.seam_curve"
A **seam_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve,surface_curve>>, that is a curve on a closed parametric surface which has two distinct representations as constant parameter curves at the two extremes of the parameter range for the surface.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.seam_curve.__example"
The `seam' on a cylinder has representations as the lines stem:[u = 0] or  stem:[u = 360] degrees in parameter space.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.seam_curve.wr:WR1"
The seam curve shall have precisely two <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve.associated_geometry,associated_geometry>>s.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.seam_curve.wr:WR2"
The two <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve.associated_geometry,associated_geometry>>s shall be related to the same surface.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.seam_curve.wr:WR3"
The first <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve.associated_geometry,associated_geometry>> shall be a <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.seam_curve.wr:WR4"
The second <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve.associated_geometry,associated_geometry>> shall be a <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bounded_surface_curve"
A **bounded_surface_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve,surface_curve>>, which also has the properties of a <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bounded_surface_curve.wr:WR1"
The <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve.curve_3d,curve_3d>> attribute of the <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve,surface_curve>> supertype shall be a <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_curve,bounded_curve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface"
A **composite_curve_on_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve,composite_curve>>, that is a collection of segments which are curves on a surface. Each segment shall lie on the basis surface, and shall reference a <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface_curve,bounded_surface_curve>>, or a <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_pcurve,bounded_pcurve>>, or a <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface,composite_curve_on_surface>>.

There shall be at least positional continuity between adjacent segments. The parametrisation of the composite curve is obtained from the accumulation of the parametric ranges of the segments. The first segment is parametrised from 0 to stem:[l_1], and, for stem:[i ge 2], the stem:[i"th"] segment is parametrised from

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sum_(k=1)^(i-1) l_k " to " sum_(k=1)^(i) l_k ,
++++

where stem:[l_k] is the parametric length (i.e., difference between maximum and minimum parameter values) of the stem:[k^{th}] curve segment.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface.__note"
A <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface,composite_curve_on_surface>> can be included as the <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment.parent_curve,parent_curve>> attribute of a <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment,composite_curve_segment>> since it is a bounded curve subtype.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface.basis_surface"
the surface on which the composite curve is defined.;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface\composite_curve.n_segments"
The component bounded curves, their transitions and senses. The transition for the last segment defines the transition between the end of the last segment and the start of the first; this element may take the value **discontinuous**, which indicates an open curve. For each segment the **parent_curve** shall be either a **bounded_pcurve**, a **bounded_surface_curve**, or a **composite_curve_on_surface**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface\composite_curve.self_intersec"
Indication of whether the curve intersects itself or not.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface\composite_curve.dim"
The dimensionality of the coordinate space for the composite curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface\composite_curve.closed_curve"
Indication of whether the curve is closed or not.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface.wr:WR1"
The **basis_surface** SET shall contain at least one surface. This ensures that all segments reference curves on the same surface.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface.wr:WR2"
Each segment shall reference a <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve,pcurve>>, or a <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve,surface_curve>>, or a <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface,composite_curve_on_surface>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface.wr:IP1"
Each <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_segment.parent_curve,parent_curve>> referenced by a **composite_curve_on_surface** segment shall be a curve on surface and a bounded curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_2d"
A **offset_curve_2d** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>>, at a constant distance from a basis curve in two-dimensional space. This entity defines a simple plane-offset curve by offsetting by **distance** along the normal to **basis_curve** in the plane of **basis_curve**.

The underlying curve shall have a well-defined tangent direction at every point. In the case of a composite curve, the transition code between each segment shall be **cont_same_gradient** or **cont_same_gradient_same_curvature**.

The **offset_curve_2d** takes its parametrisation from the **basis_curve**. The **offset_curve_2d** is parametrised as

--
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = C(u) + d cdot O(t)
++++

[%key]
stem:[bb C(u)]:: <<express:geometry_schema.offset_curve_2d.basis_curve,basis curve>> at location stem:[u];
stem:[bb t]:: Unit-vector tangent to the basis curve at parameter u;
stem:[bb O(bb t)]:: <<express:geometry_schema.orthogonal_complement,orthogonal complement>> of stem:[bb t];
stem:[d]:: <<express:geometry_schema.offset_curve_2d.distance,distance>>;
--


The underlying curve shall be two-dimensional.,
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_2d.__note"
The *offset_curve_2d* may differ in nature from the *basis_curve*; the offset of a non self-intersecting curve can be self-intersecting. Care should be taken to ensure that the offset to a continuous curve does not become discontinuous.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_2d.basis_curve"
the curve that is being offset;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_2d.distance"
the distance of the offset curve from the basis curve. **distance** may be positive, negative or zero. A positive value of **distance** defines an offset in the direction which is normal to the curve in the sense of an anti-clockwise rotation through 90 degrees from the tangent vector **T** at the given point. (This is in the direction of <<express:geometry_schema.orthogonal_complement,orthogonal_complement>>(t).)
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_2d.self_intersect"
an indication of whether the offset curve self-intersects; this is for information only.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_2d.wr:WR1"
The The underlying **basis_curve** shall be defined in two-dimensional space..
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d"
An **offset_curve_3d** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>>, at a constant distance from a basis curve in three-dimensional space. The underlying curve shall have a well-defined tangent direction at every point. In the case of a composite curve the transition code between each segment shall be **cont_same_gradient** or **cont_same_gradient_same_curvature**. The offset curve at any point (parameter) on the basis curve is in the direction stem:[langle v xx t rangle ] where stem:[v] is the fixed reference direction and stem:[t] is the unit tangent to the **basis_curve**. For the offset direction to be well defined, stem:[t] shall not at any point of the curve be in the same, or opposite, direction as stem:[v].

The **offset_curve_3d** takes its parametrisation from the **basis_curve**. The **offset_curve_3d** is parametrised as

--
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
lambda(u) = C(u) + d <<v xx t>>,
++++

[%key]
stem:[C(u)]:: <<express:geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d.basis_curve,basis curve at location u>>;
stem:[bb t]:: Unit-vector tangent to the basis curve at parameter u.
stem:[d]:: <<express:geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d.distance,distance>>;
stem:[v]:: <<express:geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d.ref_direction,reference distance>>;
-- *)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d.__note"
The *offset_curve_3d* may differ in nature from the *basis_curve*; the offset of a non-self-intersecting curve can be self-intersecting. Care should be taken to ensure that the offset to a continuous curve does not become discontinuous.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d.basis_curve"
the curve that is being offset;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d.distance"
the distance of the offset curve from the basis curve; **distance** may be positive, negative or zero;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d.self_intersect"
an indication of whether the offset curve self-intersects; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d.ref_direction"
the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> used to define the direction of the **offset_curve_3d** from the **basis_curve**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d.wr:WR1"
Both the underlying curve and the reference direction shall be in three-dimensional space.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_curve_3d.wr:IP1"
At no point on the curve shall **ref_direction** be parallel, or opposite to, the direction of the tangent vector.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_replica"
A **curve_replica** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>>, and is a replica of a curve in a different location. It is defined by referencing the parent curve and a transformation. The geometric form of the curve produced will be the same as the parent curve, but, where the transformation includes scaling, the dimensions will differ. The curve replica takes its parametric range and parametrisation directly from the parent curve. Where the parent curve is a curve on surface, the replica will not in general share the property of lying on the surface.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_replica.parent_curve"
the curve that is being copied;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_replica.transformation"
the <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator,cartesian_transformation_operator>> which defines the location of the curve replica. This transformation may include scaling.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_replica.wr:WR1"
The coordinate space dimensionality of the **transformation** attribute shall be the same as that of the **parent_curve**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_replica.wr:WR2"
A **curve_replica** shall not participate in its own definition.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface"
A **surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>>, that can be envisioned as a set of connected points in 3-dimensional space which is always locally 2-dimensional, but need not be a manifold. A surface shall not be a single point or in part, or entirely, a curve.

Each surface has a parametric representation of the form
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v),
++++

where stem:[u] and stem:[v] are independent dimensionless parameters. The unit normal **N**, at any point on the surface, is given by the equation

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N(u,v) = (del sigma)/(del u) xx (del sigma)/(del u);
++++
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface.wr:IP1"
A **surface** has non-zero area.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface.wr:IP2"
A **surface** is arcwise connected.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.elementary_surface"
A **elementary_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>>, that is a simple analytic surface with defined parametric representation.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.elementary_surface.position"
the location and orientation of the surface,this attribute is used in the definition of the parametrisation of the surface.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.plane"
A **plane** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.elementary_surface,elementary_surface>>, that is an unbounded surface with a constant normal. A **plane** is defined by a point on the plane and the normal direction to the plane.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>> ("normal to plane");

and the surface is parametrised as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = C + ux + vy
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[-oo < u, v < oo].

In the above parametrisation, the length unit for the unit vectors **x** and **y** is derived from the context of the **plane**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.plane\elementary_surface.position"
The location and orientation of the surface. This attribute is inherited from the **elementary_surface** supertype.

**position.location** A point in the plane.

**position.p[3]** This direction, which is equal to **position.axis**, defines the normal to the plane.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_surface"
A **cylindrical_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.elementary_surface,elementary_surface>>, that is a surface at a constant distance (the **radius** {blank})from a straight line. A **cylindrical_surface** is defined by its radius and its orientation and location.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[R]:: <<express:geometry_schema.conical_surface.radius,radius>>;

and the surface is parametrised as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = C + R((cos u)x + (sin u)y) + vz
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 360] degrees and stem:[-oo < v < oo]. In the above parametrisation the length unit for the unit vector stem:[bb z] is equal to that of the **radius**. stem:[u] is an angular parameter and when a numerical value is specified it shall use the current units for <<express:measure_schema.plane_angle_measure,plane_angle_measure>>.

In the placement coordinate system defined above, the surface is represented by the equation stem:[S = 0], where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
S(x, y, z) = x^{2} + y^{2} - R^{2}
++++

The positive direction of the normal to the surface at any point on the surface is given by:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
( S_{x}, S_{y}, S_{z} ).
++++

The unit normal is given by

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N(u,v) = (cos(u))x + (sin (u))y.
++++

The sense of this normal is away from the axis of the cylinder.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_surface\elementary_surface.position"
The location and orientation of the surface. This attribute is inherited from the **elementary_surface** supertype.

**position.location** A point on the axis of the cylinder.

**position.p[3]** The direction of the axis of the cylinder.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_surface.radius"
the radius of the cylinder.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.conical_surface"
A **conical_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.elementary_surface,elementary_surface>>, which could be produced by revolving a line in 3-dimensional space about any intersecting line.
The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>> ("axis of conical_surface");
stem:[R]:: <<express:geometry_schema.conical_surface.radius,radius>>;
stem:[alpha]:: <<express:geometry_schema.conical_surface.semi_angle,semi_angle>>;

and the surface is parametrised as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = C + (R + v tan(alpha))((cos u)x + (sin u)y) + v * z
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 360] degrees and stem:[-oo < v < oo]. In the above parametrisation the length unit for the unit vector stem:[bb z] is equal to that of the **radius**.

stem:[u] is an angular parameter and when a numerical value is specified it shall use the current units for <<express:measure_schema.plane_angle_measure,plane_angle_measure>>. In the placement coordinate system defined above, the surface is represented by the equation stem:[S = 0], where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
S(x, y, z) = x^{2} + y^{2} - (R + z * tan(alpha))^{2}
++++

The positive direction of the normal to the surface at any point on the surface is given by

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
( S_{x}, S_{y}, S_{z} ).
++++

The unit normal is given by

//N(u,v) = ((cos u)x + (sin u)y - (tan(alpha))z)/( (√(1 + (tan(alpha))^{2})), if R + v tan(alpha) > 0.0
//rewritten for clarity
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N(u,v) = ((cos u) x + (sin u) y - (tan(alpha)) z) / sqrt(1 + (tan(alpha))^2),
where (R + v tan(alpha)) > 0
++++

//rewritten for clarity
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N(u,v) = - ((cos u)x + (sin u)y - (tan(alpha))z)/ (sqrt(1 + (tan(alpha))^{2})),
if (R + v tan(alpha)) < 0.0.
++++

The sense of the normal is away from the axis of the cone. If the radius is zero, the cone apex is at the point stem:[(0, 0, 0)] in the placement coordinate system (i.e., at **SELF\elementary_surface.position.location** {blank}).
*)

(*"geometry_schema.conical_surface.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig13]]
.Conical_surface
====
image::Geomfig13.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.conical_surface.__note"
This form of representation is designed to provide the greatest geometric precision for those parts of the surface which are close to the location point *C*. For this reason the apex should only be selected as location point if the region of the surface close to the apex is of interest.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.conical_surface.__note"
The normal to the surface is undefined at the point where  stem:[R + v tan(alpha) = 0.0].
*)

(*"geometry_schema.conical_surface\elementary_surface.position"
//tttt this is wrong. we don't reference supertype attributes like this.
The location and orientation of the surface. This attribute is inherited from the **elementary_surface** supertype.

**position.location** A point on the axis of the cone.

**position.p[3]** The direction of the axis of the cone.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.conical_surface.radius"
the radius of the circular curve of intersection between the cone and a plane perpendicular to the axis of the cone passing through the location point (i.e., **SELF\elementary_surface.position.location** {blank});
*)


(*"geometry_schema.conical_surface.semi_angle"
the cone semi-angle.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.conical_surface.semi_angle.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig13>> 14 for illustration of the attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.conical_surface.wr:WR1"
The radius shall not be negative.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.conical_surfacee.wr:WR2"
The semi-angle shall be between 0 and 90 degrees.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_surface"
A **spherical_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.elementary_surface,elementary_surface>>, which is at a constant distance (the **radius** {blank}) from a central point. A **spherical_surface** is defined by the radius and the location and orientation of the surface.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:


[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[R]:: <<express:geometry_schema.spherical_surface.radius,radius>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;

and the surface is parametrised as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = C + R cos v ((cos u) x + (sin u) y) + R(sin v) z
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 360] degrees and _stem:[-90 le v le 90] degrees.

stem:[u] and stem:[v] are angular parameters and when numerical values are specified they shall use the current units for **plane_angle_measure.**

In the placement coordinate system defined above, the surface is represented by the equation stem:[S = 0], where
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
S(x, y, z) = x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} - R^{2}.
++++

The positive direction of the normal to the surface at any point on the surface is given by

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
( S_{x}, S_{y}, S_{z} ).
++++

The unit normal is given by

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N(u,v) = cos v((cos u)x + (sin u)y) + (sin v)z,
++++
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_surface\elementary_surface.position"
The location and orientation of the sphere. This attribute is inherited from the **elementary_surface** supertype.

**position.location** The centre of the sphere.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_surface.radius"
the radius of the sphere.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.toroidal_surface"
A **toroidal_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.elementary_surface,elementary_surface>>, which could be produced by revolving a circle about a line in its plane. The radius of the circle being revolved is referred to here as the **minor_radius** and the **major_radius** is the distance from the centre of this circle to the axis of revolution. A **toroidal_surface** is defined by the major and minor radii and the position and orientation of the surface.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>> ("axis of toroidal_surface");
stem:[R]:: <<express:geometry_schema.conical_surface.radius,radius>>;
stem:[r]:: "minor_radius";

and the surface is parametrised as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = C + (R + rcos v)((cos u)x + (sin u))y) + r(sin v)z
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u], stem:[v le 360] degrees.

stem:[u] and stem:[v] are angular parameters and when numerical values are specified they shall use the current units for **plane_angle_measure.**

In the placement coordinate system defined above, the surface is represented by the equation stem:[S = 0], where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
S(x, y, z) = x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} -2R * sqrt(x^{2}+y^{2}) - r^{2} + R^{2}.
++++

The positive direction of the normal to the surface at any point on the surface is given by

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
( S_{x}, S_{y}, S_{z} ).
++++

The unit normal is given by

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N(u,v) = cosv((cos u)x + (sin u)y) + (sin v)z.
++++

The sense of this normal is away from the nearest point on the circle of radius stem:[R] with centre stem:[C]anifold surface will be produced if the major radius is greater than the minor radius. If this condition is not fulfilled, the resulting surface will be self-intersecting.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.toroidal_surface\elementary_surface.position"
The location and orientation of the sphere. This attribute is inherited from the **elementary_surface** supertype.

**position.location** The central point of the torus.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.toroidal_surface.major_radius"
the major-radius of the torus;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.toroidal_surface.minor_radius"
the minor-radius of the torus.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_toroidal_surface"
A **degenerate_toroidal_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.toroidal_surface,toroidal_surface>>, in which the **minor_radius** is greater than the **major_radius**. In this subtype the parametric range is restricted in order to define a manifold surface which is either the inner 'lemon-shaped' surface, or the outer 'apple-shaped' portion of the self-intersecting surface defined by the supertype.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>> ("axis of degenerate_toroidal_surface");
stem:[R]:: <<express:geometry_schema.conical_surface.radius,radius>>;
stem:[r]:: "minor_radius";

and the surface is parametrised as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = C + (R + r cos(v))((cos(u))x + (sin u))y) + r(sin v))z
++++

where the parametrisation range is

If **select_outer** = .TRUE. :

stem:[0 le u le 360] degrees. stem:[-phi le v le phi] degrees.

If **select_outer** = .FALSE. :

stem:[0 le u le 360] degrees. stem:[phi le v le 360 - phi] degrees.

Where stem:[phi] degrees is the angle given by stem:[r cos(phi) = -R]. stem:[u] and stem:[v] are angular parameters and when numerical values are specified they shall use the current units for <<express:measure_schema.plane_angle_measure,plane_angle_measure>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_toroidal_surface.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig14]]
.Cross section of degenerate_toroidal_surface
====
image::Geomfig14.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_toroidal_surface.__note"
When *select_outer* = .FALSE. the surface normal points out of the enclosed volume and is defined by the equation


[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N(u,v) = cos v((cos u)x + (sin u)y) + (sin v)z.
++++




The sense of this normal is away from the furthest point on the circle of radius R in the plane normal to z centred at *C*.



The sense of this normal is opposite to the direction of


[stem%unnumbered]
++++
(del sigma)/(del u) xx (del sigma)/(del v) .
++++
*)

(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_toroidal_surface.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig14>> for illustration of the attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_toroidal_surface.select_outer"
a BOOLEAN flag used to distinguish between the two portions of the **degenerate_toroidal_surface**. If **select_outer** is true, the outer portion of the surface is selected and a closed `apple-shaped' axi-symmetric surface is defined. If **select_outer** is false, the inner portion is selected to define a closed 'lemon-shaped' axi-symmetric surface.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.degenerate_toroidal_surface.wr:WR1"
The major radius shall be less than the minor radius.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface"
A **dupin_cyclide_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.elementary_surface,elementary_surface>>, that is a generalisation of a <<express:geometry_schema.toroidal_surface,toroidal_surface>> in which the radius of the generatrix varies as it is swept around the directrix, passing through a maximum and a minimum value. The directrix is in general an ellipse, though that fact is not germane to the definition given here. The surface has two orthogonal planes of symmetry, and in both of them its cross-section is a pair of circles.

As with the <<express:geometry_schema.toroidal_surface,toroidal_surface>>, self-intersecting forms occur. The Dupin cyclides are special cases of a more general class of surfaces known as _generalized cyclides_ (or sometimes simply _cyclides_). The present specification does not cover the wider class.

The interpretation of the data is as follows:


[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>> (centre);
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[R]:: <<express:geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.generalised_major_radius,generalised_major_radius>>;
stem:[r]:: <<express:geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.generalised_minor_radius,generalised_minor_radius>>;
stem:[s]:: <<express:geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.skewness,skewness>> != ss;

and the surface is parametrised as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = ((x),(y),(z)) = C + 1/{R + s cosu cosv} ((r(s + R cosu cosv) + (R^2 - s^2)cosu),(sqrt(R^2 - s^2) sin u(R + cosv)),(sqrt(R^2 - s^2) sin v(r - s cosu)))
++++

//prior representation:: image::Dupc1.gif[]

where the domain of parametrisation is stem:[0 "P{degree}" le u,v le 360 "P{degree}"] , and stem:[sqrt] denotes the positive square root. stem:[u] and stem:[v] are angular parameters and when numerical values are specified they shall use the current units for <<express:measure_schema.plane_angle_measure,plane_angle_measure>>.

In the placement coordinate system defined above the Dupin cyclide surface has the algebraic representation stem:[S = 0], where

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
S = (x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} + R^{2} - r^{2} - s^{2})^{2} - 4(Rx - rs)^{2}- 4(R^{2} - s^{2})y^{2}.
++++

The positive direction of the normal vector at any point on the surface is given by

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
(S_{x},S_{y},S_{z}).
++++

In parametric terms, the unit surface normal vector is

//N(u,v) = (R cos(u) cos(v) + s)x + (√(R^{2} - s^{2}) sin(u) cos(v))y + (√(R^{2} - s^{2}) sin(v))z
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N(u,v) = (R cos(u) cos(v) + s)x + (sqrt(R^{2} - s^{2}) sin(u) cos(v))y + (sqrt(R^{2} - s^{2}) sin(v))z
++++

This enables the parametric surface representation to be rewritten as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = sigma_{0}(u,v) + rN(u,v),
++++

which shows that any Dupin cyclide with given values of stem:[R] and stem:[s] is a parallel offset from a base Dupin cyclide stem:[sigma_0(u,v)] with the same values of _R,s_ but with stem:[r = 0].

Further, the offset distance is precisely stem:[r]. This generalizes an important property of the torus.

The Dupin cyclide is a manifold surface under the conditions stem:[0 le s < r < R]. This form is known as a _ring cyclide_. Self-intersecting forms arise when the circles in either plane of symmetry intersect. The conditions stem:[0 < r le s < R] give a _horned cyclide_ and the conditions stem:[0 le s le R < r] a _spindle cyclide_. The sense of the surface normal given above is outwards from both circles in the upper view and from the annular region in the lower cross-sectional view in Figure 15. For the ring cyclide this means that it is outwards-pointing over the entire surface. For the horned cyclide the normal is inward-pointing over the smaller portion of the surface lying between the two self-intersection points. For the spindle cyclide the `spindle' corresponds to the `lemon' solid arising in the case of a self-intersecting torus. For this case of the Dupin cyclide the normal is outward-pointing over both the `apple' and 'lemon' solids enclosed by the surface.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig15]]
.Cross-sections of a Dupin cyclide with C = 0
====
image::Geomfig15.gif[]
====
*)
(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig16]]
.A Dupin ring cyclide
====
image::Geomfig16.gif[]
====
*)
(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig17]]
.A Dupin horned cyclide
====
image::Geomfig17.gif[]
====
*)
(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig18]]
.A Dupin spindle cyclide
====
image::Geomfig18.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__note"
These circles are illustrated in Figure 15, where the upper cross-section contains the generatrix circles of maximum and minimum radius, and the lower cross-section is in the plane of the directrix.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__note"
Further details of the properties and applications of this useful but unfamiliar surface may be found in PRATT [13],[14], and the further references they contain.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__note"
The three parameters stem:[r], stem:[R] and stem:[s] determine the centres and radii of the circles in the planes of symmetry, as shown in Figure 15. Conversely, knowledge of the geometry of these circles allows the defining cyclide parameters to be determined. In the upper and lower diagrams respectively of Figure 15 the circles have parameter values stem:[u = 0 * "deg"] (right), stem:[u = 180 * "deg"] (left), stem:[v = 0 "deg"] (inner) and stem:[v = 180 "deg"] (outer). The point with parameter values (0,0) is the extreme point on the positive x-axis. The parameter stem:[u] runs anticlockwise around both circles in the lower diagram, and the parameter stem:[v] runs clockwise round the left-hand circle and anticlockwise round the right-hand circle in the upper diagram.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__note"
The three forms of the Dupin cyclide are shown in s <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig15>>. In Figure 18 part of the exterior surface is removed to reveal the inner surface.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__note"
For ISO 10303 purposes, the values of stem:[R] and stem:[r] are of type *positive_length_measure* and stem:[s] is non-negative. The surface defined by the foregoing equations when one or more of stem:[R], stem:[r] and stem:[s] is negative corresponds to a reparametrisation of a Dupin cyclide for which these constants are all non-negative.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__note"
Both families of isoparametric curves of the Dupin cyclide consist of circles.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__note"
Dupin cyclides can be used to construct smooth joins between cylindrical and/or conical surfaces whose (possibly skew) axes have arbitrary relative orientations. Additionally, smooth T-junctions between cones and cylinders can be designed using Dupin cyclides.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__note"
Dupin cyclides also have uses as blending surfaces in solid modeling, generalising the use of the torus for this purpose.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.__note"
The Dupin cyclide as defined here is a quartic (degree four) algebraic surface of bounded extent. There also exists a cubic Dupin cyclide of infinite extent, not currently defined in this document.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface\elementary_surface.position"
defines a local system of coordinates in which two of the coordinate planes are axes of symmetry of the cyclide;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.generalised_major_radius"
the mean of the radii of the two circles forming the cyclide cross-section in the plane of the directrix;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.generalised_minor_radius"
the mean of the radii of the largest and smallest generatrix circles;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.skewness"
half the difference between the radii of the two cross-sectional circles in either plane of symmetry; when the **skewness** attribute is zero the surface is a torus; otherwise, its value determines the degree of asymmetry of the surface about the third plane perpendicular to its two planes of symmetry.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dupin_cyclide_surface.wr:WR1"
The skewness shall not be negative.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.swept_surface"
A **swept_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>>, that is constructed by sweeping a curve along another curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve"
the curve to be swept in defining the surface; if the swept curve is a pcurve, it is the image of this curve in 3D geometric space which is swept, not the parameter space curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_linear_extrusion"
A **surface_of_linear_extrusion** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface,swept_surface>>, or a generalised cylinder obtained by sweeping a curve in a given direction.

The parametrisation is as follows, where the curve has a parametrisation stem:[bblambda (u)], and stem:[bbV = bb"extrusion_axis"]

[%key]
stem:[bb V]:: <<express:geometry_schema.surface_of_linear_extrusion.extrusion_axis,extrusion axis>>;

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = lambda(u) + vV
++++

The parametrisation range for stem:[v] is stem:[-oo < v < oo] and for stem:[u] is defined by the curve parametrisation.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_linear_extrusion.extrusion_axis"
the direction of extrusion, the magnitude of this vector determines the parametrisation;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_linear_extrusion\swept_surface.swept_curve"
the curve to be swept in defining the surface.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_linear_extrusion.wr:IP1"
The surface shall not self-intersect.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_revolution"
A **surface_of_revolution** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface,swept_surface>>, obtained by rotating a curve one complete revolution about an axis.

The data shall be interpreted as below.

The parametrisation is as follows, where the curve has a parametrisation stem:[lambda(v)], stem:[C = "position.location"] and stem:[V = "position.z"]

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb v]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = C + (lambda(v) - C) cos(u) + ((lambda(v) - C) cdot V) V (1 - cos(u)) + V xx (lambda(v) - C) sin(u)
++++

In order to produce a single-valued surface with a complete revolution, the curve shall be such that when expressed in a cylindrical coordinate system stem:[(r,phi,z)] centred at stem:[C] with axis stem:[V], no two distinct parametric points on the curve shall have the same values for stem:[(r,z)].

For a surface of revolution the parametric range is stem:[0 le u le 360] degrees. stem:[u] is an angular parameter and when a numerical value is specified it shall use the current units for <<express:measure_schema.plane_angle_measure,plane_angle_measure>>.

The parameter range for stem:[v] is defined by the referenced curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_revolution.__note"
In this context a single valued surface is interpreted as one for which the mapping, from the interior of the rectangle in parameter space corresponding to its parametric range, to geometric space, defined by the surface equation, is one-to-one.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_revolution.__note"
The geometric shape of the surface is not dependent upon the curve parametrisation.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_revolution.axis_position"
a point on the axis of revolution and the direction of the axis of revolution;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_revolution\swept_surface.swept_curve"
the curve that is revolved about the axis line;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_revolution.axis_line"
the line coinciding with the axis of revolution.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_revolution.wr:IP1"
The surface shall not self-intersect.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_of_revolution.wr:IP2"
The <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> shall not be coincident with the **axis_line** for any finite part of its length.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve_swept_surface"
A **surface_curve_swept_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface,swept_surface>>, which is the result of sweeping a curve along a **directrix** curve lying on the **reference_surface**. The orientation of the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> during the sweeping operation is related to the normal to the **reference_surface**.

The <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> is required to be a curve lying in the plane stem:[z = 0] and this is swept along the **directrix** in such a way that the origin of the local coordinate system used to define the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> is on the **directrix** and the local X axis is in the direction of the normal to the **reference_surface**. The resulting surface has the property that the cross section of the surface by the normal plane to the **directrix** at any point is a copy of the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>>.

The orientation of the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> as it sweeps along the directrix is precisely defined by a **cartesian_transformation_operator_3d** with attributes:

**local_origin** as point (0,0,0),

**axis1** as the normal stem:[N] to the **reference_surface** at the point of the **directrix** with parameter stem:[u].

**axis3** as the direction of the tangent vector **t** at the point of the **directrix** with parameter stem:[u].

The remaining attributes are defaulted to define a corresponding transformation matrix stem:[T(u)].

The parametrisation is as follows, where the **directrix** has parametrisation stem:[mu(u)] and the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> curve has a parametrisation stem:[lambda(v)],


[%key]
stem:[bb mu(u)]:: Point on directrix;
stem:[bb T(u)]:: Transformation matrix at parameter(u);

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = mu(u) + T(u) lambda(v)
++++

In order to produce a continuous surface the **directrix** curve shall be tangent continuous.

For a **surface_curve_swept_surface** the parameter range for stem:[u] is defined by the **directrix** curve.

The parameter range for stem:[v] is defined by the referenced <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve_swept_surface.__note"
In the special case where the *directrix* is a planar curve the *reference_surface* is the plane of the *directrix* and the normal *N* is a constant.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve_swept_surface.__note"
The geometric shape of the surface is not dependent upon the curve parametrisations.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve_swept_surface.directrix"
the curve used to define the sweeping operation; the surface is generated by sweeping the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> along the **directrix**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve_swept_surface.reference_surface"
the surface containing the **directrix**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve_swept_surface.wr:WR1"
If the **directrix** is a <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve,surface_curve>> then the **reference_surface** shall be in the <<express:geometry_schema.surface_curve.basis_surface,basis_surface>> set for this curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve_swept_surface.wr:IP1"
The <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> shall be a curve lying in the plane stem:[z = 0].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_curve_swept_surface.wr:IP2"
The **directrix** shall be a curve lying on the **reference_surface**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.fixed_reference_swept_surface"
A **fixed_reference_swept_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface,swept_surface>>, which is the result of sweeping a curve along a **directrix**. The orientation of the curve during the sweeping operation is controlled by the **fixed_reference** direction. The <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> is required to be a curve lying in the plane stem:[z = 0] and this is swept along the **directrix** in such a way that the origin of the local coordinate system used to define the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> is on the **directrix** and the local X axis is in the direction of the projection of **fixed_reference** onto the normal plane to the **directrix** at this point. The resulting surface has the property that the cross section of the surface by the normal plane to the **directrix** at any point is a copy of the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>>.

The orientation of the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> as it sweeps along the directrix is precisely defined by a **cartesian_transformation_operator_3d** with attributes:

**local_origin** as point stem:[(0,0,0)],

**axis1** as **fixed_reference**,

**axis3** as the direction of the tangent vector **t** at the point of the **directrix** with parameter stem:[u].

The remaining attributes are defaulted to define a corresponding transformation matrix stem:[bb T(u)].

The parametrisation is as follows, where the **directrix** has parametrisation stem:[bb mu (u)] and the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> curve has a parametrisation stem:[bb lambda (v)],

[%key]
stem:[bb mu(u)]:: Point on directrix;
stem:[bb T(u)]:: Transformation matrix at parameter(u);

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = mu(u) + T(u)lambda(v)
++++

In order to produce a continuous surface the **directrix** curve the curve shall be tangent continuous.

For a **fixed_reference_swept_surface** the parameter range for stem:[u] is defined by the **directrix** curve. The parameter range for stem:[v] is defined by the referenced <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.fixed_reference_swept_surface.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig19]]
.Fixed_reference_swept_surface
====
image::Geomfig19.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.fixed_reference_swept_surface.__note"
The geometric shape of the surface is not dependent upon the curve parametrisations.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.fixed_reference_swept_surface.__note"
The attributes are illustrated in <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig19>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.fixed_reference_swept_surface.directrix"
the curve used to define the sweeping operation. The surface is generated by sweeping the <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> along the **directrix**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.fixed_reference_swept_surface.fixed_reference"
the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> used to define the orientation of <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> as it sweeps along the **directrix**..
*)


(*"geometry_schema.fixed_reference_swept_surface.wr:IP1"
The <<express:geometry_schema.swept_surface.swept_curve,swept_curve>> shall be a curve lying in the plane stem:[z = 0].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.fixed_reference_swept_surface.wr:IP2"
The **fixed_reference** shall not be parallel to a tangent vector to the **directrix** at any point along this curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bounded_surface"
A **bounded_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>> of finite area with identifiable boundaries.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bounded_surface.wr:IP1"
A **bounded_surface** has a finite non-zero surface area.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bounded_surface.wr:IP2"
A **bounded_surface** has boundary curves.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface"
A **b_spline_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface,bounded_surface>>, and is a general form of rational or polynomial parametric surface which is represented by control points, basis functions, and possibly, weights. As with the corresponding curve entity it has some special subtypes where some of the data can be derived.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[K1]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_upper,upper index on u control points>>;
stem:[K2]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_upper,upper index on v control points>>;
stem:[bb P_{ij}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.control_points,control_points>>;
stem:[w_{ij}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_surface.weights,weights>>;
stem:[d1]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_degree,u_degree>>;
stem:[d2]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_degree,v_degree>>;


The control points are ordered as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
P_{00}, P_{01}, P_{02}, ldots P_{K1K2-1}, P_{K1K2}.
++++

The weights, in the case of the rational subtype, are ordered similarly.

For each parameter, stem:[s = u] or stem:[v], if stem:[k] is the upper index on the control points and stem:[d] is the degree for stem:[s], the knot array is an array of stem:[(k + d + 2)] real numbers stem:[[s_{-d}, ldots ,s_{k+1}\]]_, such that for all indices stem:[j in [-d, k\], s_j le s_{j+1}]. This array is obtained from the appropriate **u_knots** or **v_knots** list by repeating each multiple knot according to the multiplicity.

stem:[N_i^d], the stem:[i"th"] normalised B-spline basis function of degree stem:[d], is defined on the subset:

stem:[[s_{i-d}, ldots ,s_{i+1}\]] of this array.

Let stem:[L] denote the number of distinct values amongst the knots in the knot list; stem:[L] will be referred to as the 'upper index on knots'. Let stem:[m_j] denote the multiplicity (i.e., number of repetitions) of the stem:[j"th"] distinct knot value. Then:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sum_(i=1)^{L} m_i = d + k + 2
++++

All knot multiplicities except the first and the last shall be in the range stem:[1, ldots, d]; the first and last may have a maximum value of stem:[d + 1].

The **surface_form** is used to identify specific quadric surface types (which shall have degree two), ruled surfaces and surfaces of revolution. As with the **b_spline_curve**, the **surface_form** is informational only and the spline data takes precedence.

The surface is to be interpreted as follows:

In the polynomial case the surface is given by the equation:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = sum_(i=0)^{K1} sum_(j=0)^{K2} (P_(i j) N_i^(d_1)(u) N_j^(d_2)(v))
++++

In the rational case the surface equation is:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = (sum_(i=0)^{K1} sum_(j=0)^{K2}
(w_(i j) P_(i j) N_i^(d_1)(u) N_j^(d_2)(v))) / (sum_(i=0)^{K1}
sum_(j=0)^{K2} (w_(i j) N_i^(d_1)(u) N_j^(d_2)(v)))
++++

*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.__note"
Identification of B-spline surface default values and subtypes is important for performance considerations and for efficiency issues in performing computations.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.__note"
A B-spline is _rational_ if and only if the weights are not all identical. If it is polynomial, the weights may be defaulted to all being 1.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.__note"
In the case where the B-spline surface is uniform, quasi-uniform or piecewise Bézier, the knots and knot multiplicities may be defaulted (i.e., non-existent in the data as specified by the attribute definitions). When the knots are defaulted, a difference of 1.0 between separate knots is assumed, and the effective parameter range for the resulting surface starts from 0.0. These defaults are provided by the subtypes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.__note"
The knots and knot multiplicities shall not be defaulted in the non-uniform case.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.__note"
The defaulting of weights and knots are done independently of one another.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.__note"
In evaluating the basis functions, a knot stem:[u] of, for example, multiplicity 3 is interpreted as a sequence _u, u, u,_ in the knot array.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.__note"
Definitions of the B-spline basis functions, stem:[N_i^(d1)(u)] and stem:[N_i^(d2)(v)], can be found in [1], [5] and [6]. It should be noted that there is a difference in terminology between these references.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_degree"
algebraic degree of basis functions in stem:[u];
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_degree"
algebraic degree of basis functions in stem:[v];
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.control_points_list"
a list of lists of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.surface_form"
an indicator of special surface types;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_closed"
indication of whether the surface is closed in the stem:[u] direction; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_closed"
indication of whether the svrface is closed in the stem:[v] direction; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.self_intersect"
a flag to indicate whether, or not, surface is self-intersecting; this is for information only.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_upper"
the upper index on control points in stem:[u] direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_upper"
the upper index on control points in stem:[v] direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.control_points"
an array (two-dimensional) of control points defining surface geometry. This array is constructed from the control points list.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.wr:WR1"
Any instantiation of this entity shall include one of the subtypes

<<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots,b_spline_surface_with_knots>>, <<express:geometry_schema.uniform_surface,uniform_surface>>, <<express:geometry_schema.quasi_uniform_surface,quasi_uniform_surface>>, or <<express:geometry_schema.bezier_surface,bezier_surface>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots"
A **b_spline_surface_with_knots** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface,b_spline_surface>>, in which the knot values are explicitly given. This subtype shall be used to represent non-uniform B-spline surfaces, and may also be used for other knot types. All knot multiplicities except the first and the last shall be in the range stem:[1, ..., d]; the first and last may have a maximum value of stem:[d + 1].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.__note"
In evaluating the basis functions, a knot stem:[u] of, for example, multiplicity 3 is interpreted as a sequence _u, u, u,_ in the knot array.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.u_multiplicities"
the multiplicities of the knots in the stem:[u] parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.v_multiplicities"
the multiplicities of the knots in the stem:[v] parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.u_knots"
the list of the distinct knots in the stem:[u] parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.v_knots"
the list of the distinct knots in the stem:[v] parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.knot_spec"
the description of the knot type;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.knot_u_upper"
the number of distinct knots in the stem:[u] parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.knot_v_upper"
the number of distinct knots in the stem:[v] parameter direction;
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.wr:WR1"
The function <<express:geometry_schema.constraints_param_b_spline,constraints_param_b_spline>> returns TRUE when the parameter constraints are verified for the stem:[u] direction.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.wr:WR2"
The function <<express:geometry_schema.constraints_param_b_spline,constraints_param_b_spline>> returns TRUE when the parameter constraints are verified for the stem:[v] direction.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.wr:WR3"
The number of **u_multiplicities** shall be the same as the number of **u_knots**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots.wr:WR4"
The number of **v_multiplicities** shall be the same as the number of **v_knots**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.uniform_surface"
A **uniform_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface,b_spline_surface>>, in which the knots are evenly spaced. Suitable default values for the knots and knot multiplicities can be derived in this case. A B-spline is _uniform_ if and only if all knots are of multiplicity and they differ by a positive constant from the preceding knot. In this subtype the knot spacing is 1.0, starting from _-degree_.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.uniform_surface.__note"
If explicit knot values for the surface are required, they can be derived as follows:

[%key]
stem:[K1]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_upper,upper index on u control points>>;
stem:[K2]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_upper,upper index on v control points>>;
stem:[bb P_{ij}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.control_points,control_points>>;
stem:[w_{ij}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_surface.weights,weights>>;
stem:[d1]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_degree,u_degree>>;
stem:[d2]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_degree,v_degree>>;
stem:[f1]:: <<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>;
stem:[f2]:: <<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>;`
stem:["uniform_knots"]:: <<express:geometry_schema.knot_type.uniform_knots,uniform_knots>>;`


stem:["ku_up" = "K1" + "d1" + 2],
ku_up is the value required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists in the stem:[u] direction. This is computed from the degree and the number of control points in this direction.


stem:["kv_up" = "K2" + "d2" + 2],
kv_up is the value required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists in the stem:[v] direction. This is computed from the degree and the number of control points in this direction.

The knot multiplicities and knots in the stem:[u] and stem:[v] parameter directions are then given by the function calls:


[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f1(d1,"ku_up","uniform_knots");
++++

and

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f2(d1,"ku_up","uniform_knots");
++++

and
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f1(d2,"kv_up","uniform_knots");
++++

and
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f2(d2,"kv_up","uniform_knots").
++++
*)

(*"geometry_schema.quasi_uniform_surface"
A **quasi_uniform_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface,b_spline_surface>>, in which the knots are evenly spaced, and except for the first and last, have multiplicity 1. Suitable default values for the knots and knot multiplicities are derived in this case. A B-spline is _quasi-uniform_ if and only if the knots are of multiplicity stem:[("degree"+1)] at the ends, of multiplicity 1 elsewhere, and they differ by a positive constant from the preceding knot. In this subtype the knot spacing is 1.0, starting from 0.0.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.quasi_uniform_surface.__note"
If explicit knot values for the surface are required, they can be derived as follows:



[%key]
stem:[K1]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_upper,upper index on u control points>>;
stem:[K2]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_upper,upper index on v control points>>;
stem:[bb P_{ij}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.control_points,control_points>>;
stem:[w_{ij}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_surface.weights,weights>>;
stem:[d1]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_degree,u_degree>>;
stem:[d2]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_degree,v_degree>>;
stem:[f1]:: <<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>;
stem:[f2]:: <<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>;`
stem:["quasi_uniform_knots"]:: <<express:geometry_schema.knot_type.uniform_knots,quasi_uniform_knots>>;`


stem:["ku_up" = "K1" + "d1" + 2],
ku_up is the value required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists in the stem:[u] direction. This is computed from the degree and the number of control points in this direction.


stem:["kv_up" = "K2" + "d2" + 2],
kv_up is the value required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists in the stem:[v] direction. This is computed from the degree and the number of control points in this direction.

The knot multiplicities and knots in the stem:[u] and stem:[v] parameter directions are then given by the function calls:


[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f1(d1,"ku_up","quasi_uniform_knots");
++++

and

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f2(d1,"ku_up","quasi_uniform_knots");
++++

and
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f1(d2,"kv_up","quasi_uniform_knots");
++++

and
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f2(d2,"kv_up","quasi_uniform_knots").
++++
*)

(*"geometry_schema.bezier_surface"
A **bezier_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface,b_spline_surface>>, in which the knots are evenly spaced and have high multiplicities. Suitable default values for the knots and knot multiplicities are derived in this case. In this subtype the knot spacing is 1.0, starting from 0.0.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bezier_surface.__note"
If explicit knot values for the surface are required, they can be derived as follows:

[%key]
stem:[K1] :: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_upper,upper index on u control points>>;
stem:[K2] :: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_upper,upper index on v control points>>;
stem:[bb P_{ij}] :: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.control_points,control_points>>;
stem:[w_{ij}] :: <<express:geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_surface.weights,weights>>;
stem:[d1] :: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.u_degree,u_degree>>;
stem:[d2] :: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface.v_degree,v_degree>>;
stem:[f1] :: <<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>;
stem:[f2] :: <<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>;
stem:["bezier_knots"] :: <<express:geometry_schema.knot_type.piecewise_bezier_knots,piecewise_bezier_knots>>;

stem:["ku_up" = "K1" + "d1" + 2],
ku_up is the value required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists in the stem:[u] direction. This is computed from the degree and the number of control points in this direction.


stem:["kv_up" = "K2" + "d2" + 2],
kv_up is the value required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists in the stem:[v] direction. This is computed from the degree and the number of control points in this direction.

The knot multiplicities and knots in the stem:[u] and stem:[v] parameter directions are then given by the function calls:


[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f1(d1,"ku_up","bezier_knots");
++++

and

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f2(d1,"ku_up","bezier_knots");
++++

and
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f1(d2,"kv_up","bezier_knots");
++++

and
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
f2(d2,"kv_up","bezier_knots").
++++
*)

(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_surface"
A **rational_b_spline_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface,b_spline_surface>>, which is a piecewise parametric rational surface described in terms of control points, associated weight values and basis functions. It is instantiated with any of the other subtypes of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface,b_spline_surface>>, which provide explicit or implicit knot values from which the basis functions are defined.

The surface is to be interpreted as follows:

image::BSsurf2.gif[]
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_surface.__note"
See <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface,b_spline_surface>> for details of the symbology used in the above equation.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_surface.weights_data"
the weights associated with the control points in the rational case;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_surface.weights"
the array (two-dimensional) of weight values constructed from the **weights_data**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_surface.wr:WR1"
The array dimensions for the weights shall be consistent with the control points data.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_surface.wr:WR2"
The weight value associated with each control point shall be greater than zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface"
A **locally_refined_spline_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface,bounded_surface>> that is a piecewise parametric polynomial or rational surface described in terms of control points and local B-spline functions. If the set of B-spline functions are linearly independent, they will form a basis. With appropriate attribute values, the surface is capable of representing single span or spline surfaces of explicit polynomial, rational, Bézier or B-spline type. However, the **locally_ refined_spline_surface** entity is intended for spline surfaces that do not have a tensor product structure. The degree of the surface can vary as the B-splines from which it is defined, need not to have the same polynomial degree.

The entity **locally_refined_spline_surface** can, with proper pre and post processing, be used to represent surfaces of the types: analysis suitable T-spline, standard T-spline, semi-standard T-spline, hierarchical B-spline and LR B-spline. Interpretation of the data is as follows: The surface, in the polynomial case, is given by:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = sum_(i=1)^K( P_i s_i N_i^(d_1)(u) N_i^(d_2)(v) )
++++

//prior representation::image::LRsurface.gif[]

In the rational case all weights shall be positive and the surface is given by:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = (
  sum_(i=1)^K ( w_i P_i s_i N_i^(d_1)(u) N_i^(d_2)(v) )
) / (
  sum_(i=1)^K ( w_i s_i N_i^(d_1)(u) N_i^(d_2)(v) )
)
++++

//prior representation::image::RationalLRsurf.gif[]

where (in both equations)
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
K = number of control points,
++++

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
P_{i} = control points(i),
++++

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
s_{i}= "scaling_factors"(i),
++++

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
w_{i} = "weights_data"(i),
++++

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N_{i}^{d1}(u) = "u_bspline"(i), and
++++

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
d1="u_degree".
++++

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N_{i}^{d2}(v) = "v_bspline"(i), and
++++

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
d2="v_degree".
++++

The weights, in the case of the rational subtype, are given in the same sequence as the coefficients. The knot values and multiplicities are defined in the entity <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>>. The B-splines are defined over a domain described by knot vectors in the parameter directions of the surface. However, in contrast to B-bspline surfaces, the basis functions or B-splines do not need to adapt to all knot lines in their supports. This facilitates the definition of a spline surface on a box partition.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig20]]
.Mesh for a locally refined spline surface of degree 2
====
image::Splinesurf.gif[]
====
*)
(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.__example"
The parameter domain of a locally refined spline surface illustrated as a regular mesh. The knot lines can have varying multiplicity throughout the domain. The numbers show the maximum multiplicity of each knot.There may exist B-splines with a given knot in its domain where this knot has a multiplicity less than the maximum multiplicity. Zero multiplicity indicates that this knot does not exist in that part of the domain. No B-splines have this knot in their definition. This mesh corresponds to a surface that is quadratic in both parameter directions for all local B-splines.



The locally refined spline surface contains two knot values lists, one for each parameter direction. The knot values are (0, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0) in the first parameter (stem:[u]) direction and (0.0, 1.0, 1.2, 1.8, 2.0, 3.0) in the second parameter direction.



The univariate B-splines in the first parameter direction are:



*  1u. Degree =2, knots=(0,1), multiplicities=(3,1)
*  2u. Degree=2, knots=(0,1,2), multiplicities=(2,1,1)
*  3u. Degree=2, knots=(0,1,3), multiplicities=(2,1,1)
*  4u. Degree=2, knots=(0,1,2), multiplicities=(1,1,2)
*  5u. Degree=2, knots=(0,1,2,3), multiplicities=(1,1,1,1)
*  6u. Degree=2, knots=0,1,3,4), multiplicities=(1,1,1,1)
*  7u. Degree=2, knots=(1,2,3), multiplicities=(1,2,1)
*  8u. Degree=2, knots=(1,2,3,4), multiplicities=(1,1,1,1)
*  9u. Degree=2, knots=(1,3,4), multiplicities=(1,1,2)
*  10u. Degree=2, knots=(2,3,4), multiplicities=(1,1,2)
*  11u. Degree=2, knots=(3,4), multiplicities=(1,3)



The univariate B-splines in the second parameter direction are:



*  1v. Degree =2, knots=(0,1), multiplicities=(3,1)
*  2v. Degree=2, knots=(0,1,2), multiplicities=(2,1,1)
*  3v. Degree=2, knots=(0,1,4), multiplicities=(2,1,1)
*  4v. Degree=2, knots=(0,1,2,3), multiplicities=(1,1,1,1)
*  5v. Degree=2, knots=(0,1,2,4), multiplicities=(1,1,1,1)
*  6v. Degree=2, knots=(0,1,4,5), multiplicities=(1,1,1,1)
*  7v. Degree=2, knots=(1,2,3,4), multiplicities=(1,1,1,1)
*  8v. Degree=2, knots=(1,2,4,5), multiplicities=(1,1,1,1
*  9v. Degree=2, knots=(1,4,5), multiplicities=(1,1,2)
*  10v. Degree=2, knots=(2,4,5), multiplicities=(1,1,2)
*  11v. Degree=2, knots=(4,5), multiplicities=(1,3)



Each control point is associated with the product of 2 B-splines one in stem:[u] and one in stem:[v]. The list of pairs of B-splines starts with all pairs of products of B-splines with knot values (0,0):



(1u.1v, 2u.1v, 5u.1v, 1u.3v, 2u.3v, 5u.3v 1u.6v, 2u.6v, 5u.6v).



The list continues with all the remaining B-spline products with first knot value stem:[v]  = 0 :



(8u.1v, 8u.2v, 8u.5v, 10u.1v, 10u.2v, 10u.5v, 11u.1v, 11u.2v, 11u.5v)



The list continues with all the B-spline products with first knot value stem:[v  = 1]



(1u.9v, 2u.9v, 5u.9v, 7u.7v, 11u.8v).



Finally we list all B-spline products with first knot values  stem:[v  = 2] and  stem:[v  = 4]



(9u.10v, 11u.10v) and (1u.11v, 3u.11v, 6u.11v, 9u.11v, 11u.11v).



In this example the splines are of type LR B-spline and all scaling factors have value 1.0. Corresponding to the orders in the consecutive lists above the complete data defining the geometry of the curve is a list of 30 *u_b_splines*, a corresponding list of 30 *v_b_splines*, a list of 5 *u_knots*, a list of 6 *v_knots*, a list of 30 control points and a corresponding list of 30 scaling factors.



The combined B-splines are not tested for linearly independence. The domain is [0,3]x[0,3]. The *surface_form* is unspecified and the matter of self-intersection and whether the surface is closed in any direction depends on the *control_points_list*.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.__note"
A B-spline surface compactly represents a large smooth area. However, if the shape is mainly smooth, but with some areas of higher complexity, the data size of a B-spline surface tends to be high. Due to the tensor product construction of the B-spline surface the knot lines are global and high data size in one area of the surface cannot be kept locally. The following approaches to define a B-spline surface with the property of local refinement have been pursued:



*  PHT splines,
*  General T-splines,
*  Analysis suitable T-splines,
*  Standard and semi-standard T-splines,
*  Hierarchical B-splines,
*  LR B-splines.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.__note"
A locally refined spline surface is rational if and only if the weights are not all identical; this can be represented by the <<express:geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_surface,rational_locally_refined_spline_surface>> subtype. If it is polynomial, the weights may be defaulted to all being 1.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.__note"
The degrees are associated with the local_b_splines and do not need to be constant throughout the surface.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.__note"
When the polynomial degree is the same for all B-splines and no knot lines exist that do not cross the entire parameter domain, the *locally_refined_spline_surface* will coincide with <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots,b_spline_surface_with_knots>>. In that case <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface_with_knots,b_spline_surface_with_knots>>. has the simplest and most compact representation.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.__note"
A *locally_refined_spline_surface* can be of type analysis suitable T-spline, hierarchical B-spline, LR-spline, semi standard T-spline or standard T-spline. The type is given by the enumeration <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum,locally_refined_spline_type_enum>>, and the packing and unpacking of the surface will depend on this type.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.u_b_splines"
the <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>> functions in the first (stem:[u]) parameter direction from which the surface is defined. These functions refer to the **u_knots** list for their knot parameter values;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.v_b_splines"
the <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>> functions in the second (stem:[v]) parameter direction from which the surface is defined. These functions refer to the **v_knots** list for their knot parameter values;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.v_b_splines.__note"
There may be some repetitions in the 2 lists of local_b_splines.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.u_knots"
the list of knot values for parameter stem:[u];
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.v_knots"
the list of knot values for parameter stem:[v];
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.control_points_list"
the list of control points. The **control_points_list** and consequently also the **u_b_splines**, the **v_b_splines** and **scaling_factors**, are ordered according to the knot values of the the combined B-spline coefficient constructed by multiplying corresponding univariate B-splines starting from the lowest knot value for each parameter. The knots in the v-parameter are the major sorting factor while the knots in the u-parameter the minor sorting factor. In case of ambiguities, the lists are sorted according to the upper knot value pair of the combined B-splines and the multiplicities of the first knots of the univariate B-splines.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.scaling_factors"
the list of scaling factors used to ensure that the non-zero B-spline basis functions add up to unity (partition of unity) at all points in the domain. Scaling factors not equal to 1.0 are sometimes needed for semi_standard_t_spline and lr_b_spline definitions to ensure that the collection of B-spline basis functions form a partition of unity. For analysis_suitable_t_spline and standard_t_spline definitions, partition of unity is achieved without use of scaling factors (i.e., the scaling factor is always equal to 1.0). For hierarchical_b_spline definitions, a scaling factor equal to 1.0 is used, although there is no partition of unity achieved.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.linearly_independent"
indicates whether this property has been tested for and in that case the result of the test; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.linearly_independent.__note"
The test is applied to the set of products of <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>>s used as coefficients of the control points . Not all types of locally refined surfaces are linearly independent by default.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.locally_refined_spline_type"
the type of the initial locally refined spline surface that provides rules for how the stored surface can be unpacked to its original format;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.self_intersect"
indication whether the surface is self-intersecting; this is for information only.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.surface_form"
indication of special surface types.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.u_closed"
indication whether the surface is closedin the stem:[u] direction; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.v_closed"
indication whether the surface is closedin the stem:[v] direction; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.domain"
the parameter domain corresponding to the surface. domain(1),(1) is the minimum parameter value in the stem:[u] direction; domain(1),(2) is the maximum parameter value in the stem:[u] direction; domain(2),(1) and domain (2),(2) gives the minimum and maximum parameter values in the stem:[v] direction. If all B-splines at the boundary have a knot multiplicity equal to the degree+1 at this boundary, the domain will coincide with the domain given by the minimum and maximum values in **u_knots** and **v_knots**. Otherwise, it will be coincide with a domain given by nearby knots depending on knot multiplicities.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.wr:WR1"
the number of **u_b_splines** shall be equal to the number of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.wr:WR2"
the number of **v_b_splines** shall be equal to the number of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.wr:WR3"
the number of **scaling_factors** shall be equal to the number of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface.wr:WR4"
all **scaling_factors** shall be positive and not greater than 1.0.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_surface"
A **rational_locally_refined_spline_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_surface,locally_refined_spline_surface>> that is a piecewise parametric rational surface described in terms of control points and local B-spline functions.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_surface.weights_data"
the supplied values of the weights.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_surface.wr:WR1"
the size of the of **weights_data** list shall be equal to the number of control points
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_surface.wr:WR2"
all values in the **weights_data** list shall be positive.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface"
A **rectangular_trimmed_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface,bounded_surface>>, in which the boundaries are the constant parametric lines stem:[u_1 = u1], u_2 = u2_, stem:[v_1 = v1] and stem:[v_2 = v2]. All these values shall be within the parametric range of the referenced surface. Cyclic properties of the parameter range are assumed.

The rectangular trimmed surface inherits its parametrisation directly from the basis surface and has parameter ranges from 0 to stem:[|u_2 - u_1|] and 0 to stem:[|v_2 - v_1|]. The derivation of the new parameters from the old uses the algorithm described in <<express:geometry_schema.trimmed_curve,trimmed_curve>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.__note"
For example, 370 degrees is equivalent to 10 degrees, for those surfaces whose parametric form is defined using circular functions (sine and cosine).
*)

(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.__note"
If the surface is closed in a given parametric direction, the values of stem:[u_1] or stem:[v_2] may require to be increased by the cyclic range.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.basis_surface"
the surface being trimmed;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.u1"
the first stem:[u] parametric value;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.u2"
the second stem:[u] parametric value;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.v1"
the first stem:[v] parametric value;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.v2"
the second stem:[v] parametric value;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.usense"
a flag to indicate whether the direction of the first parameter of the trimmed surface agrees with or opposes the sense of stem:[u] in the basis surface;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.vsense"
a flag to indicate whether the direction of the second parameter of the trimmed surface agrees with or opposes the sense of stem:[v] in the basis surface.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.wr:WR1"
The attributes **u1** and **u2** shall have different values.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.wr:WR2"
The attributes **v1** and **v2** shall have different values.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.wr:WR3"
With the exception of those surfaces closed in the stem:[u] parameter direction, **usense** shall be compatible with the ordered parameter values for stem:[u].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.wr:WR4"
With the exception of those svrfaces closed in the stem:[v] parameter direction, **vsense** shall be compatible with the ordered parameter valves for stem:[v].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface.wr:IP1"
The domain of the trimmed surface shall be within the domain of the surface being trimmed.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface"
A **curve_bounded_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface,bounded_surface>>, and is a parametric surface with curved boundaries defined by one or more <<express:geometry_schema.boundary_curve,boundary_curve>>s or <<express:geometry_schema.degenerate_pcurve,degenerate_pcurve>>s. One of the <<express:geometry_schema.boundary_curve,boundary_curve>>s may be the outer boundary; any number of inner boundaries is permissible. The outer boundary may be defined implicitly as the natural boundary of the surface; this is indicated by the **implicit_outer** flag being true. In this case at least one inner boundary shall be defined. For certain types of closed, or partially closed, surface (such as a cylinder) it may not be possible to identify any given boundary as outer. The region of the **curve_bounded_surface** in the **basis_surface** is defined to be the portion of the basis surface in the direction of stem:[n xx t] from any point on the boundary, where stem:[n] is the surface normal and stem:[t] the boundary curve tangent vector at this point. The region so defined shall be arcwise connected.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig21]]
.Curve_bounded_surface
====
image::Geomfig20.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.basis_surface"
the surface to be bounded;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.boundaries"
the bounding curves of the surface, other than the implicit outer boundary, if present; at most, one of these may be identified as an outer boundary by being of type <<express:geometry_schema.outer_boundary_curve,outer_boundary_curve>>;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.implicit_outer"
a Boolean flag which, if true, indicates the natural boundary of the surface is used as an outer boundary.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.implicit_outer.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig11>> for interpretation of these attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.wr:WR1"
No explicit outer boundary shall be present when **implicit_outer** is TRUE.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.wr:WR2"
The outer boundary shall only be implicitly defined if the **basis_surface** is bounded.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.wr:WR3"
At most, one outer boundary curve shall be included in the set of boundaries.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.wr:WR4"
Each <<express:geometry_schema.boundary_curve,boundary_curve>> shall lie on the **basis_surface**. This is verified from the **basis_surface** attribute of the <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface,composite_curve_on_surface>> supertype for each element of the **boundaries** list.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.wr:IP1"
Each curve in the set of **boundaries** shall be closed.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.wr:IP2"
No two curves in the set of **boundaries** shall intersect.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.wr:IP3"
At most one of the boundary curves may enclose any other boundary curve. If an <<express:geometry_schema.outer_boundary_curve,outer_boundary_curve>> is designated, only that curve may enclose any other boundary curve.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.boundary_curve"
A **boundary_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve_on_surface,composite_curve_on_surface>> suitable for the definition of a surface boundary.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.boundary_curve.wr:WR1"
The derived <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve.closed_curve,closed_curve>> attribute of the <<express:geometry_schema.composite_curve,composite_curve>> supertype shall be TRUE.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.outer_boundary_curve"
An **outer_boundary_curve** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.boundary_curve,boundary_curve>> which has the additional semantics of defining an outer boundary of a surface. No more than one such curve shall be included in the set of <<express:geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface.boundaries,boundaries>> of a <<express:geometry_schema.curve_bounded_surface,curve_bounded_surface>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface"
A **rectangular_composite_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface,bounded_surface>> composed of a rectangular array of **n_u** by **n_v** segments or patches. Each segment shall be finite and topologically rectangular (i.e., it corresponds to a rectangle in parameter space). The segment shall be either a <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_surface,b_spline_surface>> or a <<express:geometry_schema.rectangular_trimmed_surface,rectangular_trimmed_surface>>. There shall be at least positional continuity between adjacent segments in both directions; the composite surface may be open or closed in the stem:[u] direction and open or closed in the stem:[v] direction.

For a particular segment stem:[S_{ij} = bb"segments" [i\][j\]]:

The preceding segment in the stem:[u] direction is stem:[S_{(i-1)j}] and the preceding segment in the stem:[v] direction is stem:[S_{i(j-1)}]; similarly for following segments.

If **segments[i][j].u_sense** is TRUE, the boundary of stem:[S_{ij}] where it adjoins stem:[S_{(i=1)j}] is that where the stem:[u] parameter (of the underlying bounded surface) is high.

If **segments[i][j].u_sense** is FALSE, it is at the low stem:[u] boundary; similarly for the **v_sense** indicator.

The stem:[u] parametrisation of stem:[S_{ij}] in the composite surface is from stem:[i-1] to stem:[i], mapped linearly from the parametrisation of the underlying bounded surface.

If stem:[U] is the stem:[u] parameter for the **rectangular_composite_surface** and stem:[u_{ij0} le u_{ij} ij le u_{ij1}], is the stem:[u] parameter for **segments[i][j]**, these parameters are related by the equations:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
U = (i - 1) + (u_{ij} - u_{ij0})/(u_{ij1} - u_{ij0}), u_{ij} = u_{ij0} + (U -(i-1))(u_{ij1} - u_{ij0}),
++++

if **segments[i][j].u_sense** = TRUE;

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
U = i - (u_{ij} - u_{ij0})/(u_{ij1} - u_{ij0}), u_{ij} = u_{ij0} - (U -i)(u_{ij1} - u_{ij0}),
++++

if **segments[i][j].u_sense** = FALSE.

The stem:[v] parametrisation is obtained in a similar way.

Thus the composite surface has parametric range 0 to **n_u**, 0 to **n_v**.

The degree of continuity of the joint between stem:[S_{ij}] and stem:[S_{(i+1)j}] is given by **segments[i][j].u_transition**.

For the last patch in a row stem:[S_{(n_u)j}] this may take the value **discontinuous**, if the composite surface is open in the stem:[u] direction; otherwise it is closed here, and the transition code relates to the continuity to stem:[S_{1j}]; similarly for **v_transition**. **discontinuous** shall not occur elsewhere in the **segments surface_patch** transition codes.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface.segments"
a rectangular array (represented by a list of list) of component surface patches. Each such patch contains information on the senses and transitions.

**segments[i][j].u_transition** refers to the state of continuity between **segments[i][j]** and **segments[i+1][j]**. The last column ({blank}**segments[n_u][j].u_transition**{blank}) may contain the value **discontinuous**, meaning that (that row of) the surface is not closed in the stem:[u] direction; the rest of the list shall not contain this value.

The last row ({blank}**segments[i][n_v].v_transition**{blank}) may contain the value **discontinuous**, meaning that (that column of) the surface is not closed in the stem:[v] direction; the rest of the list shall not contain this value.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface.n_u"
the number of surface oatches in the stem:[u] parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface.n_v"
the number of surface oatches in the stem:[v] parameter direction.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface.wr:WR1"
Each sub-list in the **segments** list shall contain **n_v** <<express:geometry_schema.surface_patch,surface_patch>>es.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface.wr:WR2"
Other constraints on the segments (veriofied by <<express:geometry_schema.constraints_rectangular_composite_surface,constraints_rectangular_composite_surface>> function are:

that the component surfaces are all either rectangular trimmed surfaces or B-spline surfaces;

that the <<express:geometry_schema.transition_code,transition_code>>s in the **segments** list do not contain the value **discontinuous** except for the last row or column; when this occurs, it indicates that the surface is not closed in the appropriate direction.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface.wr:IP1"
The senses of the component surfaces are as specified in the <<express:geometry_schema.surface_patch.u_sense,u_sense>> and <<express:geometry_schema.surface_patch.v_sense,v_sense>> attributes of each element of **segments**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_patch"
A **surface_patch** is a type of <<express:representation_schema.founded_item,founded_item>> and is a bounded surface with additional transition and sense information which is used to define a <<express:geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface,rectangular_composite_surface>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_patch.parent_surface"
the surface which defines the geometry and boundaries of the surface patch;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_patch.parent_surface.__note"
Since *surface_patch* is not a subtype of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> the instance of <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface,bounded_surface>> used as *parent_surface* is not automatically associated with the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> of the <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> using a <<express:geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface,rectangular_composite_surface>> containing this *surface_patch*. It is therefore necessary to ensure that the <<express:geometry_schema.bounded_surface,bounded_surface>> instance is explicitly included in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with the appropriate <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.surface_patch.u_transition"
the minimum state of geometric continuity along the second stem:[u] boundary of the patch as it joins the first stem:[u] boundary of its neighbour. In the case of the last patch, this defines the state of continuity between the first stem:[u] boundary and last stem:[u] boundary of the <<express:geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface,rectangular_composite_surface>>;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_patch.v_transition"
the minimum state of geometric continuity along the second stem:[v] boundary of the patch as it joins the first stem:[v] boundary of its neighbour. In the case of the last patch, this defines the state of continuity between the first stem:[v] boundary and last stem:[v] boundary of the <<express:geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface,rectangular_composite_surface>>;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_patch.u_sense"
the relationship between the sense (increasing parameter value) of the patch and the sense of the **parent_surface**. If **u_sense** is TRUE, the first stem:[u] boundary of the patch is the one where the parameter stem:[u] takes its lowest value; it is the highest value boundary if sense is FALSE.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_patch.v_sense"
the relationship between the sense (increasing parameter value) of the patch and the sense of the **parent_surface**. If **v_sense** is TRUE, the first stem:[v] boundary of the patch is the one where the parameter stem:[v] takes its lowest value; it is the highest value boundary if sense is FALSE.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_patch.using_surfaces"
the bag of <<express:geometry_schema.rectangular_composite_surface,rectangular_composite_surface>>s which use this **surface_patch** in their definition. This bag shall not be empty.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_patch.wr:WR1"
A curve bounded surface shall not be used to define a surface patch.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_surface"
A **offset_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>> and is a procedural definition of a simple offset surface at a normal distance from the originating surface. **distance** may be positive, negative or zero to indicate the preferred side of the surface. The positive side and the resultant offset surface are defined as follows:

Define unit tangent vectors of the base surface in the stem:[u] and stem:[v] directions; denote these by stem:[sigma_u] and stem:[sigma_v].

Take the cross product, stem:[N = sigma_u xx sigma_v], of these (which shall be linearly independent, or there is no offset surface).

stem:[N] shall be extended by continuity at singular points, if possible.

Normalise stem:[N] to get a unit normal (to the surface) vector.

Move the offset distance (which may be zero) along that vector to find the point on the offset surface.

The offset surface takes its parametrisation directly from that of the basis surface, corresponding points having identical parameter values. The **offset_surface** is parametrised as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v) = S(u,v) + dN.
++++

Where stem:[N] is the unit normal vector to the basis surface stem:[S (u,v)] at parameter values stem:[(u,v)], and stem:[d] is **distance**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_surface.__note"
The definition allows the *offset_surface* to be self-intersecting.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.offset_surface.__note"
Care should be taken when using this entity to ensure that the offset distance never exceeds the radius of curvature in any direction at any point of the basis surface. In particular, the surface should not contain any ridge or singular point.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.offset_surface.basis_surface"
the surface that is to be offset;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_surface.distance"
the offset distance, which may be positive, negative or zero. A positive offset distance is measured in the direction of the surface normal;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.offset_surface.self_intersect"
a flag to indicate whether or not the surface is self-intersecting; this is for information only.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.oriented_surface"
A **oriented_surface** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>> for which the direction of the surface normal may be reversed.

The unit normal **N**, at any point on the **oriented_surface** is defined by the eqations:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N (u,v) = ((del sigma)/(del  u) xx (del sigma)/(del v)), "if orientation = .TRUE.",
++++

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
N (u,v) = - ((del sigma)/(del  u) xx (del sigma)/(del v)), "if orientation = .FALSE.",
++++
*)


(*"geometry_schema.oriented_surface.__note"
An *oriented_surface* may be instantiated with other subtypes of surface. For example a complex instance of *oriented_surface*, with *orientation* = .FALSE., and *spherical_surface* defines a spherical surface with an inward pointing normal.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.oriented_surface.orientation"
a flag which indicates whether, or not, the direction of the surface normal is reversed.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_replica"
A **surface_replica** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>> which defines a replica of an existing surface in a different location. It is defined by referencing the parent surface and a transformation which gives the new position and possible scaling. The original surface is not affected. The geometric characteristics of the surface produced will be identical to that of the parent surface, but, where the transformation includes scaling, the size may differ.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_replica.parent_surface"
the surface that is being copied;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_replica.transformation"
the <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_transformation_operator_3d,cartesian_transformation_operator_3d>> which defines the location, orientation and scaling of the surface replica relative to that of the parent surface.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.surface_replica.wr:WR1"
A **surface_replica** shall not participate in its own definition.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.volume"
A **volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> that is a three dimensional solid of finite volume with a tri-parametric representation.

Each volume has a parametric representation
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w)
++++

where stem:[u, v, w] are independent dimensionless parameters. For each stem:[(u,v,w)] within the parameter range:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
r = V(u,v,w),
++++

gives the coordinates of a point within the volume.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.volume.__note"
In this version of the standard the parameter ranges for the standard primitives have been standardised, mainly to [0:1], to ensure that they are dimensionless quantities.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.volume.wr:WR1"
The coordinate space dimensionality shall be 3.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.block_volume"
A **block_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> in the form of a solid rectangular parallelepiped, defined with a location and placement coordinate system.
The **block_volume** is specified by the positive lengths **x**, **y**, and **z** along the axes of the placement coordinate system, and has one vertex at the origin of the placement coordinate system.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>> (corner),
stem:[x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.block_volume.p,x>> (length),
stem:[y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.block_volume.p,y>> (depth),
stem:[z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.block_volume.p,z>> (height).

and the volume is parametrised as:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = C + x + vdy + whz
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[0 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.block_volume.position"
the location and orientation of the axis system for the primitive. The block has one vertex at **position.location** and its edges aligned with the placement axes in the positive sense;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.block_volume.x"
the size of the block along the placement X axis, ({blank}**position.p[1]**{blank});
*)


(*"geometry_schema.block_volume.y"
the size of the block along the placement Y axis, ({blank}**position.p[2]**{blank});
*)


(*"geometry_schema.block_volume.z"
the size of the block along the placement Z axis, ({blank}**position.p[3]**{blank}).
*)


(*"geometry_schema.wedge_volume"
A **wedge_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> which can be envisioned as the result of intersecting a block with a plane perpendicular to one of its faces. It is defined with a location and local coordinate system. A triangular/trapezoidal face lies in the plane defined by the placement X and Y axes. This face is defined by positive lengths **x** and **y** along the placement X and Y axes, by the length **ltx** (if non-zero) parallel to the X axis at a distance **y** from the placement origin, and by the line connecting the ends of the **x** and **ltx** segments.

The remainder of the wedge is specified by the positive length **z** along the placement Z axis which defines a distance through which the trapezoid or triangle is extruded. If **ltx** = 0, the wedge has five faces; otherwise, it has six faces.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>> (corner);
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[l]:: x ("length");
stem:[d]:: y ("depth");
stem:[h]:: z ("height");
stem:[l_{min}]:: ("length");

and the volume is parametrised as:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = C + u((1 - v)l + vl_{min}) x + vdy + whz
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[0 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1].
*)

(*"geometry_schema.wedge_volume.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig22]]
.Wedge_volume and its attributes
====
image::Geomfig21.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.wedge_volume.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig22>> for interpretation of attributes.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.wedge_volume.position"
the location and orientation of the axis system for the primitive. The wedge has one vertex at **position.location** and its edges aligned with the placement axes in the positive sense;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.wedge_volume.x"
the size of the wedge along the placement X axis, ({blank}**position.p[1]**{blank});
*)


(*"geometry_schema.wedge_volume.y"
the size of the wedge along the placement Y axis, ({blank}**position.p[2]**{blank});
*)


(*"geometry_schema.wedge_volume.z"
the size of the wedge along the placement Z axis, ({blank}**position.p[3]**{blank});
*)


(*"geometry_schema.wedge_volume.ltx"
the length in the positive X direction of the smaller surface of the wedge.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.wedge_volume.wr:WR1"
The attribute **ltx** shall be non-negative and less than **x**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pyramid_volume"
A **pyramid_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> in the form of a solid pyramid with a rectangular base. The apex of the pyramid is directly above the centre point of the base. The **pyramid_volume** is specified by its position, which provides a placement coordinate system, its length, depth and height.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>> (corner);
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[l]:: x "length";
stem:[d]:: y "depth";
stem:[h]:: "height";

and the volume is parametrised as:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = C + w((l/2)x + (d/2)y + hz) + (1 - w)(ulx + vdy)
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[0 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pyramid_volume.position"
the location and orientation of the pyramid. **position** defines a placement coordinate system for the pyramid; the pyramid has one corner of its base at **position.location** and the edges of the base are aligned with the first two placement axes in the positive sense;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pyramid_volume.xlength"
the length of the base measured along the placement X axis ({blank}**position.p[1]**{blank});
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pyramid_volume.ylength"
the length of the base of the pyramid along the placement Y axis, ({blank}**position.p[2]**{blank});
*)


(*"geometry_schema.pyramid_volume.height"
the height of the apex above the plane of the base, measured in the direction of the placement Z axis, ({blank}**position.p[3]**{blank}).
*)


(*"geometry_schema.tetrahedron_volume"
A **tetrahedron_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> with 4 vertices and 4 triangular faces. It is defined by the four **cartesian_point** s which locate the vertices. These points shall not be coplanar.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[bb a]:: "point_1.coordinates";
stem:[bb b]:: "point_2.coordinates";
stem:[bb c]:: "point_3.coordinates";
stem:[bb d]:: "point_4.coordinates";

and the volume is parametrised as:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = a + u(b - a) + v(c - a) + w(d - a)
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[0 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1], with stem:[u + v + w le 1].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.tetrahedron_volume.point_1"
the <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> that locates the first vertex of the **tetrahedron_volume** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.tetrahedron_volume.point_2"
the <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> that locates the second vertex of the **tetrahedron_volume** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.tetrahedron_volume.point_3"
the <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> that locates the third vertex of the **tetrahedron_volume** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.tetrahedron_volume.point_4"
the <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> that locates the fourth vertex of the **tetrahedron_volume**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.tetrahedron_volume.wr:WR1"
The coordinate space dimension of **point_1** shall be 3.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.tetrahedron_volume.wr:WR1.__note"
The rule <<express:geometry_schema.compatible_dimension,compatible_dimension>> ensures that all the <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> attributes of this entity have the same dimension.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.tetrahedron_volume.wr:WR2"
The points **point_1, point_2, point_3** and **point_4** shall not be coplanar. This is tested by verifying that the <<express:geometry_schema.cross_product,cross_product>> of the three directions from **point_1** to each of the other points is non-zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume"
A **hexahedron_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> with 8 vertices and 6 four-sided faces. It is defined by the 8 points which locate the vertices. The volume is parametrised as

//todo: look at multi-line encoding alternatives
[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = (1 - u)(1 - v)(1 - w)P_{1} + (1 - u)(v)(1 -w)P_{2} + u * v(1 - w)P_{3} + u(1 -v)(1 - w)P_{4} + (1 - u)(1 - v)w * P_{5} + (1 - u)(v)w * P_{6} + u * v * wP_{7} + u(1 -v)w * P_{8}
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[0 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1]. and stem:[bb"P"_i] denotes the position vector of **points[i]**
*)

(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.__figure"
[[figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig23]]
.Hexahedron_volume and its attributes
====
image::Geomfig22.gif[]
====
*)

(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.points"
the <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>>s that locate the vertices of the **hexahedron_volume**. These points are ordered such that **points[1], points[2], points[3], points[4]** define, in anti-clockwise order, one planar face of the solid and, in corresponding order.

**points[5], points[6], points[7], points[8]** define the opposite face.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.points.__note"
See <<figure-geometry_schema-Geomfig23>> for further information about the positions of the vertices.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR1"
The first 4 **points** shall be coplanar.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR2"
The final 4 **points** shall be coplanar.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR3"
The points **points[1], points[4], points[8], points[5]**, shall be coplanar.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR4"
The points **points[4], points[3], points[7], points[8]**, shall be coplanar..
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR5"
The points **points[3], points[2], points[6], points[7]**, shall be coplanar.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR6"
The points **points[1], points[5], points[6], points[2]**, shall be coplanar.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR7"
The points **points[5], points[6], points[7], points[8]**, shall all lie on the same side of the plane of **points[1], points[2], points[3]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR8"
The points **points[3], points[7], points[6], points[2]**, shall all lie on the same side of the plane of **points[1], points[4], points[8]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR9"
The points **points[4], points[3], points[7], points[8]**, shall all lie on the same side of the plane of **points[1], points[2], points[5]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR10"
The points **points[1], points[2], points[3], points[4]**, shall all lie on the same side of the plane of **points[5], points[6], points[7]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR11"
The points **points[1], points[4], points[8], points[5]**, shall all lie on the same side of the plane of **points[3], points[7], points[6]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR12"
The points **points[1], points[5], points[6], points[2]**, shall all lie on the same side of the plane of **points[3], points[7], points[8]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR12.__note"
The above 6 rules ensure that the *points* define a convex figure.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.hexahedron_volume.wr:WR13"
The point **points[1]** shall have coordinate space dimensionality 3.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_volume"
A **spherical_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> in the form of a sphere of radius stem:[R]. A **spherical_volume** is defined by the radius and the position of the solid.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>> (centre);
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[R]:: <<express:geometry_schema.conical_surface.radius,radius>>;

and the volume is parametrised as:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = C + w R cos((pi v)/2)((cos(2 pi u))x + (sin(2 pi u))y) + w R(sin ((pi v)/2))z
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[-1 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1].

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[0 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_volume.position"
the location and parametric orientation of the volume; **position.location** is the centre of the sphere;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.spherical_volume.radius"
the radius of the sphere.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_volume"
A **cylindrical_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> in the form of a circular cylinder. A **cylindrical_volume** is defined by its orientation and location, its radius and its height.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[R]:: <<express:geometry_schema.conical_surface.radius,radius>>;
stem:[h]:: "height";

and the volume is parametrised as:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = C + wR(cos (2 * pi * u))x + (sin(2 * pi * u)y) + vHz
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[0 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1] .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_volume.position"
the location and orientation of the cylinder; **position.location** is a point on the axis of the cylinder, and **position.p[3]** is the direction of the axis of the cylinder;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_volume.radius"
the radius of the cylinder;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cylindrical_volume.height"
the height of the cylinder.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume"
A **eccentric_conical_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> in the form of a skew cone. The **eccentric_conical_volume** may have an elliptic cross section, and may have a central axis which is not perpendicular to the base. Depending upon the value of the **ratio** attribute it may be truncated, or may take the form of a generalised cylinder. When truncated the top face of the cone is parallel to the plane of the base and has a similar cross section.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[bb R_{1}]:: "semi_axis_1";
stem:[bb R_{2}]:: "semi_axis_2";
stem:[H]:: "height";
stem:[o_x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.x_offset,x_offset>>;
stem:[o_y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.y_offset,y_offset>>;
stem:[s]:: "ratio";

and the volume is parametrised as:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = C + v(o_{x}x + o_{y}y)+ w(1 + v(s - 1))( R_{1}((2 * pi * u))x + R_{2} (sin(2 * pi * u)y)) + vHz
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[0 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1] .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.__note"
In the placement coordinate system defined by *position* the central point of the top face of the *eccentric_conical_volume* has coordinates stem:[(bb "x_offset", bb "y_offset", bb "height")].
*)

(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.__note"
If *ratio* = 0.0 the *eccentric_conical_volume* includes the apex. If *ratio* = 1.0 the *eccentric_conical_volume* is in the form of a generalised cylinder with all cross sections of the same dimensions.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.__note"
If *x_offset* = *y_offset* = 0.0 the eccentric_conical_volume has the form of a right elliptic cone or, with  stem:[R_1 = R_2], a right circular cone.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.position"
the location of the central point on the axis and the direction of **semi_axis_1** ; this defines the centre and plane of the base of the **eccentric_conical_volume**. **position.p[3]** is normal to the base of the **eccentric_conical_volume** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.semi_axis_1"
the length of the first radius of the base of the cone in the direction of **position.p[1]**;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.semi_axis_2"
the length of the second radius of the base of the cone in the direction of **position.p[2]**;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.height"
the height of the cone above the base measured in the direction of **position.p[3]**;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.x_offset"
the distance, in the direction of **position.p[1]**, from the central point of the top face of the cone to the point in the plane of this face directly above the central point of the base;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.y_offset"
the distance, in the direction of **position.p[2]**, from the central point of the top face of the cone to the point in the plane of this face directly above the central point of the base;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.ratio"
the ratio of a radius of the top face to the corresponding radius of the base of the cone.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.eccentric_conical_volume.wr:WR1"
The **ratio** shall not be negative.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.toroidal_volume"
A **toroidal_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> which could be produced by revolving a circular face about a line in its plane. The radius of the circle being revolved is referred to here as the **minor_radius** and the **major_radius** is the distance from the centre of this circle to the axis of revolution. A **toroidal_volume** is defined by the major and minor radii and the position and orientation of the volume.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>>;
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[R]:: <<express:geometry_schema.conical_surface.radius,radius>>;
stem:[r]:: "minor_radius";

and the volume is parametrised as:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = C + (R + wrcos(2 * pi * v))((cos(2 * pi * u))x + (sin(2 * pi * u))y) + wr(sin(pi * v))z
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[0 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1] .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.toroidal_volume.position"
the location and orientation of the solid, **position.location** is the central point of the torus;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.toroidal_volume.major_radius"
the major radius of the torus;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.toroidal_volume.minor_radius"
the minor radius of the torus.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.toroidal_volume.wr:WR1"
The minor radius shall be less than the major radius. This ensures that the parametric coordinates are unique for each point inside the volume.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.ellipsoid_volume"
A **ellipsoid_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> in the form of a solid ellipsoid. It is defined by its location and orientation and by the lengths of the three semi-axes.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[C]:: <<express:geometry_schema.placement.location,location>> (centre);
stem:[bb x]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[1]>>;
stem:[bb y]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[2]>>;
stem:[bb z]:: <<express:geometry_schema.axis2_placement_3d.p,p[3]>>;
stem:[bb a]:: "semi_axis_1";
stem:[bb b]:: "semi_axis_2";
stem:[bb c]:: "semi_axis_3";

and the volume is parametrised as:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = C + wcos((pi * v)/2) (a(cos(pi * u))x + b(sin(2 * pi * u))y) + wc(sin ((pi * v)/2))z
++++

where the parametrisation range is stem:[0 le u le 1], stem:[0 le v le 1] , and stem:[0 le w le 1] .
*)


(*"geometry_schema.ellipsoid_volume.position"
the location and orientation of the ellipsoid. **position.location** is a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> at the centre of the ellipsoid and the axes of the ellipsoid are aligned with the directions **position.p** ;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.ellipsoid_volume.semi_axis_1"
the length of the semi-axis of the ellipsoid in the **direction**** position.p[1]**;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.ellipsoid_volume.semi_axis_2"
the length of the semi-axis of the ellipsoid in the **direction**** position.p[2]**;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.ellipsoid_volume.semi_axis_3"
the length of the semi-axis of the ellipsoid in the **direction**** position.p[3]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume"
A **b_spline_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> that is a general form of tri-parametric volume field which is represented by control points and basis functions. As with the B-spline curve and surface entities it has special subtypes where some of the data can be derived.

The data is to be interpreted as follows:

[%key]
stem:[K1]:: "upper_index_on_u_control_values";
stem:[K2]:: "upper_index_on_v_control_values";
stem:[K2]:: "upper_index_on_v_control_values";
stem:[P_{ijk}]:: "control_values";
stem:[d1]:: "u_degree";
stem:[d2]:: "v_degree";
stem:[d3]:: "w_degree";

The control values are ordered as

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
P_{000}, P_{001}, P_{002}, . . . , P_{K1K2(K3-1)}, P_{K1K2K3}
++++

For each parameter, stem:[s = u] or stem:[v], or stem:[w] if stem:[k] is the upper index on the control points and stem:[d] is the degree for stem:[s], the knot array is an array of stem:[(k + d + 2)] real numbers stem:[[s_{-d}, ... ,s_{k+1}\]],

such that for all indices stem:[j in [-d, k\], s_{j} le s_{j+1}].

This array is obtained from the appropriate **knots_data** list by repeating each multiple knot according to the multiplicity.

stem:[N_i^d], the stem:[i"th"] normalised B-spline basis function of degree stem:[d], is defined on the subset:

stem:[[s_{i-d}, ... ,s_{i+1}\]] of this array.

Let stem:[L] denote the number of distinct values amongst the knots in the knot list; stem:[L] will be referred to as the 'upper index on knots'. Let stem:[m_j] denote the multiplicity (i.e., number of repetitions) of the stem:[j"th"] distinct knot value. Then:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
Sigma^L_{i=1} m_i = d + k + 2
++++

All knot multiplicities except the first and the last shall be in the range stem:[1, ..., d]; the first and last may have a maximum value of stem:[d + 1].

The parametric volume is given by the equation:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) =
  sum_(i=0)^{K1} sum_(j=0)^{K2} sum_(k=0)^{K3}
    P_(i j k) N_i^(d_1)(u) N_j^(d_2)(v) N_k^(d_3)(w)
++++

//prior representation::image::BSvol1.gif[]
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume.__note"
In evaluating the basis functions, a knot stem:[u] of, for example, multiplicity _3_ is interpreted as a sequence  stem:[u, u, u], in the knot array.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume.u_degree"
the algebraic degree of basis functions in stem:[u];
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume.v_degree"
the algebraic degree of basis functions in stem:[v];
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume.w_degree"
the algebraic degree of basis functions in stem:[w];
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume.control_points_list"
a list of lists of lists of control values;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume.u_upper"
the upper index on control values in u direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume.v_upper"
the upper index on control values in v direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume.w_upper"
the upper index on control values in w direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume.control_points"
the array (three-dimensional) of control values defining field geometry. This array is constructed from the control values list.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume.wr:WR1"
Any instantiation of this entity shall include one of the subtypes <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots,b_spline_volume_with_knots>>, or <<express:geometry_schema.bezier_volume,bezier_volume>>, or <<express:geometry_schema.uniform_volume,uniform_volume>>, or <<express:geometry_schema.quasi_uniform_volume,quasi_uniform_volume>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots"
A **b_spline_volume_with_knots** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_volume,b_spline_volume>> in which the knot values are explicitly given. This subtype shall be used to represent non-uniform B-spline volumes, and may also be used for other knot types.

All knot multiplicities except the first and the last shall be in the range stem:[1 ... "degree"]; the first and last may have a maximum value of stem:["degree" + 1].
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.__note"
In evaluating the basis functions, a knot stem:[u] of, for example, multiplicity _3_ is interpreted as a sequence stem:[u, u, u], in the knot array.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.u_multiplicities"
the multiplicities of the knots in the u parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.v_multiplicities"
the multiplicities of the knots in the v parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.w_multiplicities"
the multiplicities of the knots in the w parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.u_knots"
the list of the distinct knots in the u parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.v_knots"
the list of the distinct knots in the v parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.w_knots"
the list of the distinct knots in the w parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.knot_u_upper"
the number of distinct knots in the u parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.knot_v_upper"
the number of distinct knots in the v parameter direction;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.knot_w_upper"
the number of distinct knots in the w parameter direction.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.wr:WR1"
The function <<express:geometry_schema.constraints_param_b_spline,constraints_param_b_spline>> returns TRUE when the parameter constraints are verified for the **u-** direction.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.wr:WR2"
The function <<express:geometry_schema.constraints_param_b_spline,constraints_param_b_spline>> returns TRUE when the parameter constraints are verified for the **v-** direction.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.wr:WR3"
The function <<express:geometry_schema.constraints_param_b_spline,constraints_param_b_spline>> returns TRUE when the parameter constraints are verified for the **w-** direction.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.wr:WR4"
The number of **u_multiplicities** shall be the same as the number of **u_knots**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.wr:WR5"
The number of **v_multiplicities** shall be the same as the number of **v_knots**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots.wr:WR6"
The number of **w_multiplicities** shall be the same as the number of **w_knots**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bezier_volume"
A **bezier_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_volume,b_spline_volume>> in which the knots are evenly spaced and have high multiplicities. Suitable default values for the knots and knot multiplicities are derived in this case. In this subtype the knot spacing is 1.0, starting from 0.0
*)


(*"geometry_schema.bezier_volume.__note"
If explicit knot values for the volume are required, they can be derived as follows:



*ku_up := (SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.u_upper)/(SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.u_degree* + 1)



*kv_up := (SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.v_upper)/(SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.v_degree* + 1)



*kw_up := (SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.w_upper)/(SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.w_degree* + 1)



*ku_up* is the value required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists in the u direction. This is computed from the degree and the number of control values in this direction.



Similar computations are used to determine *kv_up, kw_up*.



The knot multiplicities and knots in the stem:[u], stem:[v] and stem:[w] parameter directions are then given by the function calls:



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.u_degree, ku_up, bezier_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.u_degree,ku_up, bezier_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.v_degree, kv_up, bezier_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.v_degree,kv_up, bezier_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.w_degree, kw_up, bezier_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.w_degree,kw_up, bezier_knots)
*)

(*"geometry_schema.uniform_volume"
A **uniform_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_volume,b_spline_volume>> in which the knots are evenly spaced. Suitable default values for the knots and knot multiplicities can be derived in this case.

A B-spline is _uniform_ if and only if all knots are of multiplicity 1 and they differ by a positive constant from the preceding knot. In this subtype the knot spacing is 1.0, starting from _-degree_.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.uniform_volume.__note"
If explicit knot values for the volume are required, they can be derived as follows:



*ku_up := SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.u_upper + SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.u_degree* + 2



*kv_up := SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.u_upper + SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.v_degree* + 2



*kw_up := SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.u_upper + SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.w_degree* + 2



*ku_up* is the value required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists in the u direction. This is computed from the degree and the number of control values in this direction.



Similar computations are used to determine *kv_up, kw_up*.



The knot multiplicities and knots in the stem:[u], stem:[v] and stem:[w] parameter directions are then given by the function calls:



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.u_degree, ku_up, uniform_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.u_degree,ku_up, uniform_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.v_degree, kv_up, uniform_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.v_degree,kv_up, uniform_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.w_degree, kw_up, uniform_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.w_degree,kw_up, uniform_knots)
*)

(*"geometry_schema.quasi_uniform_volume"
A **quasi_uniform_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_volume,b_spline_volume>> in which the knots are evenly spaced, and except for the first and last, have multiplicity 1. Suitable default values for the knots and knot multiplicities are derived in this case.

A B-spline is _quasi-uniform_ if and only if the knots are of multiplicity (degree+1) at the ends, of multiplicity 1 elsewhere, and they differ by a positive constant from the preceding knot. In this subtype the knot spacing is 1.0, starting from 0.0.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.quasi_uniform_volume.__note"
If explicit knot values for the volume are required, they can be derived as follows:



*ku_up := SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.u_upper - SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.u_degree* + 2



*kv_up := SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.u_upper - SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.v_degree* + 2



*kw_up := SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.u_upper - SELF\backslash b_spline_volume.w_degree* + 2



*ku_up* is the value required for the upper index on the knot and knot multiplicity lists in the u direction. This is computed from the degree and the number of control values in this direction.



Similar computations are used to determine *kv_up, kw_up*.



The knot multiplicities and knots in the stem:[u], stem:[v] and stem:[w] parameter directions are then given by the function calls:



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.u_degree, ku_up, quasi_uniform_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.u_degree,ku_up, quasi_uniform_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.v_degree, kv_up, quasi_uniform_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.v_degree,kv_up, quasi_uniform_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knot_mult,default_b_spline_knot_mult>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.w_degree, kw_up, quasi_uniform_knots)



<<express:geometry_schema.default_b_spline_knots,default_b_spline_knots>>(SELF\b_spline_volume.w_degree,kw_up, quasi_uniform_knots)
*)

(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_volume"
A **rational_b_spline_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_volume,b_spline_volume>> that is a piecewise parametric rational volume described in terms of control points, associated weight values and basis functions. It is instantiated with any of the other subtypes of <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_volume,b_spline_volume>>, which provide explicit or implicit knot values from which the basis functions are defined.

The volume is to be interpreted as follows:


[stem%unnumbered]
++++
V(u,v,w) = (
  sum_(i=0)^{K1} sum_(j=0)^{K2} sum_(k=0)^{K3} (
    w_(i j k) P_(i j k) N_i^(d_1)(u) N_j^(d_2)(v) N_k^(d_3)(w)
  )
) / (
  sum_(i=0)^{K1} sum_(j=0)^{K2} sum_(k=0)^{K3} (
    w_(i j k) N_i^(d_1)(u) N_j^(d_2)(v) N_k^(d_3)(w)
  )
)
++++

//prior representation::image::BSvol2.gif[]
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_volume.__note"
See <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_volume,b_spline_volume>> for details of the symbology used in the above equation.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_volume.weights_data"
the weights associated with the control points in the rational case;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_volume.weights"
the array (three-dimensional) of weight values constructed from the **weights_data**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_volume.wr:WR1"
The array dimensions for the weights shall be consistent with the control points data.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_b_spline_volume.wr:WR2"
The weight value associated with each control point shall be greater than zero.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume"
A **locally_refined_spline_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.volume,volume>> that is a piecewise parametric polynomial or rational volume described in terms of control points and local B-spline functions. If the set of B-spline functions are linearly independent, they will form a basis. With appropriate attribute values, the volume is capable of representing single span or spline volumes of explicit polynomial, rational, Bézier or B-spline type. However, the **locally_ refined_spline_volume** entity is intended for spline volumes that do not have a tensor product structure. The degree of the volume can vary as the B-splines from which it is defined, need not to have the same polynomial degree.

The entity **locally_refined_spline_volume** can, with proper pre and post processing, be used to represent volumes of the types:

* analysis suitable T-spline,
* standard T-spline,
* semi-standard T-spline,
* hierarchical B-spline
* LR B-spline.

Interpretation of the data is as follows:
//tt split sentence because of reference to two equations in the sentence.
//needs review by WG12 T1
The volume, in the polynomial case, is given by the following equation.

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v,w) = sum_(i=1)^K( P_i s_i N_i^(d_1)(u) N_i^(d_2)(v) N_i^(d_3)(w) )
++++

//prior representation::image::LRvol.gif[]

In the rational case all weights shall be positive and the volume is given by the following equation..

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
sigma(u,v,w) = (
  sum_(i=1)^K (h_i P_i s_i N_i^(d_1)(u) N_i^(d_2)(v) N_i^(d_3)(w))
) / (
  sum_(i=1)^K (h_i s_i N_i^(d_1)(u) N_i^(d_2)(v) N_i^(d_3)(w))
)
++++

//prior representation::image::RationalLRvol.gif[]

where (in both equations)

[%key]
stem:[P_{i}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.control_points_list,control points>>,
stem:[N_{i}^{d1}(u)]:: <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.u_b_splines,u_b_splines[i]>>,
stem:[N_{i}^{d2}(v)]:: <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.u_b_splines,v_b_splines[i]>>,
stem:[N_{i}^{d3}(w)]:: <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.u_b_splines,w_b_splines[i]>>,
stem:[s_{i}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_curve.scaling_factors,scaling factors>>,
stem:[d_{1}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.degree,u degree>>,
stem:[d_{2}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.degree,v degree>>,
stem:[d_{3}]:: <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_curve.degree,w degree>>,
stem:[K]:: number of control points,
stem:[h_{i}]:: weights_data[i];

The weights, in the case of the rational subtype, are given in the same sequence as the coefficients. The knot values and multiplicities are defined in the entity <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>>. The B-splines are defined over a domain described by knot vectors in the parameter directions of the volume. However, in contrast to B-bspline volumes, the basis functions or B-splines do not need to adapt to all knot lines in their supports. This facilitates the definition of a spline volume on a box partition.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.__note"
A locally refined spline volume is rational if and only if the weights are not all identical; this can be represented by the <<express:geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_volume,rational_locally_refined_spline_volume>> subtype. If it is polynomial, the weights may be defaulted to all being 1.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.__note"
Scaling factors are necessary to ensure that the non-zero B-spline basis functions add up to unity (partition of unity) at all points in the domain of <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.semi_standard_t_spline,semi_standard_t_spline>> and <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.lr_b_spline,lr_b_spline>> definitions. For <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.analysis_suitable_t_spline,analysis_suitable_t_spline>>, <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.standard_t_spline,standard_t_spline>>, and <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.hierarchical_b_spline,hierarchical_b_spline>>, the scaling factor is always equal to 1.0. The collection of B-spline basis functions for <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.analysis_suitable_t_spline,analysis_suitable_t_spline>> and <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.standard_t_spline,standard_t_spline>> definitions form a partition of unity, while for <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_type_enum.hierarchical_b_spline,hierarchical_b_spline>> there is no partition of unity achieved.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.__note"
The degrees are associated with the <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>>s and do not need to be constant throughout the volume.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.__note"
When the polynomial degree is the same for all B-splines and no knot lines exist that do not cross the entire parameter domain, the locally_refined_spline_volume will coincide with <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots,b_spline_volume_with_knots>>. In that case <<express:geometry_schema.b_spline_volume_with_knots,b_spline_volume_with_knots>> has the simplest and most compact representation.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.__note"
A *locally_refined_spline_volume* can be of type analysis suitable T-spline, hierarchical B-spline, LR-spline, semi standard T-spline or standard T-spline. The type is given by the enumeration locally_refined_spline_type_enum, and the packing and unpacking of the volume will depend on this type.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.u_b_splines"
the <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>> functions in the first (stem:[u]) parameter direction from which the volume is defined. These functions refer to the **u_knots** list for their knot parameter values.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.v_b_splines"
the <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>> functions in the second (stem:[v]) parameter direction from which the volume is defined. These functions refer to the **v_knots** list for their knot parameter values.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.w_b_splines"
the <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>> functions in the thirdd (stem:[w]) parameter direction from which the volume is defined. These functions refer to the **w_knots** list for their knot parameter values.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.w_b_splines.__note"
There may be some repetitions in the 3 lists of local_b_splines.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.u_knots"
the list of knot values for parameter stem:[u];
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.v_knots"
the list of knot values for parameter stem:[v];
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.w_knots"
the list of knot values for parameter stem:[w];
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.control_points_list"
the list of control points. The **control_points_list** and consequently also the **u_b_splines**, the **v_b_splines**, the **w_b_splines** and **scaling_factors**, are ordered according to the knot values of the the combined B-spline coefficient constructed by multiplying corresponding univariate B-splines, starting from the lowest knot value for each parameter. The knots in the w-parameter are the major sorting factor followed by knots in the v direction and finally the u direction. In case of ambiguities, the lists are sorted according to the maximum knot values of the combined B-splines and the multiplicities of the first knots of the univariate B-splines.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.scaling_factors"
the list of scaling factors used to ensure that the non-zero B-spline basis functions add up to unity (partition of unity) at all points in the domain. Scaling factors not equal to 1.0 are sometimes needed for semi_standard_t_spline and lr_b_spline definitions to ensure that the collection of B-spline basis functions form a partition of unity. For analysis_suitable_t_spline and standard_t_spline definitions, partition of unity is achieved without use of scaling factors (i.e., the scaling factor is always equal to 1.0). For hierarchical_b_spline definitions, a scaling factor equal to 1.0 is used, although there is no partition of unity achieved.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.linearly_independent"
indicates whether this property has been tested for and in that case the result of the test; this is for information only;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.linearly_independent.__note"
The test is applied to the set of products of <<express:geometry_schema.local_b_spline,local_b_spline>>s used as coefficients of the control points . Not all types of locally refined volumes are linearly independent by default.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.locally_refined_spline_type"
the type of the initial locally refined spline volume that provides rules for how the stored volume can be unpacked to its original format;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.domain"
the parameter domain corresponding to the volume. domain(1),(1) and domain (1),(2) give the minimum and maximum parameter values in the stem:[u] direction; domain(2),(1) and domain (2),(2) give the minimum and maximum parameter values in the stem:[v] direction; domain(3),(1) and domain (3),(2) give the minimum and maximum parameter values in the stem:[w] direction. If all B-splines at the boundary have a knot multiplicity equal to the degree+1 at this boundary, the domain will coincide with the domain given by the minimum and maximum values in **u_knots**, **v_knots** and **w_knots**. Otherwise, it will be coincide with a domain given by nearby knots depending on knot multiplicities.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.wr:WR1"
the number of **u_b_splines** shall be equal to the number of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.wr:WR2"
the number of **v_b_splines** shall be equal to the number of control points
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.wr:WR3"
the number of **w_b_splines** shall be equal to the number of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.wr:WR4"
the number of **scaling_factors** shall be equal to the number of control points;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume.wr:WR5"
all **scaling_factors** shall be positive and not greater than 1.0.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_volume"
A **rational_locally_refined_spline_volume** is a type of <<express:geometry_schema.locally_refined_spline_volume,locally_refined_spline_volume>> that is a piecewise parametric rational volume described in terms of control points and local B-spline functions.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_volume.weights_data"
the supplied values of the weights.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_volume.wr:WR1"
the size of the of **weights_data** list shall be equal to the number of control points
*)


(*"geometry_schema.rational_locally_refined_spline_volume.wr:WR2"
all values in the **weights_data** list shall be positive.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.compatible_dimension"
All <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>>s are geometrically founded in one or more <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> coordinate spaces.

The rule **compatible_dimension** ensures that when <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>>s are geometrically founded together in a coordinate space, they have the same coordinate space <<express:geometry_schema.dimension_count,dimension_count>> by ensuring that each matches the <<express:geometry_schema.dimension_count,dimension_count>> of the coordinate space in which it is geometrically founded. The rule returns TRUE when there are no <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>>, no <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>, and no <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.compatible_dimension.__note"
Two-dimensional <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>>s that are geometrically founded in a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> are only geometrically founded in context with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context.coordinate_space_dimension,coordinate_space_dimension>> of 2. All <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>>s founded in such a context are two-dimensional. All other values of <<express:geometry_schema.dimension_count,dimension_count>> behave similarly.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.compatible_dimension.wr:WR1"
There shall be no <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> that has a number of coordinates that differs from the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context.coordinate_space_dimension,coordinate_space_dimension>> of the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>s in which it is geometrically founded; there shall be no <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> that has a number of <<express:geometry_schema.direction.direction_ratios,direction_ratios>> that differs from the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context.coordinate_space_dimension,coordinate_space_dimension>> of the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>s in which it is geometrically founded. These properties are verifieded by the function <<express:geometry_schema.check_geometric_dimension,check_geometric_dimension>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.compatible_dimension.wr:WR1.__note"
A check of only <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>>s and <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s is sufficient for all <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>>s because:



All <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>>s appear in trees of <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>>s descending from the <<express:representation_schema.representation.items,items>> attribute of entity <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>>. See WR1 of entity <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>> in ISO 10303-43.



Each <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> gains its position and orientation information only by being, or referring to, a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>> or <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> entity in such a tree. In many cases this reference is made via an <<express:geometry_schema.placement,placement>>.



No other use of any <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> is allowed that would associate it with a coordinate space or otherwise assign a <<express:geometry_schema.dimension_count,dimension_count>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.dimension_of"
The **dimension_of** function returns the dimensionality of the input <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>>. If the item is a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>>, <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>, or <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>>, the dimensionality is obtained directly by counting components. For all other other subtypes the dimensionality is the integer <<express:geometry_schema.dimension_count,dimension_count>> of a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>> in which the input <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> is geometrically founded. By virtue of the constraints in global rule <<express:geometry_schema.compatible_dimension,compatible_dimension>>, this value is the <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item.dim,dim>> of the input <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dimension_of.item"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_item,geometric_representation_item>> for which the <<express:geometry_schema.dimension_count,dimension_count>> is determined.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.acyclic_curve_replica"
The **acyclic_curve_replica** boolean function is a recursive function which determines whether, or not, a given <<express:geometry_schema.curve_replica,curve_replica>> participates in its own definition. The function returns FALSE if the <<express:geometry_schema.curve_replica,curve_replica>> refers to itself, directly or indirectly, in its own definition.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.acyclic_curve_replica.rep"
(input) The <<express:geometry_schema.curve_replica,curve_replica>> which is to be tested for a cyclic reference.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.acyclic_curve_replica.parent"
a <<express:geometry_schema.curve,curve>> used in the definition of the replica.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.acyclic_point_replica"
The **acyclic_point_replica** boolean function is a recursive function which determines whether, or not, a given <<express:geometry_schema.point_replica,point_replica>> participates in its own definition. The function returns FALSE if the <<express:geometry_schema.point_replica,point_replica>> refers to itself, directly or indirectly, in its own definition.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.acyclic_point_replica.rep"
(input) the <<express:geometry_schema.point_replica,point_replica>> which is to be tested for acyclic reference;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.acyclic_point_replica.parent"
(input) the <<express:geometry_schema.point,point>> used to define the replica.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.acyclic_surface_replica"
The **acyclic_surface_replica** boolean function is a recursive function which determines whether, or not, a given <<express:geometry_schema.surface_replica,surface_replica>> participates in its own definition. The function returns FALSE if the <<express:geometry_schema.surface_replica,surface_replica>> refers to itself, directly or indirectly, in its own definition.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.acyclic_surface_replica.rep"
(input) The <<express:geometry_schema.surface_replica,surface_replica>> which is to be tested for acyclic reference.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.acyclic_surface_replica.parent"
(input) A <<express:geometry_schema.surface,surface>> used in the definition of the replica.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.associated_surface"
The **associated_surface** function determines the unique surface which is associated with the <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve_or_surface,pcurve_or_surface>> type. It is required by the propositions which apply to surface curve and its subtypes.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.associated_surface.arg"
(input) The <<express:geometry_schema.pcurve_or_surface,pcurve_or_surface>> for which the determination of the associated parent surface is required.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.associated_surface.surf"
(output) The parent surface associated with **arg**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.base_axis"
The **base_axis** function returns normalised orthogonal directions, **u[1], u[2]** and, if appropriate, **u[3]**.

In the three-dimensional case, with complete input data, **u[3]** is in the direction of **axis3, u[1]** is in the direction of the projection of **axis1** onto the plane normal to **u[3]**, and **u[2]** is orthogonal to both **u[1]** and **u[3]**, taking the same sense as **axis2**.

In the two-dimensional case **u[1]** is in the direction of **axis1** and **u[2]** is perpendicular to this, taking its sense from **axis2**.

For incomplete input data appropriate default values are derived.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.base_axis.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that they are used in a *representation* with a *geometric_representation_context*.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.base_axis.dim"
(input) The integer value of the dimensionality of the space in which the normalised orthogonal directions are required.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.base_axis.axis1"
(input) A direction used as a first approximation to the direction of output axis **u[1]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.base_axis.axis2"
(input) A direction used to determine the sense of **u[2]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.base_axis.axis3"
(input) The direction of **u[3]** in the case **dim** = 3, or indeterminate in the case **dim** = 2.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.base_axis.u"
(output) A list of **dim** (i.e., 2 or 3) mutually perpendicular directions.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.build_2axes"
The **build_2axes** function returns two normalised orthogonal directions. **u[1]** is in the direction of

**ref_direction** and **u[2]** is perpendicular to **u[1]**.

A default value of (1.0, 0.0) is supplied for **ref_direction** if the input data is incomplete.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.build_2axes.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the **direction**s returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that they are used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.build_2axes.ref_direction"
(input) A reference <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> in 2 dimensional space, this may be defaulted to (1.0, 0.0).
*)


(*"geometry_schema.build_2axes.u"
(output) A list of 2 mutually perpendicular <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s, **u[1]** is parallel to **ref_direction**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.build_axes"
The **build_axes** function returns three normalised orthogonal <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s. **u[3]** is in the direction of **axis**, **u[1]** is in the direction of the projection of **ref_direction** onto the plane normal to **u[3]**, and **u[2]** is the cross product of **u[3]** and **u[1]**. Default values are supplied if input data is incomplete.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.build_axes.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that they are used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.build_axes.axis"
(input) The intended <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> of **u[3]**, this may be defaulted to (0.0, 0.0, 1.0).
*)


(*"geometry_schema.build_axes.ref_direction"
(input) A <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> in a direction used to compute **u[1]**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.build_axes.u"
(output) A list of 3 mutually orthogonal <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s in 3D space. defaulted to (0.0, 0.0, 1.0).
*)


(*"geometry_schema.check_geometric_dimension"
The **check_geometric_dimension** function checks that whenever a <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>>.is used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> the number of coordinates is equal to the dimensionality of the corresponding <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>. The function also checkes that whenever a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> is used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> the number of direction ratios is equal to the dimensionality of the corresponding <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.

The function first determines whether there are mixed dimensions in the contexts, if not a simple check of the numbers of coordinates or direction ratios is sufficient. In other cases each point or direction is checked against the dimensionalites of all the contexts in which it is used. The value FALE is returned if any mis-match of dimensions is detected.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.check_geometric_dimension.capt"
(input) A set of <<express:geometry_schema.cartesian_point,cartesian_point>>s to be checked against context dimensionalities.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.check_geometric_dimension.dir"
(input) A set of <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s to be checked against context dimensionalities.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.check_geometric_dimension.grc"
(input) The set of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>s in which the points or directions might be used.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.geometric_dimensionalities_in_contexts"
The **geometric_dimensionalities_in_contexts** function checks the different values of dimensionality which are found in a set of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>s. If all contexts have the same <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context.coordinate_space_dimension,coordinate_space_dimension>> then the integer value 1, 2, or 3 is returned depending upon the shared value for <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context.coordinate_space_dimension,coordinate_space_dimension>>. If mixed values are found the ineger 0 is returned.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.geometric_dimensionalities_in_contexts.grcs"
(input) The set of <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>s to be checked for mixed dimensionality.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.orthogonal_complement"
The **orthogonal_complement** function returns a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> which is the orthogonal complement of the input <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>. The input <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> shall be a two-dimensional <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> and the result is two dimensional and perpendicular to the input <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.orthogonal_complement.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that it is used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.orthogonal_complement.vec"
(input) A <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> in 2D space.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.orthogonal_complement.result"
(output) A <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> orthogonal to **vec**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.first_proj_axis"
The **first_proj_axis** function produces a 3-dimensional <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> which is, with fully defined input, the projection of **arg** onto the plane normal to the **z_axis**. With **arg** defaulted the result is the projection of [1,0,0] onto this plane; except that, if **z_axis** = [1,0,0], or, **z_axis** = [-1,0,0], [0,1,0] is the default for **arg**. A violation occurs if **arg** is in the same direction as the input **z_axis**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.first_proj_axis.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that it is used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.first_proj_axis.z_axis"
(input) A <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> defining a local Z coordinate axis.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.first_proj_axis.arg"
(input) A <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> not parallel to **z_axis**..
*)


(*"geometry_schema.first_proj_axis.arg"
(output) A <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> which is in the direction of the projection of **arg** onto the plane with normal **z_axis**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.second_proj_axis"
The **second_proj_axis** function returns the normalised <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> that is simultaneously the projection of **arg** onto the plane normal to the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> **z_axis** and onto the plane normal to the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> **x_axis**. If **arg** is NULL, the projection of the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> (0, 1, 0) onto **z_axis** is returned.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.second_proj_axis.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that it is used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.second_proj_axis.z_axis"
(input) a defining a local Z axis.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.second_proj_axis.x_axis"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> not parallel to **z_axis**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.second_proj_axis.arg"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> which is used as the first approximation to the direction of **y_axis**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.second_proj_axis.y_axis"
(output) a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> determined by first projecting **arg** onto the plane with normal **z_axis**, then projecting the result onto the plane normal to **x_axis**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cross_product"
The **cross_product** function returns the vector, or cross, product of two input <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s. The input <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s must be three-dimensional and are normalised at the start of the computation. The result is always a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> which is unitless. If the input <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s are either parallel or anti-parallel, a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> of zero magnitude is returned with <<express:geometry_schema.vector.orientation,orientation>> as **arg1**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cross_product.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that it is used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.cross_product.arg1"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> defining the first operand in cross product operation.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cross_product.arg2"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> defining the second operand for cross product.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.cross_product.result"
(output) a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> which is the cross product of **arg1** and **arg2**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dot_product"
The **dot_product** function returns the scalar, or dot (stem:[cdot]), product of two <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s. The input arguments can be <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s in either two- or three-dimensional space and are normalised at the start of the computation. The returned scalar is undefined if the input <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s have different dimensionality, or if either is undefined.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dot_product.arg1"
(input)a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> defining first vector in dot product, or scalar product, operation..
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dot_product.arg2"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> defining second operand for dot product operation.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dot_product.scalar"
(output) a scalar which is the dot product of **arg1** and **arg2**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.normalise"
The **normalise** function returns a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> or <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> whose components are normalised to have a sum of squares of 1.0. The output is of the same type (<<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> or <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>>, with the same units) as the input argument. If the input argument is not defined or is of zero length, the output vector is undefined.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.normalise.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that it is used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.normalise.arg"
(input)a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> or <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> to be normalised.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.normalise.result"
(output) a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> or <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> which is parallel to **arg**, of unit length and of the same type.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.scalar_times_vector"
The **scalar_times_vector** function returns the <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> that is the scalar multiple of the input vector. It accepts as input a scalar and a `vector' which may be either a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> or a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>. The output is a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> of the same units as the input <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>>, or unitless if a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> is input. If either input argument is undefined, the returned <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> is also undefined. The <<express:geometry_schema.vector.orientation,orientation>> of the <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> is reversed if the scalar is negative.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.scalar_times_vector.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that it is used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>.
*)

(*"geometry_schema.scalar_times_vector.scalar"
(input) a real number to participate in the product.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.scalar_times_vector.vec"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> or <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> which is to be multiplied.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.dimension_of.result"
(output) a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> which is the product of **scalar** and **vec**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector_sum"
The **vector_sum** function returns the sum of the input arguments. The function returns as a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> the vector sum of the two input `vectors'. For this purpose <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s are treated as unit vectors. The input arguments must both be of the same dimensionality but may be either <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s or <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> s. Where both arguments are <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>>s, they must be expressed in the same units. A zero sum vector produces a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> of zero magnitude in the direction of **arg1**. If both input arguments are <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s, the result is unitless.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector_sum.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that it is used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>
*)

(*"geometry_schema.vector_sum.arg1"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> or <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> defining the first operand in vector sum operation..
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector_sum.arg2"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> or <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>> defining the second operand for vector sum operation.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector_sum.result"
(output) a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> which is the vector sum of **arg1** and **arg2**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector_difference"
The **vector_difference** function returns the difference of the input arguments as ({blank} **arg1** - **arg2** {blank}). The function returns as a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> the vector difference of the two input `vectors'. For this purpose <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s are treated as unit vectors. The input arguments shall both be of the same dimensionality but may be either <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s or <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>>s. If both input arguments are <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>>s, they must be expressed in the same units; if both are <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s, a unitless result is produced. A zero difference vector produces a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> of zero magnitude in the direction of **arg1**.
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector_difference.__note"
This function does not provide geometric founding for the <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> returned, the caller of the the function is responsible for ensuring that it is used in a <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> with a <<express:geometry_schema.geometric_representation_context,geometric_representation_context>>
*)

(*"geometry_schema.vector_difference.arg1"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s or <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> defining first operand in the vector difference operation;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector_difference.arg2"
(input) a <<express:geometry_schema.direction,direction>>s or <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> defining the second operand for vector difference;
*)


(*"geometry_schema.vector_difference.result"
(output) a <<express:geometry_schema.vector,vector>> which is the vector difference of **arg1** and **arg2**.
*)


