==== Element coordinate systems

The ENTITY data types that are associated with *element coordinate systems* are:

* *aligned_axis_tolerance*
* *arbitrary_volume_3d_element_coordinate_system*
* *parametric_volume_3d_element_coordinate_system*
* *arbitrary_volume_2d_element_coordinate_system*
* *parametric_volume_2d_element_coordinate_system*
* *aligned_surface_3d_element_coordinate_system*
* *parametric_surface_3d_element_coordinate_system*
* *constant_surface_3d_element_coordinate_system*
* *aligned_surface_2d_element_coordinate_system*
* *parametric_surface_2d_element_coordinate_system*
* *aligned_curve_3d_element_coordinate_system*
* *parametric_curve_3d_element_coordinate_system*
* *parametric_curve_3d_element_coordinate_direction*
* *curve_2d_element_coordinate_system*
* *directionally_explicit_element_coordinate_system_arbitrary*
* *directionally_explicit_element_coordinate_system_aligned*
* *euler_angles*

The coordinate systems that are used for element information fall into three different categories:

* an arbitrary placement coordinate system that is defined without reference to the geometry of the element. Such a system can be used to orient property information associated with a volume 3D element.

* an aligned orthogonal system in which the orientation of the axes for a surface or curve element coordinate system at each point in the element is partly defined by the element geometry;

* a parametric coordinate system whereby each point within an element is derived from the orientation of the parametric axes for the element at that point. The parametric axes are not necessarily orthogonal. Such a system can be used when a curved surface of orthotropic or anisotropic material is modelled by volume or surface 3D elements.

If a non-cartesian coordinate system is specified for an element then information at a point within an element is defined with respect to the local orthogonal cartesian coordinate system whose axes are coincident to the specified system at that point. An aligned system at a point is also derived from an orthogonal cartesian coordinate system whose axes are coincident to the specified coordinate system and the element geometry at that point.

Element information may be defined with respect to an orthogonal system of coordinate axes derived from the parametric coordinate system of the element. A parametric coordinate system orientation is established graphically for each element type along with the element node sequence in the figures in <<sub-eltopo>>. In this clause only two axes for each orthogonal coordinate system are specified. The third axis is then chosen according to right hand rule to complete the triad. The axes of an orthogonal element coordinate system are denoted (stem:[x, y, z]). The orthogonal system may be an intermediate orthogonal system, with the element orthogonal system related to the intermediate orthogonal coordinate system by one angle (for surface elements) or three angles (for volume elements).

Finite element information is defined with respect to an orthogonal coordinate system that shall obey the following rules:

* for a surface element, the stem:[z] axis of the coordinate system is defined to remain normal to the element surface. Hence for a non-planar (curved) element the orientation of the coordinate system varies over the element surface. The stem:[z] axis of a surface element is normal to the surface in both 2D and 3D analyses.

* for a curve element, the stem:[x] axis of the coordinate system is defined to be tangential to the curve. Hence for a curved element the orientation of the coordinate system varies along the curve.

==== Element matrix integration

The ENTITY data types that are associated with *element matrix integration* are:

* *volume_3d_element_integrated_matrix*
* *volume_3d_element_integrated_matrix_with_definition*
* *volume_3d_element_field_integration*
* *element_integration_algebraic*
* *volume_3d_element_field_integration_rule*
* *volume_3d_element_field_integration_explicit*
* *volume_position_weight*
* *volume_2d_element_integrated_matrix*
* *volume_2d_element_integrated_matrix_with_definition*
* *volume_2d_element_field_integration*
* *volume_2d_element_field_integration_rule*
* *volume_2d_element_field_integration_explicit*
* *surface_3d_element_integrated_matrix*
* *surface_3d_element_integrated_matrix_with_definition*
* *surface_3d_element_integration*
* *surface_3d_element_field_integration*
* *surface_section_integration*
* *surface_3d_element_field_integration_rule*
* *surface_3d_element_field_integration_explicit*
* *surface_position_weight*
* *surface_section_integration_rule*
* *surface_section_integration_explicit*
* *surface_section_position_weight*
* *surface_2d_element_integrated_matrix*
* *surface_2d_element_integrated_matrix_with_definition*
* *surface_2d_element_integration*
* *surface_2d_element_length_integration*
* *surface_2d_element_length_integration_rule*
* *surface_2d_element_length_integration_explicit*
* *curve_3d_element_integrated_matrix*
* *curve_3d_element_integrated_matrix_with_definition*
* *curve_3d_element_integration*
* *curve_3d_element_length_integration*
* *curve_3d_element_length_integration_rule*
* *curve_3d_element_length_integration_explicit*
* *curve_3d_element_position_weight*
* *curve_section_integration_explicit*
* *curve_2d_element_integrated_matrix*
* *curve_2d_element_integrated_matrix_with_definition*
* *curve_2d_element_integration*

A finite element formulation is typically composed of many different matrices, including stiffness, mass, and others. For some of the more complex elements, the stiffness matrix may be partitioned into several matrices. Each of these matrices typically needs to be integrated, mostly over the element area or volume. Many of these matrices are mathematically complex that explicit integration is not feasible, and, therefore, numerical integration techniques are used. For each matrix being integrated, the element integrated matrix definition can specify the interpolation rule for the quantity within the element, and the method of integration of the quantity. An element may have different interpolation rules for different matrices. Diagonal (lumped) matrices are often used for element mass and damping. Such matrices are not treated as a special case, but are specified by numerical integration points at the nodes of an element.

As described in <<sub-eltopo>>, a volume, surface or curve element has shape functions for geometric interpolation. The definitions of these shape functions depend on the element type, and in some element formulations, the actual element node coordinates and normals. The element descriptor entity for the particular element type specifies the element shape (such as triangle or quadrilateral), the element order, and a text description that can be used to make the element geometric shape functions definitions precise. All locations within an element are specified using the parametric axes system derived from the element shape functions for geometric interpolation. However, the interpolation of other quantities within an element need not use the same shape functions. Each quantity may have a different interpolation rule that is defined by the shape functions and the element geometry. The element shape function specifies the interpolation order for the quantity and a text description that can be used to make the shape function definitions for the quantity precise.

NOTE: For volume, surface, and curve elements it is possible to define a explicit numerical integration by an arbitrary selection of integration points and weights within the volume of the element. This is shown in <<fig-volume-element-integration-points>> for volume elements.

[[fig-volume-element-integration-points]]
.Volume element integration points
image::images/fig-50.svg[]

However for surface and curve elements it is more common to define field and section integration methods separately where *field integration* is integration over the surface or along the length of an element, and *section integration* is the integration through the thickness of a surface element or over the cross section of a curve element. The separate field and section integration definitions are used even when each point of a numerical field uses the same integration method. For linear analyses, section integrations are usually carried out algebraically; thus only one integration point would be defined through the section.

NOTE: <<fig-surface-element-integration-points>> illustrates the location of integration points in the field of a surface element and through the section (thickness) of the element. The section positions are with respect to the entire section depth, which is the sum of all the plies in a layered material. A series of extent searches needs to be made to correlate integration points with a given ply. See the Fundamental Concepts and Assumptions subclause of this clause for discussion on why this modelling method was selected.

[[fig-surface-element-integration-points]]
.Surface element integration points
image::images/fig-51.svg[]

There are three choices of defining the location of an integration point within a volume element.

NOTE: The EXPRESS-G Partial Model shown in <<fig-volume-element-integration-express-g-partial-model>> is presented as an aid in visualising the integration options for volume elements.

The algebraic integration option does not have integration points as this option defines that the integration is done exactly. The rule option defines that an integration rule and integration order is used to integrate the matrix. The explicit option is the most general numerical integration option where the integration positions and weights are explicitly defined. This option is included to avoid having to catalogue all the many types of integration rules. The explicit integration option will form the foundation for the description of analysis output points when that portion of this standard is complete.

[example]
Integration rule includes Gaussian integration and Simpson's rule.

[[fig-volume-element-integration-express-g-partial-model]]
.Volume element integration EXPRESS-G partial model
image::images/fig-52.svg[]

The way in which the location of an integration point is defined within a surface or curve element depends on whether surface-section or surface-field integration is being used.

NOTE: The curve and surface element integration EXPRESS-G Partial Model is shown in <<fig-surface-and-curve-element-integration-partial-model>> to aid in visualising the integration options available.

[[fig-surface-and-curve-element-integration-partial-model]]
.Surface and curve element integration EXPRESS-G partial model
image::images/fig-53.svg[]

For surface-section integration there are no integration points within an element as the integration for both surface and section is carried out exactly.

For surface-field integration the location of an integration point within an element is defined by both a field location specified using the element parametric coordinate system and a section location with respect to the element coordinate system. In a surface 3D element a field location has two parametric coordinates, a surface 2D element or curve 3D element has a single parametric coordinate, and a field location is undefined for a 2D curve element. The field integration can be carried out by either algebraic (exact integration), rule or explicit integration options. The definition of these options is identical to that presented in the discussion on volume elements above.

In a surface element a section location is defined by the distance from the reference plane of the element in the direction of the stem:[z] axis of the surface element property coordinate system. In a curve element a section location is defined by the distances from the reference axis of the element in the y and z directions of the curve element property coordinate system. A section location is defined using physical distances, not unit distances. The field and section axes when used in combination to define a location within a surface or curve element do not necessarily form a right hand triad.

The following paragraphs are included as a reference for the theoretical basis of the integration referenced in this standard.

The numerical integration of a quantity within the volume of an element requires the evaluation of that quantity at one or more points within the element. The integral is a weighted sum of these values. The location of the integration points and the weighting assigned to them are defined either by a numerical integration rule (Gaussian or Simpson's rule) or by an explicit list of locations and weights.

The integration rules specify points within a unit space, usually within the range of -1.0 to 1.0. If section integration by rule is specified then these unit distances are converted to physical distances separately for each field integration point. Hence section integration by rule can only be specified for a surface with a defined thickness.

The form of the summation and the weighting depend of the type of the element. The specific forms for volume, surface and curve elements follow:

===== Volume 3D Element

For a volume 3D element, the integral quantity can be obtained numerically by a summation of the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
Q =  \sum_{i=1}^n \: j_i \: w_i \:  q_i
++++

where:

stem:[n]:: is the number of points within the volume;

stem:[q_i]:: is the value of the quantity at point i;

stem:[w_i]:: is the weighting for the evaluation at point i;

stem:[j_i]:: is the determinant of the Jacobian matrix at point i.

This form of integration may also be used for a surface 3D element or curve 3D element, however it is more usual to use separate field and section integrations for these elements.

===== Volume 2D Element

For a volume 2D element, the integral quantity can be obtained numerically by a summation of the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
Q =  \sum_{i=1}^n \: s_i \: j_i \: w_i \: q_i
++++

where:

stem:[n]:: is the number of points within the volume;

stem:[q_i]:: is the value of the quantity at point i;

stem:[w_i]:: is the weighting for the evaluation at point i;

stem:[j_i]:: is the determinant of the Jacobian matrix at point i;

stem:[s_i]:: is either the depth of section for a plane stress or strain element or the distance from the axis of symmetry for an axisymmetric
   element, of point i.

This form of integration may also be used for a surface 2D element, however it is more usual to use separate field and section integrations for these elements.

===== Curve 2D Element

For a curve 2D element, the integral quantity can be obtained numerically by a summation of the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
Q =  \sum_{i=1}^n \: s_i \: w_i \: q_i
++++

where:

stem:[n]:: is the number of points within the volume;

stem:[q_i]:: is the value of the quantity at point i;

stem:[w_i]:: is the weighting for the evaluation at point i;

stem:[s_i]:: is either the depth of section for a plane stress or strain element or the distance from the axis of symmetry for an axisymmetric element, of point i.

===== Surface 3D or Curve Element

For a surface 3D or curve element with separate integration the integral quantity can be obtained numerically by a summation of the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
Q =  \sum_{i=1}^n \:  j_i \: w^f_i \: Q^s_i
++++

where stem:[Q_i^s] is the integral of the quantity through the section at field point i, which can be obtained numerically by a summation of the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
Q^s_i =  \sum_{j=1}^m \:  w^s_j \: q_{ji}
++++

where:

stem:[n]:: is the number of points over the field;

stem:[m]:: is the number of points through the section;

stem:[q_{ji}]:: is the value of the quantity at section point j and field point i;

stem:[w_i^f]:: is the weighting for the evaluation of the field integration at point i;

stem:[w_j^s]:: is the weighting for the evaluation of the section integration at point j;

stem:[j_i]:: is the determinant of the Jacobian matrix at field point i.

===== Surface 2D Element

For a surface 2D element with separate integration the integral quantity can be obtained numerically by a summation of the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
Q =  \sum_{i=1}^n \:  j_i \: w^f_i \: Q^s_i
++++

where stem:[Q_i^s] is the integral of the quantity through the section at field point i, which can be obtained numerically by a summation of the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
Q^s_i =  \sum_{j=1}^m \:  s_{ji} \: w^s_j \: q_{ji}
++++

where:

stem:[n]:: is the number of points over the field;

stem:[m]:: is the number of points through the section;

stem:[q_{ji}]:: is the value of the quantity at section point j and field point i;

stem:[s_{ji}]:: is either the depth of section for a plane stress or strain element or the distance from the axis of symmetry for an axisymmetric element, of section point j at field point i;

stem:[w_i^f]:: is the weighting for the evaluation of the field integration at point i;

stem:[w_j^s]:: is the weighting for the evaluation of the section integration at point j;

stem:[j_i]:: is the determinant of the Jacobian matrix at field point i.

==== Element locations

The ENTITY data types that are associated with *element locations* are:

* *fea_parametric_point*
* *volume_element_location*
* *surface_volume_element_location*
* *surface_element_location*
* *surface_section_element_location*
* *surface_section_element_location_absolute*
* *surface_section_element_location_dimensionless*
* *curve_volume_element_location*
* *curve_element_location*
* *curve_section_element_location*

A field variable may be evaluated for a list of locations within an element. This list of locations is specified by reference to the entity within this section, which is appropriate to the element type.

==== Finite element analysis material properties

The ENTITY data types that are associated with *finite element analysis material properties* are:

* *element_material*
* *fea_material_property_geometric_relationship*
* *fea_material_property_representation*
* *fea_material_property_representation_item*
* *fea_linear_elasticity*
* *fea_mass_density*
* *fea_area_density*
* *fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion*
* *fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion*
* *fea_moisture_absorption*
* *fea_shell_membrane_stiffness*
* *fea_shell_bending_stiffness*
* *fea_shell_membrane_bending_coupling_stiffness*
* *fea_shell_shear_stiffness*

The material specifications and properties for an element are referenced through a set of *fea_material_property_representation* entities. The material specifications are inherited from entities associated with a *material_property_representation*, which in turn may have an *fea_material_property_representation* subtype.

NOTE: See <<fig-relationship-between-fea-and-iso-10303-45>> for a perspective on how the *fea_material_property_representation* entity associates finite element analysis model information with entities from <<iso10303-45>>.

[example]
Each *fea_material_property_representation* is defined with respect to a *data_environment* entity that is specified by a reference from an instantiation of a *material_property_representation* and the associated material product *representation* related entities. The environment variables (such as temperature, moisture content) are associated to the *material_property_representation* by the set of *property_definition_representation* entities referenced by the *data_environment* entity. There is a single FEA material property representation item for each material property and associated environment variables per where rule WR1 in the *fea_material_property_representation* entity. An *element_material* aggregates together *material_property_representation* entities to represent all the material properties necessary to define an element.

[[fig-relationship-between-fea-and-iso-10303-45]]
.Relationship between FEA and <<iso10303-45>>
image::images/fig-54.svg[]

==== Element properties

The ENTITY data types that are associated with *element properties* are:

* *surface_element_property*
* *surface_section_field*
* *surface_section_field_constant*
* *surface_section_field_varying*
* *surface_section*
* *uniform_surface_section*
* *uniform_surface_section_layered*
* *fea_surface_section_geometric_relationship*
* *curve_3d_element_property*
* *curve_element_interval*
* *curve_element_interval_constant*
* *curve_element_interval_linearly_varying*
* *curve_2d_element_property*
* *curve_element_section_definition*
* *curve_element_section_derived_definitions*
* *fea_curve_section_geometric_relationship*
* *curve_element_end_offset*
* *curve_element_end_release*
* *curve_element_end_release_packet*
* *axisymmetric_2d_element_property*
* *plane_2d_element_property*
* *simple_plane_2d_element_property*

Element properties are information associated with elements that are not derivable from the element connectivity and the associated node geometry.

Volume elements have no associated physical properties other than material properties, so no property entities exist for them. The volume elements refer directly to a material to supply all other needed property information.

===== Surface element properties

Surface elements are idealised volumes, and as such require other property values, such as thickness, to complete their definition.

A surface element can model a volume of material where the geometry of the volume is specified by a reference surface which is defined by the positions of the nodes, the element shape functions, and a thickness at each point on the surface. The thickness is often defined by nodal values which are then interpolated by the element shape functions.

NOTE: Other independent definitions of thickness over the surface of an element are possible, such as one described by bicubic polynomials. These independent definitions are not in this document at the present, though a subtyping has been constructed to allow inclusion at a later date with minimum impact on the current information model.

Alternatively a surface may be a composite or fabricated shell for which the geometric and section properties may be separated in a simple way. A full description of the material and section shall be contained in a referencing application protocol. In this case an aggregate section thickness is not required within a finite element analysis model, and is not supplied.

The material model may contain constitutive matrices to describe directly the aggregate behaviour of a composite or fabricated surface.

It is also possible to specify constitutive matrices for a non-homogeneous surface within the finite element model. This is done by supplying a separate effective thickness and effective material for each of the membrane, bending, shear, and membrane-coupling matrices. A precise definition for these constitutive matrices and for the different effective thicknesses and effective materials is given in the paragraphs below.

The engineering theory for the thermo-mechanical behaviour of a shell defines the kinematics of deformation within the entire volume of the surface element with respect to variables defined with respect to the reference surface of an element.   Classical thin shell theory assumes that normals remain straight and normal to the reference surface. The principal underlying the finite element method - the variational principal of minimum potential energy for the displacement formulation elements - is not affected by such kinematic assumptions. However the kinematic assumptions allow strains and stresses to be generalised to give quantities such as curvatures, and bending and twisting moments.

For an analysis of a surface that assumes linear material behaviour it is possible to consider separately the integration through the thickness and the integration over the surface. Consider the strain energy for a surface element is given by an integral of the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
V = \frac{1}{2} \: {\bf u}^t \:
            \int_{surface} \: \int_{thickness} \: B^t \: D \: B \: dt \: ds
               \; {\bf u}
++++

where:

stem:[bii u]:: represents the vector of nodal generalised displacements for the element;

stem:[B]:: represents the element shape function derivatives which relate the nodal generalised displacements to strains at any point within the element such that:
+
[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
\varepsilon = B \: {\bf u}
++++

stem:[D]:: represents the elasticity matrix for the material which relates the strains to the stresses at a point within the element such that:
+
[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
\sigma = D \: \varepsilon
++++

where:

stem:[sigma]:: is the stress tensor components in a vector form;

stem:[varepsilon]:: is the strain tensor components in a vector form.

Alternatively, for an analysis with linear material behaviour the integration through the thickness can be carried out algebraically so that the strain energy is given by an integral of the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
V = \frac{1}{2} \: {\bf u}^t \:
            \int_{surface} \: {B'}^t \: D' \: B' \: ds
               \; {\bf u}
++++

where:

stem:[B']:: represents the element shape function derivatives which relate the nodal generalised displacements to strains at any point within the element such that:
+
[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
\varepsilon' = B' \: {\bf u}
++++

stem:[D']:: represents the constitutive matrix for the surface which relates the generalised strains to generalised stresses at a point on the surface such that:
+
[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
\sigma' = D' \: \varepsilon'
++++

where:

stem:[sigma']:: is the vector of membrane force and bending moment components:
+
[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
\left( \begin{array}{c}
N_{xx}\\
N_{yy}\\
N_{xy}\\
M_{xx}\\
M_{yy}\\
M_{xy} \end{array} \right)
++++

within which:

stem:[N_{x x}], stem:[N_{y y}], stem:[N_{x y}]:: are components of membrane force;

stem:[M_{x x}], stem:[M_{y y}], stem:[M_{x y}]:: are components of bending moment.

stem:[varepsilon']:: is the vector of membrane strain and curvature components:
+
[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
\left( \begin{array}{c}
\varepsilon_{xx}\\
\varepsilon_{yy}\\
\gamma_{xy}\\
\chi_{xx}\\
\chi_{yy}\\
\tau_{xy}
        \end{array} \right)
++++

within which:

stem:[varepsilon_{x x}], stem:[varepsilon_{y y}], stem:[gamma_{x y}]:: are the components of membrane strain;

stem:[chi_{x x}], stem:[chi_{y y}], stem:[tau_{x y}]:: are the components of curvature.

NOTE: The curvature components stem:[chi_{x x}] and stem:[chi_{y y}] are positive when the center of curvature is on the stem:[+z] side. The sign convention is shown in <<fig-bending-moments>>.

[[fig-bending-moments]]
.Bending moments
image::images/fig-bendx.svg[]

At a point on the surface with thickness stem:[h] and offset stem:[p], the limits for section integration are (stem:[p - h/2] to stem:[p + h/2]). At this point the stem:[D'] matrix is defined with respect to the coordinate axes for the surface section properties by:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
D' =
\left( \begin{array}{cc}
      h \, D_m    &        h \, p \, D_m  \\
      h \, p \, D_m  &  {\displaystyle (\frac{1}{12} h^3 + h \, p^2) \, D_m  }
\end{array} \right)
++++

where:

stem:[D_m]:: is the membrane elasticity matrix, which for a isotropic material has the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
D_m =  \frac{E}{(1 - \nu^2)} \:
   \left( \begin{array}{ccc}
         1 & \nu & 0 \\
         \nu &  1  & 0  \\
         0 & 0 & {\displaystyle \frac{(1 - \nu)}{2} }
      \end{array}   \right)
++++

where:

stem:[E]:: is Young's modulus;

stem:[nu]:: is Poisson's ratio.

For some applications it may be desired to use different material and section properties to define the different sub-matrices of stem:[D']. When this is desired, the following form of stem:[D'] is obtained:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
D' =
\left( \begin{array} {cc}
      h_m \, D_{mm}    &        h_b \, p \, D_{mc}  \\
      h_b \, p \, D_{mc}  &  {\displaystyle
                              (\frac{1}{12} h_b^3 + h_b \, p^2) \, D_{mb}  }
\end{array} \right)
++++

where:

stem:[D_{mm}]:: is the membrane elasticity matrix;

stem:[D_{mb}]:: is the bending elasticity matrix;

stem:[D_{mc}]:: is the membrane - bending coupling matrix;

stem:[h_m]:: is the membrane thickness;

stem:[h_b]:: is the bending thickness.

For some applications it may be desirable to use separate material and section properties for shear behaviour. In this case an aggregate constitutive matrix can be formed to relate shear strain to shear force as follows:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
\left( \begin{array}{c}
      V_x  \\
      V_y  \end{array} \right)  =
          h_s \: D_s \: \left( \begin{array}{c}
                    \varepsilon_{xz}   \\
                    \varepsilon_{yz}    \end{array} \right)
++++

where:

stem:[V_x], stem:[V_y]:: are components of shear force;

stem:[varepsilon_{xz}], stem:[varepsilon_{yz}]:: are components of shear strain;

stem:[h_s]:: is the effective shear thickness for transverse shear;

stem:[D_s]:: is the elasticity matrix for shear.

For an isotropic material the elasticity matrix stem:[D_s] has the form:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
D_s = G \: \left( \begin{array}{cc}
         1 & 0 \\
         0 & 1    \end{array}  \right)
++++

where:

stem:[G]:: is the shear modulus.

NOTE: The relationships of the surface element property subtypes are shown in the EXPRESS-G Partial Model in <<fig-surface-element-properties-partial-model>>.

[[fig-surface-element-properties-partial-model]]
.Surface element properties EXPRESS-G partial model
image::images/fig-55.svg[]

===== Curve element properties

Curve elements, like surface elements, are idealised volumes, and as such require other property values, such as cross-sectional area, to complete their definition.

A curve element has a single parametric axis that is established graphically in <<sub-eltopo>>. The position of this axis is defined by the positions of the nodes of an element, the shape functions of an element, and any element end offsets.

The parametric axis runs between the end positions of the element.

NOTE: The end positions can be offset from the end nodes of an element as shown in <<fig-curve-element-end-offsets>>.

[[fig-curve-element-end-offsets]]
.Curve element end offsets
image::images/fig-56.svg[]

The parametric axis cannot be offset from the mid-span nodes of higher order curve elements.

Curve element section properties are defined with respect to the y and z axes of the property coordinate system of an element, whose origin is on the parametric axis of the element.

NOTE: Neither the section centroid or shear centre need to lie on the parametric axis of the element as shown in <<fig-curve-element-property-coord-system-origin-orientation>>.

[[fig-curve-element-property-coord-system-origin-orientation]]
.Curve element property coordinate system origin orientation
image::images/fig-57.svg[]

A curve 3D element can be divided into intervals that may have different material and property specifications. The intervals within a curve element shall be defined in a sequence from node 1 to node 2. For each interval except the last, the position of the interval end that is closest to end 2 of the element shall be specified. A parametric position of 1.0 is specified for the last interval to ensure that it ends at the end of the element. A position within a curve element is defined with respect to the parametric coordinate system for the element such that:

* position -1.0 is the end of the element closest to node 1;

* position 1.0 is the end of the element closest to node 2.

NOTE: An element divided into 2 equal length intervals is shown in <<fig-curve-element-interval-specification-conventions>>.

[[fig-curve-element-interval-specification-conventions]]
.Curve element interval specification conventions
image::images/fig-58.svg[]

The end fixings of a curve 3D element determine the way in which it is connected to its nodes. A fixing is described by an end offset and an end release, as described in the following two paragraphs.

The end of a curve element can be offset from its node so that a rigid body provides the link between the end of the element and the node. The offset is defined as the distance from the node to the end position of the element. This distance can be specified with respect to an arbitrary coordinate system or the property coordinate system at the end of the element. The orientation of the property coordinate system may itself depend on the offset.

One or more degrees of freedom can be released between a node and the end of the element. For each released degree of freedom, a connecting stiffness shall be specified (which may be zero). The released degrees of freedom may be specified with respect to an arbitrary coordinate system or the property coordinate system at the end of the element. The strain energy stem:[V] associated with the release is given by:

[latexmath%unnumbered]
++++
V = \frac{1}{2} \: k \: d^2
++++

where:

stem:[k]:: is the release stiffness;

stem:[d]:: is the difference between the displacement for the degree of freedom on either side of the release.

If a release and offset are specified at the same end of the element then the release acts between the node and the rigid body linking the end of the element to the node.

NOTE: A release and offset are both shown in <<fig-curve-element-end-release-and-end-offset>>.

[[fig-curve-element-end-release-and-end-offset]]
.Curve element end release and end offset
image::images/fig-59.svg[]

NOTE: The relationships of the surface element property subtypes are shown in the EXPRESS-G Partial Model in <<fig-curve-element-properties-express-g-partial-model>>.

[[fig-curve-element-properties-express-g-partial-model]]
.Curve element properties EXPRESS-G partial model
image::images/fig-60.svg[]

==== Groups

The ENTITY data types that are associated with *groups* are:

* *fea_group*
* *element_group*
* *node_group*
* *fea_group_relation*
* *volume_3d_element_group*
* *volume_2d_element_group*
* *surface_3d_element_group*
* *surface_2d_element_group*
* *curve_3d_element_group*
* *curve_2d_element_group*

A group is a set of elements, nodes or groups within the FEA information model. A group may be used to collect entities that have a common attribute such as colour.

==== Function definitions

These definitions are used to ensure the correctness of information in many of the finite element analysis model schema information model entities.

The FUNCTION data types that are associated with *function definitions* are:

* *required_0d_nodes*
* *required_1d_nodes*
* *required_2d_nodes*
* *required_3d_nodes*
* *number_of_terms*
* *valid_parametric_coordinate*
* *build_direction_node*
* *consistent_geometric_reference*
* *consistent_element_or_group_reference*
* *consistent_element_reference*

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axi_or_plane"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axi_or_plane,axi_or_plane>> specifies whether an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model_2d,fea_model_2d>> is axisymmetric or planar.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axi_or_plane.axisymmetric"

the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> is an axisymmetric analysis model where 2D element geometry is assumed to be swept about the j axis of the founding coordinate system to create a volume.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axi_or_plane.planar"

the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> is a 2D analysis model where 2D elements are assumed to be extruded perpendicular to the analysis plane to create a volume.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.coordinate_system_type"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.coordinate_system_type,coordinate_system_type>> specifies the allowable types of coordinate systems.

<<fig-cartesian-coordinate-system-definition>>, <<fig-cylindrical-coordinate-system-definition>>, and <<fig-spherical-coordinate-system-definition>> define conventions for cartesian, cylindrical, and spherical coordinate systems, respectively.

[[fig-cartesian-coordinate-system-definition]]
.Cartesian coordinate system definition
image::images/fig-3.svg[]

[[fig-cylindrical-coordinate-system-definition]]
.Cylindrical coordinate system definition
image::images/fig-4.svg[]

[[fig-spherical-coordinate-system-definition]]
.Spherical coordinate system definition
image::images/fig-5.svg[]

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.coordinate_system_type.cartesian"

the coordinate system is of type cartesian.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.coordinate_system_type.cylindrical"

the coordinate system is of type cylindrical.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.coordinate_system_type.spherical"

the coordinate system is of type spherical.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_order"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_order,element_order>> specifies the allowable geometric order of the element interpolation functions of a finite element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_order.linear"

the element basic interpolation order is linear.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_order.quadratic"

the element basic interpolation order is quadratic.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_order.cubic"

the element basic interpolation order is cubic.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_2d_element_purpose"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_2d_element_purpose,plane_2d_element_purpose>> specifies the allowable assumptions of response through the thickness of a finite element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_plane_2d_element_purpose"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_plane_2d_element_purpose,enumerated_plane_2d_element_purpose>> specifies the allowable assumptions of response through the thickness of a finite element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_plane_2d_element_purpose.plane_stress"

a specialised case of 2D plane elements where the stress normal to the element is zero.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_plane_2d_element_purpose.plane_strain"

a specialised case of 2D plane elements where the strain normal to the element is zero, but the stress is not.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.application_defined_element_purpose"

An application_defined_element_purpose is the specification of an allowable response purpose to be considered for an element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_element_purpose"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_element_purpose,volume_element_purpose>> specifies the allowable types of strain-displacement relationship assumed for a volume element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_volume_element_purpose"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_volume_element_purpose,enumerated_volume_element_purpose>> specifies the allowable types of strain-displacement relationship assumed for a volume element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_volume_element_purpose.stress_displacement"

the element considers only stress-displacement behaviour.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_element_purpose"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_element_purpose,surface_element_purpose>> specifies the allowable types of strain-displacement relationship assumed for a surface element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_element_purpose"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_element_purpose,enumerated_surface_element_purpose>> specifies the allowable types of strain-displacement relationship assumed for a surface element.

NOTE: The <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_element_purpose,surface_element_purpose>> is referenced as a SET of SETs to allow the appropriate combinations of coupled and uncoupled responses, such as ((.MEMBRANE_DIRECT.)(.MEMBRANE_DIRECT.,.BENDING_DIRECT.)) to specify that an element has membrane direct and coupled membrane - bending direct responses.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_element_purpose.membrane_direct"

in-plane deformations of the surface element contribute to the element's strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_element_purpose.membrane_shear"

in-plane shear deformations contribute to the element's strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_element_purpose.bending_direct"

changes in curvature of the surface element contribute to the element's strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_element_purpose.bending_torsion"

torsional deformations of the surface element contributes to the element's strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_element_purpose.normal_to_plane_shear"

normal-to-plane shear deformations contribute to the element's strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.curve_element_purpose"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.curve_element_purpose,curve_element_purpose>> specifies the allowable types of strain-displacement relationship assumed for a curve element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_purpose"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_purpose,enumerated_curve_element_purpose>> specifies the allowable types of strain-displacement relationship assumed for a curve element.

NOTE: The <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.curve_element_purpose,curve_element_purpose>> is referenced as a SET of SETs to allow the appropriate combinations of coupled and uncoupled responses, such as ((.AXIAL.)(.TORSION.,.WARPING.)) to specify that an element has axial and coupled torsion - warping responses.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_purpose.axial"

axial deformation along the curve of the element contributes to the element's strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_purpose.y_y_bending"

changes in curvature about the curve element's local stem:[y] axis contribute to the strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_purpose.z_z_bending"

changes in curvature about the curve element's local stem:[z] axis contribute to the strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_purpose.torsion"

torsion about the curve of the element contribute to the element's strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_purpose.x_y_shear"

shear deformation in the xy plane of the curve contributes to the element's strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_purpose.x_z_shear"

shear deformation in the xz plane of the curve contributes to the element's strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_purpose.warping"

warping deformation of the cross-section of the curve contributes to the element's strain energy.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_shape"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_shape,volume_3d_element_shape>> specifies the allowable geometric shapes of a 3D finite element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_shape.hexahedron"

a six-sided polyhedron where all sides are quadrilaterals.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_shape.wedge"

a five-sided polyhedron where three sides are quadrilaterals, and two are triangles.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_shape.tetrahedron"

a four-sided polyhedron where all sides are triangles.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_shape.pyramid"

a five-sided polyhedron where four sides are triangles and one a quadrilateral.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_2d_shape"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_2d_shape,element_2d_shape>> specifies the allowable geometric shapes of a 2D finite element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_2d_shape.quadrilateral"

a four-edged polygon.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_2d_shape.triangle"

a three-edged polygon.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.matrix_property_type"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.matrix_property_type,matrix_property_type>> specifies an allowable matrix property type to be considered for an expicit or volume element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_matrix_property_type"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_matrix_property_type,enumerated_matrix_property_type>> specifies the allowable property types of an explicit or volume element matrix. The type is used to define the matrix integrated within an explicit or volume element thus allowing different interpolation rules and integration methods to be used for different types of response relationships.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_matrix_property_type.stiffness"

the matrix contributes to the explicit or volume element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_matrix_property_type.mass"

the matrix contributes to the explicit or volume element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_matrix_property_type.damping"

the matrix contributes to the explicit or volume element damping matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.application_defined_matrix_property_type"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.application_defined_matrix_property_type,application_defined_matrix_property_type>> is the specification of an allowable matrix property type to be considered for an element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_matrix_property_type"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_matrix_property_type,surface_matrix_property_type>> specifies an allowable matrix property type to be considered for a surface element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type,enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type>> specifies the allowable property types of a surface element matrix. The type is used to define the matrix integrated within a surface element thus allowing different interpolation rules and integration methods to be used for different types of response relationships.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.membrane_direct"

the matrix contributes direct membrane stiffness to the surface element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.membrane_shear"

the matrix contributes shear membrane stiffness to the surface element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.bending_direct"

the matrix contributes direct bending stiffness to the surface element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.bending_torsion"

the matrix contributes bending torsional stiffness to the surface element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.normal_to_plane_shear"

the matrix contributes normal to plane shear stiffness to the surface element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.membrane_direct_mass"

the matrix contributes direct membrane mass to the surface element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.membrane_shear_mass"

the matrix contributes shear membrane mass to the surface element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.bending_direct_mass"

the matrix contributes direct bending mass to the surface element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.bending_torsion_mass"

the matrix contributes bending torsional mass to the surface element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.normal_to_plane_shear_mass"

the matrix contributes normal to plane shear mass to the surface element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_surface_matrix_property_type.mass"

the matrix contributes mass to the surface element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.curve_matrix_property_type"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.curve_matrix_property_type,curve_matrix_property_type>> specifies an allowable matrix property type to be considered for a curve element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type,enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type>> specifies the allowable property types of curve element matrix. The type is used to define the matrix integrated within a curve element thus allowing different interpolation rules and integration methods to be used for different types of response relationships.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.axial"

the matrix contributes axial stiffness to the curve element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.y_y_bending"

the matrix contributes y bending stiffness to the curve element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.z_z_bending"

the matrix contributes z bending stiffness to the curve element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.torsion"

the matrix contributes torsional stiffness to the curve element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.x_y_shear"

the matrix contributes y shear stiffness to the curve element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.x_z_shear"

the matrix contributes z shear stiffness to the curve element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.warping"

the matrix contributes warping stiffness to the curve element small displacement linear elastic stiffness matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.axial_mass"

the matrix contributes axial mass to the curve element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.y_y_bending_mass"

the matrix contributes y bending mass to the curve element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.z_z_bending_mass"

the matrix contributes z bending mass to the curve element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.torsion_mass"

the matrix contributes torsional mass to the curve element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.x_y_shear_mass"

the matrix contributes y shear mass to the curve element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.x_z_shear_mass"

the matrix contributes z shear mass to the curve element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.warping_mass"

the matrix contributes warping mass to the curve element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_matrix_property_type.mass"

the matrix contributes mass to the curve element mass matrix.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.matrix_symmetry"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.matrix_symmetry,matrix_symmetry>> specifies the allowable types of symmetry of a square matrix. The type of symmetry determines the position in an ARRAY attribute for each term of a matrix. Except for asymmetric matrices, only the lower half of the matrix is populated.

For a *symmetric* matrix of size n (where n denotes the number of freedoms defined by the degree_of_freedom_list) the position of the terms shall be:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
((1,,,,,,("symetric")),(2,n+1,,,,,),(3,n+2,2n,,,,),( *,*,*,3n-2,,,),( *,*,*,*,*,,),( *,*,*,*,*,*,),(n,2n-1,3n-3,*,*,*,n(n+1)//2))
++++

For a *diagonal* matrix of size n (where n denotes the number of freedoms defined by the degree_of_freedom_list) the position of the terms shall be:

[stem%unnumbered]
++++
((1,,,,),(,2,,0,),(,,,3,),(,0,,ddots,),(,,,,n))
++++

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.matrix_symmetry.symmetric"

the matrix and its transpose are identical.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.matrix_symmetry.diagonal"

the matrix has non-zero terms on the main diagonal only.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.degree_of_freedom"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.degree_of_freedom,degree_of_freedom>> is the allowable degrees of freedom to be considered for the intended analysis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_degree_of_freedom"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_degree_of_freedom,enumerated_degree_of_freedom>> specifies the   allowable degrees of freedom to be considered for the intended analysis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_degree_of_freedom.x_translation"

a translation degree of freedom along the x-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_degree_of_freedom.y_translation"

a translation degree of freedom along the y-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_degree_of_freedom.z_translation"

a translation degree of freedom along the z-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_degree_of_freedom.x_rotation"

a rotation degree of freedom about the x-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_degree_of_freedom.y_rotation"

a rotation degree of freedom about the y-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_degree_of_freedom.z_rotation"

a rotation degree of freedom about the z-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_degree_of_freedom.warp"

a warping degree of freedom of a curve element cross-section.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.application_defined_degree_of_freedom"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.application_defined_degree_of_freedom,application_defined_degree_of_freedom>> is the specification of an allowable degree of freedom to be considered for the intended analysis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.curve_element_freedom"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.curve_element_freedom,curve_element_freedom>> specifies the allowable degrees of freedom to be considered for a curve element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_freedom"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_freedom,enumerated_curve_element_freedom>> specifies the allowable degrees of freedom to be considered for a curve element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_freedom.x_translation"

a translation degree of freedom along the x-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_freedom.y_translation"

a translation degree of freedom along the y-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_freedom.z_translation"

a translation degree of freedom along the z-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_freedom.x_rotation"

a rotation degree of freedom about the x-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_freedom.y_rotation"

a rotation degree of freedom about the y-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_freedom.z_rotation"

a rotation degree of freedom about the z-axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_freedom.warp"

a warping degree of freedom of a curve element cross-section.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.enumerated_curve_element_freedom.none"

no degree of freedom of a curve element is specified.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.integration_rule"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.integration_rule,integration_rule>> specifies the allowable types of numerical integration rules to be considered by subtypes of the volume, surface and curve integration entities.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.integration_rule.gaussian"

gaussian integration.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.integration_rule.simpson"

bart simpson's rule integration.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.shape_function"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.shape_function,shape_function>> specifies the allowable types of interpolation function.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.shape_function.lagrangian"

the shape function is based upon lagrangian polynomials.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.shape_function.serendipity"

the shape function is based upon the serendipity modification of the lagrangian polynomials.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.shape_function.hermitian"

the shape function is based upon hermitian polynomials.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.shape_function.unspecified"

the shape function is not specified.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_2d_element_representation"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_2d_element_representation,volume_2d_element_representation>> is either an axisymmetric or plane volume 2D element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_2d_element_representation"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_2d_element_representation,surface_2d_element_representation>> is either an axisymmetric or plane surface 2D element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.curve_2d_element_representation"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.curve_2d_element_representation,curve_2d_element_representation>> is either an axisymmetric or plane curve 2D element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_2d_element_descriptor"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_2d_element_descriptor,volume_2d_element_descriptor>> is a collection of information that specifies either an axisymmetric or plane volume element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_2d_element_descriptor"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_2d_element_descriptor,surface_2d_element_descriptor>> is a collection of information that specifies either an axisymmetric or plane surface element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.curve_2d_element_descriptor"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.curve_2d_element_descriptor,curve_2d_element_descriptor>> is a collection of information that specifies either an axisymmetric or plane curve element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_coordinate_system"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_coordinate_system,volume_3d_element_coordinate_system>> is the orthogonal coordinate system for a volume 3D element that shall be either an arbitrary or a parametric coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_2d_element_coordinate_system"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_2d_element_coordinate_system,volume_2d_element_coordinate_system>> is the orthogonal coordinate system for a volume 2D element that shall be either an arbitrary or a parametric coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_coordinate_system"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_coordinate_system,surface_3d_element_coordinate_system>> is an orthogonal coordinate system for a surface 3D element that shall be either an aligned, constant, or a parametric coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_2d_element_coordinate_system"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_2d_element_coordinate_system,surface_2d_element_coordinate_system>> is a coordinate system for a surface 2D element that shall be either an aligned or a parametric coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.curve_3d_element_coordinate_system"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.curve_3d_element_coordinate_system,curve_3d_element_coordinate_system>> is an orthogonal coordinate system for a curve 3D element that shall be either aligned or parametric.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.curve_element_end_coordinate_system"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.curve_element_end_coordinate_system,curve_element_end_coordinate_system>> is a coordinate system for the end offset and end release of curve elements.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.directionally_explicit_element_coordinate_system"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.directionally_explicit_element_coordinate_system,directionally_explicit_element_coordinate_system>> is a directionally explicit element coordinate system that shall be either an aligned or arbitrary coordinate system that is used for orienting property information associated with a directionally explicit element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_aspect"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_aspect,element_aspect>> is the aspect of an element(volume, face, or edge) to be associated with a geometric shape representation.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_volume"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_volume,element_volume>> is the entire volume of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation,element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_volume.volume"

the volume of an element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.curve_edge"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.curve_edge,curve_edge>> is the edge of a curve element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.curve_edge.element_edge"

the edge of a curve element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_or_node_group"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_or_node_group,node_or_node_group>> is a node or set of nodes.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_or_element_group"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_or_element_group,element_or_element_group>> is an element or set of elements.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> represents a specific finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model.creating_software"

the name of the software used to create an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>>. The version of the software shall be specified.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model.intended_analysis_code"

the set of one or many names of the intended analysis code that an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> was created for. Each intended analysis code shall have the vendor, version, computer system, operating system and descriptions specified.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model.description"

a description of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> providing supporting information supplied by the author(s) of the model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model.analysis_type"

a description of what type of analysis is to be performed with this <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>>.

NOTE: The model id, a unique application defined identifier of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>>, is specified by the *name* attribute of the <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> supertype.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model.UR1"

the model id shall be unique to each finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model_3d"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model_3d,fea_model_3d>> is an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> that has geometry in three dimensions for 3D analyses.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model_3d.wr:WR1"

the dimension count of the founding coordinate system shall be 3.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model_2d"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model_2d,fea_model_2d>> is an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> whose geometry is restricted to a plane for planar or axisymmetric analyses.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model_2d.type_of_2d_analysis"

indicates whether the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> is axisymmetric or planar.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model_3d.wr:WR1"

the dimension count of the founding coordinate system shall be 2.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.structural_response_property_definition_representation"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.structural_response_property_definition_representation,structural_response_property_definition_representation>> is an association between the property of a finite element model, node or element and its representation.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.structural_response_property_definition_representation.wr:WR1"

the property being represented shall be a <<express:structural_response_definition_schema.structural_response_property,structural_response_property>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.structural_response_property_definition_representation.wr:WR2"

the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> shall be associated with the shape definition of a model, or the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation,element_representation>> shall be associated with the shape definition of an element, or the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_representation,node_representation>> shall be associated with the shape definition of a node.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_representation_item"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_representation_item,fea_representation_item>> is the different types of coordinate system for a finite element. The use of the appropriate type of coordinate system for a type of element is governed by rules within each of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation,element_representation>> subtypes.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.direction_node"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.direction_node,direction_node>> is the components of the direction vector defined by a line from *node_1* to *node_2*. The actual magnitudes of the components have no effect upon the direction being defined; only the ratios x:y:z or x:y are significant.

NOTE: It is assumed that this vector will be normalised when used.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.direction_node.node_1"

the first node used to define the direction vector.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.direction_node.node_2"

the second node used to define the direction vector.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.direction_node.direction_ratios[1]"

the component of the direction in the X axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.direction_node.direction_ratios[2]"

the component of the direction in the Y axis.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.direction_node.direction_ratios[3]"

the component of the direction in the Z axis; this will not be present in the case of a 2D space.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.direction_node.wr:WR1"

*node_1* shall represent a cartesian point.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.direction_node.wr:WR2"

*node_2* shall represent a cartesian point.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.direction_node.wr:WR3"

the magnitude of the direction vector shall be greater than zero.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_2d"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_2d,fea_axis2_placement_2d>> is a location and orientation in 2D space, and declares that the Placement Coordinate System may be either Cartesian, Cylindrical, or Spherical. All coordinate systems in a finite element analysis model shall be right handed.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_2d.system_type"

the type of placement coordinate system, which may be a right handed Cartesian, Cylindrical, or Spherical coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_2d.description"

additional information about the formulation or purpose of the coordinate system.

NOTE: The coordinate system id, a unique application defined identifier of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_2d,fea_axis2_placement_2d>>, is specified by the *name* attribute of the <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>> supertype.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_2d.wr:IP1"

the combination of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> and coordinate system id shall be unique within a finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_3d"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_3d,fea_axis2_placement_3d>> is a location and orientation in 3D space, and declares that the Placement Coordinate System may be either Cartesian, Cylindrical, or Spherical. All coordinate systems in a finite element analysis model shall be right handed.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_3d.system_type"

the type of placement coordinate system, which may be a right handed Cartesian, Cylindrical, or Spherical coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_3d.description"

additional information about the formulation or purpose of the coordinate system.

NOTE: The coordinate system id, a unique application defined identifier of *an fea_axis2_placement_3d*, is specified by the *name* attribute of the representation_item supertype.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.fea_axis2_placement_3d.wr:IP1"

the combination of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> and coordinate system id shall be unique within a finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_set"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_set,node_set>> is the aggregation of <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_representation,node_representation>> entities for geometric founding in a common coordinate frame.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_set.nodes"

the *node_representations* to be founded in a common coordinate frame.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_set.wr:WR1"

the geometric representation context of each of the nodes in the set shall be the same.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.analysis_item_within_representation"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.analysis_item_within_representation,analysis_item_within_representation>> is a reference to an individual <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>> used in a specified <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.analysis_item_within_representation.name"

the word or group of words by which the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.analysis_item_within_representation,analysis_item_within_representation>> is known.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.analysis_item_within_representation.description"

the word or of group of words that characterize the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.analysis_item_within_representation,analysis_item_within_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.analysis_item_within_representation.item"

a <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>> used in the representation referenced by rep.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.analysis_item_within_representation.rep"

the <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> that the item is used in.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.analysis_item_within_representation.wr:WR1"

the referenced <<express:representation_schema.representation_item,representation_item>> shall be used in the referenced <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_geometric_relationship"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_geometric_relationship,node_geometric_relationship>> is the association of a node or set of nodes with a geometric shape representation item.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_geometric_relationship.node_ref"

the node or set of nodes being related to geometry.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_geometric_relationship.item"

the geometric representation item to which the node or set of nodes is being related.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_geometric_relationship.wr:WR1"

the item associated with the node or set of nodes shall be a geometric representation item.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_geometric_relationship"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_geometric_relationship,element_geometric_relationship>> is the association of an aspect (edge, face, or entire volume) of an element or set of elements with an appropriate type of geometric representation item.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_geometric_relationship.element_ref"

the element or set of elements being related to geometry.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_geometric_relationship.item"

the geometric representation item to which the element aspect is being related.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_geometric_relationship.aspect"

the edge, face, or volume for the element or set of elements.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_geometric_relationship.wr:WR1"

the item associated with the element or set of elements shall be a geometric representation item.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_geometric_relationship.wr:WR2"

the element aspect shall be related to the appropriate geometric shape representation item. Element edges shall be related to a curve, element faces to a surface, and the volume of an element to a solid model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_geometric_relationship.wr:WR3"

the element aspect shall be consistent with the element type for the element or set of elements.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_representation"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_representation,node_representation>> is location within a finite element model that may have associated degrees of freedom.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_representation.model_ref"

an application defined identifier of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> which possesses the node.

NOTE: The node id, a unique application defined identifier of a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_representation,node_representation>>, is specified by the *name* attribute of the <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> supertype.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_representation.UR1"

the combination of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> and node id shall be unique within a model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node,node>> is a discretisation point for the field variables of the finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node.wr:WR1"

there shall be exactly one position associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node,node>>. The position (POINT) is the coordinates of the node with respect to the founding placement coordinate system in the using <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_set,node_set>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_with_vector"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_with_vector,node_with_vector>> is a discretisation point with a normal for the field variables of surface elements in a finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_with_vector.wr:WR1"

there shall be exactly one surface normal associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_with_vector,node_with_vector>>. The surface normal (DIRECTION) is defined at the node with respect to the founding placement coordinate system, which is needed only when a node is connected to surface elements that require this information. The *normal* may correspond to the *Z-axis* of the *solution_coordinate_system*.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_with_solution_coordinate_system"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_with_solution_coordinate_system,node_with_solution_coordinate_system>> is a discretisation point with a solution coordinate system for the field variables of elements in a finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_with_solution_coordinate_system.wr:WR1"

there shall be exactly one solution coordinate system associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node,node>>, and it shall be of the same coordinate space dimension as the corresponding geometric representation context. The solution coordinate system (FEA_AXIS2_PLACEMENT_3D or FEA_AXIS2_PLACEMENT_2D) is the coordinate system in which the degrees of freedom of the node are defined.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.node_with_solution_coordinate_system.wr:IP1"

the coordinate system types shall correspond between *points* and *placements* if the points are cylindrical, spherical, or cartesian.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.dummy_node"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.dummy_node,dummy_node>> is a placeholder in the node list for an element representation. It is used where an element representation does not have one or more of the mid-edge, mid-face, or mid-volume nodes that are shown in the node sequence diagrams in <<fig-curve-3d-or-surface-2d-elements-linear>> through <<fig-volume-3d-elements-pyramid-faces-and-edges>>.

[example]
The ninth node for a quadratic-order quadrilateral element is a mid-face node as shown in the node sequence diagram in <<fig-surface-3d-or-volume-2d-elements-quadrilateral-quadratic>>. Thus for an eight-noded serendipity element, a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.dummy_node,dummy_node>> may be included in the ninth position in the node list. In addition, if any of the four mid-edge nodes are also missing, a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.dummy_node,dummy_node>> shall be used in its place in the node list.

NOTE: An instantiation of an FEA model has only one instance of <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.dummy_node,dummy_node>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.geometric_node"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.geometric_node,geometric_node>> is a node that is for geometric reference only and therefore shall not be connected to an element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.geometric_node.wr:WR1"

there shall be exactly one position associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.geometric_node,geometric_node>>. The position (POINT) is the coordinates of the node with respect to the founding placement coordinate system in the using <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_set,node_set>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.substructure_node_relationship"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.substructure_node_relationship,substructure_node_relationship>> is a association of two nodes where each node is in a separate substructure. During matrix assembly the two nodes will be merged to provide assembly between the two subsructures.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.substructure_node_relationship.wr:WR1"

the rep_1 attribute shall be associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_representation,node_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.substructure_node_relationship.wr:WR2"

the rep_2 attribute shall be associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.node_representation,node_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation,element_representation>> is the aspect of a finite element that represents the mathematical relationships between the nodes of a finite element model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation.node_list"

the list of nodes that make up the element.

NOTE: The node list defines the connection of an element to other elements within the model.

NOTE: For volume, surface, or curve elements the positions of the nodes together with the element shape functions define the shape and orientation of an element. The position of a node in the node list depends on the element shape and order as established graphically in the figures in <<sub-eltopo>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation.wr:WR1"

there shall be no geometric nodes in the list of nodes for the element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation.wr:IP1"

TYPE *dummy_nodes* shall be used as placeholders in the *node_list* if the element formulation does not include mid-edge, mid-face, and mid-volume nodes.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation,volume_3d_element_representation>> is a 3D shape.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation.model_ref"

an application defined identifier of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> which possesses the volume 3D element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation.element_descriptor"

a collection of information that specifies a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation,volume_3d_element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation.material"

the material properties associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation,volume_3d_element_representation>>.

NOTE: The element id, a unique application defined identifier of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation,element_representation>>, is specified by the *name* attribute of the <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> supertype.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation.UR1"

the combination of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> and element id shall be unique within a finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation.wr:WR1"

there shall be either a parametric material coordinate system (PARAMETRIC_VOLUME_3D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM) or an arbitrary material coordinate system (ARBITRARY_VOLUME_3D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM) associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation,volume_3d_element_representation>>.

The material coordinate system (parametric or arbitrary) is the orientation of the element coordinate system for the material of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation,volume_3d_element_representation>>. The stem:[x] axis of the element coordinate system shall coincide with the stem:[x] axis of the material coordinate system, the stem:[y] axis of the element coordinate system shall coincide with the stem:[y] axis of the material coordinate system, and the stem:[z] axis of the element coordinate system shall coincide with the stem:[z] axis of the material coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation.wr:WR2"

there shall be at least one of the following <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_material_property_representation_item,fea_material_property_representation_item>> subtypesreferenced by a *material*: <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_linear_elasticity,fea_linear_elasticity>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_mass_density,fea_mass_density>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion,fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion,fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion>>, and <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_moisture_absorption,fea_moisture_absorption>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation.wr:WR3"

there shall be a parametric representation context associated with the element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation.FU1"

the number of nodes in the node list of a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation,volume_3d_element_representation>> shall be the proper number based on the shape and order of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.volume_3d_element_representation,volume_3d_element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation>> is a 2D shape swept about the stem:[j] axis of the (stem:[i, j]) plane of the founding coordinate system (see <<subsec-geometric-founding-and-analysis-space>>). An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation>> shall lie in the (stem:[i, j]) plane of the founding coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation.model_ref"

an application defined identifier of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> which possesses the axisymmetric volume 2D element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation.element_descriptor"

a collection of information that specifies an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation.angle_property"

the properties for an axisymmetric section. Analysis applications often assume that the value of the angle attribute of this property is stem:[2 pi].

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation.material"

the material properties associated with an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation>>.

NOTE: The element id, a unique application defined identifier of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation,element_representation>>, is specified by the *name* attribute of the <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> supertype.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation.UR1"

the combination of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> and element id shall be unique within a finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation.wr:WR1"

the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> shall be axisymmetric.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation.wr:WR2"

there shall be either a parametric material coordinate system (PARAMETRIC_VOLUME_2D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM) or an arbitrary material coordinate system (ARBITRARY_VOLUME_2D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM) associated with an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation>>.

The material coordinate system (parametric or arbitrary) is the orientation of the element coordinate system for the material of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation>>. The stem:[x] axis of the element coordinate system shall coincide with the stem:[x] axis of the material coordinate system, the stem:[y] axis of the element coordinate system shall coincide with the stem:[y] axis of the material coordinate system, and the stem:[z] axis of the element coordinate system shall coincide with the stem:[z] axis of the material coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation.wr:WR3"

there shall be at least one of the following <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_material_property_representation_item,fea_material_property_representation_item>> subtypes referenced by a *material*: <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_linear_elasticity,fea_linear_elasticity>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_mass_density,fea_mass_density>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_area_density,fea_area_density>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion,fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion,fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion>>, and <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_moisture_absorption,fea_moisture_absorption>>.
*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation.wr:WR4"

there shall be a parametric representation context associated with the element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation.FU1"

the number of nodes in the node list of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation>> shall be the proper number based on the shape and order of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_volume_2d_element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation,plane_volume_2d_element_representation>> is a 2D shape in the (stem:[i, j]) plane of the founding coordinate system (see <<subsec-geometric-founding-and-analysis-space>>) with a depth perpendicular to the (stem:[i, j]) plane. A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation,plane_volume_2d_element_representation>> shall lie in the (stem:[i, j]) plane of the founding coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation.model_ref"

an application defined identifier of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> which possesses the plane volume 2D element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation.element_descriptor"

a collection of information that specifies a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation,plane_volume_2d_element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation.depth_property"

the properties for a plane section. Analysis applications often assume that the value of the depth attribute of this property is 1.0.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation.material"

the material properties associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation,plane_volume_2d_element_representation>>.

NOTE: The element id, a unique application defined identifier of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation,element_representation>>, is specified by the *name* attribute of the <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> supertype.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation.UR1"

the combination of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> and element id shall be unique within a finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation.wr:WR1"

the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> shall be planar.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation.wr:WR2"

there shall be either a parametric material coordinate system (PARAMETRIC_VOLUME_2D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM) or an arbitrary material coordinate system (ARBITRARY_VOLUME_2D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM) associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation,plane_volume_2d_element_representation>>.

The material coordinate system (parametric or arbitrary) is the orientation of the element coordinate system for the material of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation,plane_volume_2d_element_representation>>. The stem:[x] axis of the element coordinate system shall coincide with the stem:[x] axis of the material coordinate system, the stem:[y] axisof the element coordinate system shall coincide with the stem:[y] axis of the material coordinate system, and the stem:[z] axis of the element coordinate system shall coincide with the stem:[z] axis of the material coordinate system.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation.wr:WR3"

there shall be at least one of the following <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_material_property_representation_item,fea_material_property_representation_item>> subtypes referenced by a *material*: <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_linear_elasticity,fea_linear_elasticity>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_mass_density,fea_mass_density>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_area_density,fea_area_density>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion,fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion,fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion>>, and <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_moisture_absorption,fea_moisture_absorption>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation.wr:WR4"

there shall be a parametric representation context associated with the element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation.FU1"

the number of nodes in the node list of a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation,plane_volume_2d_element_representation>> shall be the proper number based on the shape and order of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_volume_2d_element_representation,plane_volume_2d_element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation,surface_3d_element_representation>> is an element that is a surface with a specified section that is a 2D shape (a triangle or quadrilateral) in 3D space.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation.model_ref"

an application defined identifier of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> which possesses the surface 3D element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation.element_descriptor"

a collection of information that specifies a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation,surface_3d_element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation.property"

the section properties of the surface.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation.material"

the material properties associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation,surface_3d_element_representation>>.

NOTE: The element id, a unique application defined identifier of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation,element_representation>>, is specified by the *name* attribute of the <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> supertype.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation.UR1"

the combination of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> and element id shall be unique within a finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation.wr:WR1"

there shall be either a parametric property coordinate system (PARAMETRIC_SURFACE_3D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM), a constant property coordinate system (CONSTANT_SURFACE_3D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM), or an aligned property coordinate system (ALIGNED_SURFACE_3D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM) associated with a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation,surface_3d_element_representation>>.

The property coordinate system (parametric, constant, or aligned) is the orientation of the coordinate system for the element material and section properties. For a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation,surface_3d_element_representation>> the property coordinate system is defined such that its stem:[z] axis is normal to the element surface at each point on the surface of the element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation.wr:WR2"

there shall be at least one of the following <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_material_property_representation_item,fea_material_property_representation_item>> subtypes referenced by a *material*: <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_linear_elasticity,fea_linear_elasticity>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_mass_density,fea_mass_density>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_area_density,fea_area_density>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion,fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion,fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_moisture_absorption,fea_moisture_absorption>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_shell_membrane_stiffness,fea_shell_membrane_stiffness>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_shell_bending_stiffness,fea_shell_bending_stiffness>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_shell_membrane_bending_coupling_stiffness,fea_shell_membrane_bending_coupling_stiffness>>, and <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_shell_shear_stiffness,fea_shell_shear_stiffness>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation.wr:WR3"

there shall be a parametric representation context associated with the element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation.FU1"

the number of nodes in the node list of a <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation,surface_3d_element_representation>> shall be the proper number based on the shape and order of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.surface_3d_element_representation,surface_3d_element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation"

An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation>> is an element that is a surface with a specified section that is a 1D shape (curve) that is swept about the stem:[j] axis of the (stem:[i, j]) plane of the founding coordinate system (see <<subsec-geometric-founding-and-analysis-space>>). An <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation>> shall lie in the (stem:[i, j]) plane of the founding coordinate system. An illustration of a 2D founding coordinate system is shown in <<fig-curve-2d-element-coord-system-derivation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.model_ref"

an application defined identifier of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> which possesses the axisymmetric surface 2D element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.element_descriptor"

a collection of information that specifies an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.property"

the section properties of the surface.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.angle_property"

the properties for an axisymmetric section. Analysis applications often assume that the value of the angle attribute of this property is stem:[2 pi].

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.material"

the material properties associated with an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation>>.

NOTE: The element id, a unique application defined identifier of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.element_representation,element_representation>>, is specified by the *name* attribute of the <<express:representation_schema.representation,representation>> supertype.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.UR1"

the combination of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> and element id shall be unique within a finite element analysis model.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.wr:WR1"

the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_model,fea_model>> shall be axisymmetric.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.wr:WR2"

there shall be either a parametric property coordinate system (PARAMETRIC_SURFACE_2D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM) or an aligned property coordinate system (ALIGNED_SURFACE_2D_ELEMENT_COORDINATE_SYSTEM) associated with an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation>>.

The property coordinate system (parametric or aligned) is the orientation of the coordinate system for the element material and section properties. For an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation>> the property coordinate system is defined such that: the stem:[x] axis is normal to the 2D analysis plane in the direction of the stem:[k] axis of the analysis 2D definition coordinate system, and the stem:[z] axis is normal to the element surface as shown in <<fig-curve-2d-element-coord-system-derivation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.wr:WR3"

there shall be at least one of the following <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_material_property_representation_item,fea_material_property_representation_item>> subtypes referenced by a *material*: <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_linear_elasticity,fea_linear_elasticity>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_mass_density,fea_mass_density>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_area_density,fea_area_density>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion,fea_tangential_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion,fea_secant_coefficient_of_linear_thermal_expansion>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_moisture_absorption,fea_moisture_absorption>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_shell_membrane_stiffness,fea_shell_membrane_stiffness>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_shell_bending_stiffness,fea_shell_bending_stiffness>>, <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_shell_membrane_bending_coupling_stiffness,fea_shell_membrane_bending_coupling_stiffness>>, and <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.fea_shell_shear_stiffness,fea_shell_shear_stiffness>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.wr:WR4"

there shall be a parametric representation context associated with the element.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation.FU1"

the number of nodes in the node list of an <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation>> shall be the proper number based on the order of the <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation,axisymmetric_surface_2d_element_representation>>.

*)

(*"structural_response_representation_schema.plane_surface_2d_element_representation"

A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_surface_2d_element_representation,plane_surface_2d_element_representation>> is an element that is a surface with a specified section that is a 1D shape (curve) in the (stem:[i, j]) plane of the founding coordinate system (see <<subsec-geometric-founding-and-analysis-space>>) with a depth perpendicular to the (stem:[i, j]) plane. A <<express:structural_response_representation_schema.plane_surface_2d_element_representation,plane_surface_2d_element_representation>> shall lie in the (stem:[i, j]) plane of the founding coordinate system.

